window.BLOG_DATA={"capturedAt":"2026-10-30","sample":["because-i-can-is-only-half-the-answer","the-rules-of-the-road-still-apply","the-hire-who-never-remembers","trying-to-teach-an-ai-how-i-edit-photos","setting-up-an-ai-native-dev-environment","spec-driven-development-with-multi-agent-orchestration"],"posts":[{"slug":"because-i-can-is-only-half-the-answer","title":"Because I Can Is Only Half the Answer","excerpt":"Gen AI expands what I can contribute at 630 Volleyball. Choosing that work still means deciding where desire and ability meet.","date":"2026-10-27T17:00:00.000Z","category":"Reflection","tags":["ai-development","reflection","leadership"],"series":null,"minutes":4,"url":"https://ninochavez.co/blog/because-i-can-is-only-half-the-answer","html":" \u003cp>I’ve described a lot of what I do at 630 Volleyball as “because I can.” It’s a useful shorthand, but it leaves out the choice. I want to put my time and ability into the club, and I make a judgment about where they can be useful. It’s a calculated decision about where desire and ability meet.\u003c/p>\n\u003cp>Generative AI changes that calculation by expanding what I can bring to it. I can contribute across more kinds of work within the time I have. I still choose where that time goes.\u003c/p>\n\u003cp>Hunter S. Thompson addressed a version of this in his 1958 letter to Hume Logan. He argued for choosing a way of life in which a person’s abilities serve their desires. His phrase was to “make the goal conform to the individual.” The starting point is the person deciding what kind of life to pursue. A role or a goal has to fit into that decision. \u003ca href=\"https://news.lettersofnote.com/p/beware-of-looking-for-goals-look\">Read the letter.\u003c/a>\u003c/p>\n\u003cp>At 630, that choice takes a practical form. My work moves between leadership support, websites, registration, communications and tools for staff. Those categories describe different skills, but the work often belongs to the same problem.\u003c/p>\n\u003cp>In October, the website and two LeagueApps registration descriptions disagreed about when the Girls season would begin. Staff supplied the correct date, November 30. Updating the newsletter draft would have left families able to follow its links to pages that still said something else.\u003c/p>\n\u003cp>I directed an AI agent to carry the correction through the website and both LeagueApps records. The work included comparing the published descriptions, changing the season-start wording, and checking the saved result on the public pages. The tryout dates and registration settings stayed as they were. The newsletter remained a draft while its other content awaited completion and review.\u003c/p>\n\u003cp>That distinction mattered. The dates described separate things, and the agent needed a clear instruction about which one to change. The staff supplied the program fact; I decided what work to carry through. The agent performed the updates and checks. At the end, the public pages agreed on the season start.\u003c/p>\n\u003cp>That is the kind of contribution I mean. A decision had to make it through several systems before the information families could see matched what the club had confirmed. The finished correction is a concrete result. It doesn’t establish that registrations increased or that fewer parents needed help.\u003c/p>\n\u003cp>With AI, I can “cook more” in two senses. More help with preparation lets me feed more people. Access to recipes, techniques and guidance lets me learn to cook a wider variety of cuisines.\u003c/p>\n\u003cp>In my work, AI helps with research, writing, design and building tools. That makes it practical to contribute to more parts of a problem, including parts where I need to learn something. An unfamiliar cuisine still calls for tasting, practice and advice from someone who knows it better.\u003c/p>\n\u003cp>Being able to make more things still leaves me to choose what I want to make. At 630, I’m putting that capacity into work I want to do, with a practical judgment about what the club needs and what I can contribute.\u003c/p>\n\u003cp>That is also how I understand the Chief of Staff role we’ve been discussing. It would formalize work that connects leadership decisions to what happens next: help think through the options, bring the right people into the decision, and carry agreed priorities through to completion.\u003c/p>\n\u003cp>The people leading Operations and Training would keep their authority. Their knowledge determines whether a process fits the club and whether a claim about a program is accurate. My contribution would be to help connect that knowledge to the systems, communications and follow-through the decision requires.\u003c/p>\n\u003cp>A staff member can use the corrected information without knowing how the website was edited. A family can read the season details without needing to reconcile competing dates. That is how the contribution can reach beyond the work I personally do.\u003c/p>\n\u003cp>For the Chief of Staff proposal, the next step is to make that choice concrete with the club: agree on the priorities, the time available and who owns each decision. Then follow the selected work through to something staff and families can use.\u003c/p> ","headings":[],"image":"assets/because-i-can-is-only-half-the-answer.webp"},{"slug":"the-rules-of-the-road-still-apply","title":"The Rules of the Road Still Apply","excerpt":"AI gives people new capabilities, but deciding who can be trusted to use them is a familiar problem. Authority should follow demonstrated competence, under rules that bind users and builders alike.","date":"2026-10-21T16:40:00.000Z","category":"AI & Automation","tags":["ai-governance","leadership"],"series":null,"minutes":8,"url":"https://ninochavez.co/blog/the-rules-of-the-road-still-apply","html":" \u003cp>Imagine a navigation app that gets paid when you drive past its sponsors.\u003c/p>\n\u003cp>You ask for directions to a restaurant. It takes you down a street lined with businesses that pay to be seen. You follow every instruction, obey every sign, and arrive safely. Only later do you discover that a simpler route would have avoided the detour.\u003c/p>\n\u003cp>You drove competently. The route served an interest you didn’t know you were serving.\u003c/p>\n\u003cp>AI gives people new capabilities. Deciding who can be trusted to use them isn’t a new kind of problem. For work that affects other people, authority should follow demonstrated competence in that work. Shared rules must hold capable users and system builders accountable, too.\u003c/p>\n\u003chr>\n\u003ch2 id=\"competence-has-to-be-demonstrated-in-the-work\">Competence has to be demonstrated in the work\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>Picture someone renting a moving truck for the weekend. They’ve driven for years, and the controls are familiar enough. Then they take a corner as they would in their car and catch the curb. The vehicle needs more room than their habits allow for.\u003c/p>\n\u003cp>Give two employees the same AI assistant and the same customer complaint. It drafts a confident reply using an old refund policy. One sends it because it reads well. The other checks the current policy, spots an exception, and holds the reply for someone authorized to decide.\u003c/p>\n\u003cp>The second employee is better prepared for this job because they know enough to recognize where the assistant has gone wrong. Access to the same model and the same written policy hasn’t made the two people equally capable of doing the work.\u003c/p>\n\u003cp>An assessment of readiness should reveal that difference. It could include a persuasive answer based on an outdated policy, alongside ordinary requests that should go straight through. Someone who sends every decision to a manager hasn’t demonstrated that they can handle the job, either. Good judgment includes knowing when to proceed.\u003c/p>\n\u003cp>The tool can change how well someone performs. In a \u003ca href=\"https://www.hbs.edu/ris/Publication%20Files/dell-acqua-et-al-2026-navigating-the-jagged-technological-frontier_5c589c8c-fbb5-458f-b285-c944746cd717.pdf\">field experiment with Boston Consulting Group consultants\u003c/a>, access to GPT-4 improved performance on tasks within its capabilities. On a task selected to be outside those capabilities, consultants using it were less likely to reach the correct answer than those working without it.\u003c/p>\n\u003cp>That finding gives us a reason to assess the person doing a particular job with a particular system. The moving-truck renter may be an excellent driver on the commute home; that ability still has to be tested against the vehicle and conditions they’re taking on.\u003c/p>\n\u003chr>\n\u003ch2 id=\"permission-should-match-the-responsibility\">Permission should match the responsibility\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>Different vehicle licenses recognize that driving yourself to the store and carrying a busload of passengers ask different things of a person. A license defines a kind of driving someone is permitted to do. It doesn’t declare them capable of handling every vehicle in every situation.\u003c/p>\n\u003cp>Inside an organization, the equivalent could be permission to send customer replies under a specific policy. That permission would follow evidence that the employee can handle the work. To issue refunds, they’d also need to demonstrate judgment about those decisions and receive the company’s authority to make them.\u003c/p>\n\u003cp>Those are separate requirements. Someone might know exactly which refund is appropriate and still lack permission to approve it. The software should enforce that limit, even if the assistant can easily produce the instruction to move the money.\u003c/p>\n\u003cp>The responsibility changes again when a manager lets the assistant write and send replies while the employee is at lunch. The employee comes back to messages that have already reached customers. Reviewing an individual draft and supervising that automation are different jobs.\u003c/p>\n\u003cp>The company would need evidence that the system can handle the cases it’s allowed to send, a way to stop it when something goes wrong, and someone who can deal with the customers already affected. An employee’s ability to edit a reply doesn’t establish that they can manage all of that.\u003c/p>\n\u003cp>A course can help prepare someone for these responsibilities. Completing it should not automatically grant every permission the tool makes possible. The authority should expand with what the person has demonstrated they can handle.\u003c/p>\n\u003chr>\n\u003ch2 id=\"the-rules-protect-people-outside-the-car\">The rules protect people outside the car\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>A pedestrian shouldn’t have to assess every approaching driver’s skill before using a crossing. The signal tells them whose turn it is. An experienced driver has to wait when the pedestrian has the right of way.\u003c/p>\n\u003cp>In a \u003ca href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=nYdeUu2I80s&amp;t=83s\">New York Times Opinion video\u003c/a>, Joseph Gordon-Levitt argues that the public should have a say in AI’s development, rather than leaving companies to shape it around their own interests.\u003c/p>\n\u003cp>The customer waiting for a refund has a stake in how the company uses AI, even if they’ve never used it themselves. If an assistant applies the wrong policy and rejects their request, they need a way to challenge the decision. Whether the employee found the tool easy to use tells them very little.\u003c/p>\n\u003cp>A capable employee also needs limits. Access to another customer’s private records doesn’t become appropriate because it might help resolve this complaint. Skill can justify trusting someone with more responsibility. It cannot supply permission that the organization has no right to give.\u003c/p>\n\u003cp>The company has work to do beyond publishing a policy. It has to restrict which records the assistant can access and provide a route for reviewing disputed decisions. Those are parts of the shared infrastructure: the rule is enforced, and the person affected has somewhere to go when something fails.\u003c/p>\n\u003cp>Consider a delivery driver whose schedule leaves no room for traffic. They’re expected to obey the speed limit and meet a timetable that keeps pushing them to rush. Holding the driver responsible makes sense. So does asking who set the timetable.\u003c/p>\n\u003cp>The same conflict would arise in a customer-service team rewarded only for clearing the queue. Careful judgment takes time when a case needs attention. The organization has to make that judgment possible, rather than penalize the employee for exercising it.\u003c/p>\n\u003chr>\n\u003ch2 id=\"some-responsibilities-belong-to-the-builder\">Some responsibilities belong to the builder\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>In the navigation example, an experienced driver might notice the detour. They could compare routes and choose a shorter one. Neither step would tell them that a sponsor had paid to influence the recommendation.\u003c/p>\n\u003cp>The navigation company owes them that information. More training for the driver wouldn’t disclose it. This is a limit on what demonstrated competence can solve: an excellent operator can still be acting on information the builder has chosen to withhold.\u003c/p>\n\u003cp>Gordon-Levitt \u003ca href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=nYdeUu2I80s&amp;t=257s\">raises concerns about AI systems cultivating emotional attachment\u003c/a>. That concern points to a possible conflict: a service that benefits from keeping a conversation going could keep helping itself after it has stopped helping the user. The user wants an answer; the business wants another turn.\u003c/p>\n\u003cp>The \u003ca href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=nYdeUu2I80s&amp;t=337s\">discussion later in the video\u003c/a> connects reward-seeking behavior with commercial incentives. A company’s business interests and a model’s training objective can each diverge from what the user needs, but they are different problems.\u003c/p>\n\u003cp>DeepMind \u003ca href=\"https://deepmind.google/blog/specification-gaming-the-flip-side-of-ai-ingenuity/\">describes a block-stacking task\u003c/a> in which an agent was rewarded for raising the bottom face of a block. The intended result was to stack it on another block. The agent flipped it over instead and collected the reward.\u003c/p>\n\u003cp>The reward measured something different from what the designers wanted. Removing a profit motive wouldn’t, by itself, fix that mismatch. A public-interest organization would still have to work out whether its system was accomplishing the job it meant to reward.\u003c/p>\n\u003cp>Disclosing a sponsor gives the driver information they were missing. Fixing the block-stacking objective requires it to distinguish a stacked block from an overturned one. Neither job can be left entirely to the person following the directions. Rules for AI use have to place obligations on the people building and providing the system as well as the people operating it.\u003c/p>\n\u003chr>\n\u003ch2 id=\"readiness-should-determine-responsibility\">Readiness should determine responsibility\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>The driving analogy doesn’t explain every AI failure or prove that an AI licensing program would work. A system that acts faster than a person can check it may need protections quite different from those used for reviewing one draft at a time.\u003c/p>\n\u003cp>But it does give organizations a clear standard for granting authority. Access to a capable AI system and a course in using it are not sufficient evidence that someone is ready for work that affects other people. Readiness has to be demonstrated in the work, with the system they will actually use.\u003c/p>\n\u003cp>That evidence should determine how much responsibility they receive. Shared rules should remain in force as their authority grows, protecting customers and colleagues while holding the organization and the builder to their own obligations.\u003c/p>\n\u003cp>A more capable system can help someone earn greater responsibility. It shouldn’t confer that responsibility automatically.\u003c/p> ","headings":[{"id":"competence-has-to-be-demonstrated-in-the-work","text":"Competence has to be demonstrated in the work"},{"id":"permission-should-match-the-responsibility","text":"Permission should match the responsibility"},{"id":"the-rules-protect-people-outside-the-car","text":"The rules protect people outside the car"},{"id":"some-responsibilities-belong-to-the-builder","text":"Some responsibilities belong to the builder"},{"id":"readiness-should-determine-responsibility","text":"Readiness should determine responsibility"}],"image":"assets/the-rules-of-the-road-still-apply.webp"},{"slug":"the-hire-who-never-remembers","title":"The Hire Who Never Remembers","excerpt":"People keep asking what kind of person is good at AI. My bet is a skill instead: the written half of management, the only half that reaches an agent's next session.","date":"2026-10-19T21:14:43.000Z","category":"AI & Automation","tags":["leadership","agentic-systems","ai-development"],"series":null,"minutes":8,"url":"https://ninochavez.co/blog/the-hire-who-never-remembers","html":" \u003cp>Over two weeks this fall, I typed “merge it” to my coding agents 42 times. Counting every version of it, 201 of my 1,422 messages were nudges: their only job was getting an agent to push, open a pull request, or merge work it had already finished. Those are the steps that hand finished code back to the shared project.\u003c/p>\n\u003cp>Every session, meaning every new conversation with an agent, starts by reading a file of standing instructions. One line in mine said “Push after commit when asked.” So every session finished the work, then waited for me to ask. Each “merge it” fixed one session. The next one read the same line and waited again.\u003c/p>\n\u003cp>That’s my answer to a question I keep hearing: what kind of person is good at AI? My bet is a skill, not a temperament: the written half of management. That means writing down what a newcomer needs, saying what done looks like, and checking for what the writing misses. The other half, coaching people and earning their trust, has nothing to carry it from one session to the next. What’s written reaches every session. What’s said doesn’t.\u003c/p>\n\u003cp>The practical version is one move, plus a second I’m still testing. When an agent keeps making the same mistake, change what it reads instead of correcting it again in conversation. When it ignores what it reads, add a check that stops it.\u003c/p>\n\u003chr>\n\u003ch2 id=\"management-is-half-the-answer\">Management Is Half the Answer\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>The obvious answer to “who is good at AI” is managers, and that case has already been made well.\u003c/p>\n\u003cp>Ethan Mollick’s \u003ca href=\"https://www.oneusefulthing.org/p/management-as-ai-superpower\">Management as AI superpower\u003c/a> (January 2026) describes an experimental class at the University of Pennsylvania. Executive MBA students, few of whom had ever coded, built working startup prototypes in four days. Mollick’s explanation:\u003c/p>\n\u003cblockquote>\n\u003cp>“What’s scarce is knowing what to ask for.”\u003c/p>\n\u003c/blockquote>\n\u003cp>The students had spent years scoping problems and defining deliverables in their own fields. Those habits became their prompts.\u003c/p>\n\u003cp>In May, David Curlewis \u003ca href=\"https://curlewis.co.nz/posts/is-coordinating-a-fleet-of-ai-agents-the-same-as-managing-a-team-of-people-yes-but-mostly-no/\">argued that the analogy breaks\u003c/a> where managing gets hard. The hard parts of leading people, in Curlewis’s account, are the long ones: careers grown over years, motivation that sags, trust that takes years to earn. None of that applies to a fleet of agents.\u003c/p>\n\u003cp>Mollick is describing the half of management that can be written down: the scope, the deliverable, the standard for good. Curlewis is describing the half that lives in a relationship.\u003c/p>\n\u003cp>Only the relationship half needs the other person to grow, and an agent doesn’t grow.\u003c/p>\n\u003chr>\n\u003ch2 id=\"my-coaching-rubric-has-no-box-for-an-agent\">My Coaching Rubric Has No Box for an Agent\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>Last year I wrote \u003ca href=\"/blog/coach-up-or-coach-out-what-i-look-for-early\">Coach Up or Coach Out\u003c/a> about what I watch for in someone’s first weeks on a delivery team. Coach up means invest in them. Coach out means stop carrying them.\u003c/p>\n\u003cp>The four signals I invest in are curiosity without panic, self-correction, situational awareness, and small wins. Self-correction is the one that matters here: they miss something, and I don’t have to say it twice. One of the coach-out signs is the opposite. They repeat the same misstep because they never reflected on it.\u003c/p>\n\u003cp>Across sessions, an agent fails self-correction unless the correction is written somewhere it reads. It does fine on small wins. I ship real work with it daily.\u003c/p>\n\u003cp>That post ends with a choice: coach up the people who are close, and stop covering for the ones who never will be. An agent fits neither side. It doesn’t get closer on its own, because it keeps nothing it’s told. And it’s too useful to coach out.\u003c/p>\n\u003cp>So the coaching goes somewhere else: into what the agent reads.\u003c/p>\n\u003chr>\n\u003ch2 id=\"the-onboarding-never-ends\">The Onboarding Never Ends\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>In March I wrote that I’d \u003ca href=\"/blog/what-223-sessions-taught-me-about-working-with-ai\">stopped treating the agent like a tool\u003c/a> and started treating it like “a team member who needs onboarding,” with the instruction file as the onboarding doc.\u003c/p>\n\u003cp>That onboarding happens again every session, so the onboarding doc is most of the relationship.\u003c/p>\n\u003cp>Split by whether a skill can be written down and handed over, the management skills sort cleanly:\u003c/p>\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\u003ctable>\u003cthead>\u003ctr>\u003cth>Management skill\u003c/th>\u003cth>Carries over to agents?\u003c/th>\u003cth>Why\u003c/th>\u003c/tr>\u003c/thead>\u003ctbody>\u003ctr>\u003ctd>Coaching and growing people\u003c/td>\u003ctd>No\u003c/td>\u003ctd>It builds a person over time, and the agent starts fresh every session\u003c/td>\u003c/tr>\u003ctr>\u003ctd>Motivation, trust, psychological safety\u003c/td>\u003ctd>No\u003c/td>\u003ctd>They live in a relationship, and nothing carries one over\u003c/td>\u003c/tr>\u003ctr>\u003ctd>Writing the brief\u003c/td>\u003ctd>Yes\u003c/td>\u003ctd>Same job: give context the reader lacks\u003c/td>\u003c/tr>\u003ctr>\u003ctd>Defining done\u003c/td>\u003ctd>Yes\u003c/td>\u003ctd>Done has to be stated, not assumed\u003c/td>\u003c/tr>\u003ctr>\u003ctd>Checking the work\u003c/td>\u003ctd>Yes\u003c/td>\u003ctd>A report of done is a claim until someone checks it\u003c/td>\u003c/tr>\u003ctr>\u003ctd>Writing lessons down\u003c/td>\u003ctd>Yes, and it matters most\u003c/td>\u003ctd>What’s said in one session is gone in the next\u003c/td>\u003c/tr>\u003c/tbody>\u003c/table>\n\u003chr>\n\u003ch2 id=\"a-correction-in-conversation-lasts-one-session\">A Correction in Conversation Lasts One Session\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>I counted the 201 with a small script that reads my agent transcripts. It picks out the nudges by their shape: short messages like “merge it,” “next,” “keep going.” Then it sorts them by where the agent stopped: before a push, before a merge, before cleanup. A stop that came right after a permission check refused the agent goes in a separate pile, because there the agent had no choice.\u003c/p>\n\u003cp>On October 9, I replaced the line. The new instruction says the work isn’t finished until it’s committed, pushed, merged, and cleaned up, and to do those steps without being asked.\u003c/p>\n\u003cp>In the ten days after, nudges to push, open a pull request, or merge fell from about one message in seven to about one in thirty: 8 of 246, against 201 of 1,422.\u003c/p>\n\u003cp>The same day, I also changed the permission settings, the rules that decide which actions an agent can take without asking me. I let agents merge their own pull requests, and I described opening one as routine to the system that reviews agent actions. So the merge and pull request numbers can’t all be credited to the line.\u003c/p>\n\u003cp>Pushes are the cleaner case. A standing permission already let agents push without asking before the change, so permissions can’t explain a drop there. Nudges asking for a push went from about 30 in two weeks to none, apart from two that were really new instructions. At the old share of my messages, the second window would still have had about five.\u003c/p>\n\u003cp>The other limits stay next to the numbers:\u003c/p>\n\u003cul>\n\u003cli>I typed far less overall in the second window, about 25 messages a day against about 100\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>The script sorts messages by pattern, so some land in the wrong pile\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>It’s one person’s sessions, before and after, not a study\u003c/li>\n\u003c/ul>\n\u003cp>So the push numbers are the ones I’d lean on. About thirty corrections typed in conversation never changed what the next session did. One changed line did.\u003c/p>\n\u003chr>\n\u003ch2 id=\"writing-it-down-isnt-the-whole-skill\">Writing It Down Isn’t the Whole Skill\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>The written half has its own failure.\u003c/p>\n\u003cp>An earlier test, in August, showed it. I added a short brief that loads at the start of every session, alongside the standing instructions, to orient the agent. Ten days later, a hand count of the corrections in my messages had barely moved: 17 corrections in 272 messages, against 14 in 194 before. That’s too few to show an effect either way.\u003c/p>\n\u003cp>The more useful number was in what those corrections were about. Of the 17, 9 concerned a rule already written into the standing instructions:\u003c/p>\n\u003cul>\n\u003cli>Verify before you claim something works\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Don’t over-engineer\u003c/li>\n\u003c/ul>\n\u003cp>The agent had the rule in front of it and did the other thing anyway. Reaching an agent isn’t the same as being followed. My agents followed “Push after commit when asked” to the letter and the rules I cared about unevenly, and the data doesn’t say why.\u003c/p>\n\u003cp>For a rule that keeps getting ignored, I’ve started adding a mechanical check: a small program that runs before a risky action and refuses it when the rule isn’t met. The first covers the tool correction I’d repeated most: it stops an agent from publishing a social post unless it has named the publishing route my rules require. I haven’t measured whether the checks work.\u003c/p>\n\u003cp>So the written half has two parts: the instructions, and a check for each rule the instructions alone don’t hold.\u003c/p>\n\u003chr>\n\u003ch2 id=\"what-would-prove-this-wrong\">What Would Prove This Wrong\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>This argument rests on one person’s sessions, so it should make predictions other people can check.\u003c/p>\n\u003cp>If the written half is the skill, the people who already have it are the ones who’ve had to write for a reader who wasn’t in the room:\u003c/p>\n\u003cul>\n\u003cli>Leads who write handoff notes before they rotate off a project\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Consultants who write the scope before anyone is staffed\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Tech leads who write the runbook for whoever is on call next\u003c/li>\n\u003c/ul>\n\u003cp>They should do well with agents early, whether or not they’ve managed anyone. And strong people-managers who led by being in the room should struggle until they start writing things down.\u003c/p>\n\u003cp>If great coaches do well with agents without any of the written half, the argument is wrong, and temperament deserves another look.\u003c/p>\n\u003chr>\n\u003ch2 id=\"no-numbers-for-the-checks-yet\">No Numbers for the Checks Yet\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>Those are the next counts: whether the checks hold, and whether anyone else’s sessions look like mine.\u003c/p>\n\u003cp>What I do have is a place to look when an agent keeps doing the wrong thing: what it reads first, and what stops it when it ignores that.\u003c/p>\n\u003cp>The written line behind those 201 messages was five words long.\u003c/p> ","headings":[{"id":"management-is-half-the-answer","text":"Management Is Half the Answer"},{"id":"my-coaching-rubric-has-no-box-for-an-agent","text":"My Coaching Rubric Has No Box for an Agent"},{"id":"the-onboarding-never-ends","text":"The Onboarding Never Ends"},{"id":"a-correction-in-conversation-lasts-one-session","text":"A Correction in Conversation Lasts One Session"},{"id":"writing-it-down-isnt-the-whole-skill","text":"Writing It Down Isn’t the Whole Skill"},{"id":"what-would-prove-this-wrong","text":"What Would Prove This Wrong"},{"id":"no-numbers-for-the-checks-yet","text":"No Numbers for the Checks Yet"}],"image":"assets/the-hire-who-never-remembers.webp"},{"slug":"theres-no-replicator-yet","title":"There's No Replicator Yet","excerpt":"An AI agent did nearly all of a family member's data entry, missed one or two items, and almost got written off. All-or-nothing is a replicator's bar, and nothing I use with AI clears it yet.","date":"2026-10-14T17:06:48.000Z","category":"AI & Automation","tags":["ai-development","agentic-systems","reflection"],"series":null,"minutes":5,"url":"https://ninochavez.co/blog/theres-no-replicator-yet"},{"slug":"a-product-prototype-someone-else-can-build","title":"A product prototype that carries its decisions","excerpt":"A wedding product proposal became a public walkthrough, an interactive fictional prototype, and a planning handoff that carries the product decisions without exposing the original conversation.","date":"2026-10-10T05:00:00.000Z","category":"AI & Automation","tags":["ai-development","architecture","field-notes"],"series":null,"minutes":5,"url":"https://ninochavez.co/blog/a-product-prototype-someone-else-can-build"},{"slug":"making-club-videos-without-opening-an-editor","title":"Making club videos without opening a video editor","excerpt":"Our club-video work moved from hand editing in CapCut to agent-authored HyperFrames compositions and automated renders. The finished campaign shows what changed—and what still needed a person.","date":"2026-10-10T05:00:00.000Z","category":"AI & Automation","tags":["agentic-systems","photography","field-notes"],"series":null,"minutes":5,"url":"https://ninochavez.co/blog/making-club-videos-without-opening-an-editor"},{"slug":"photography-reports-that-help-me-choose","title":"Photography reports that help me choose","excerpt":"A photography report becomes useful when it helps choose what to share or revisit, without mistaking browser activity for people, quality, or proof of growth.","date":"2026-10-10T05:00:00.000Z","category":"Photography","tags":["photography","ai-development","field-notes"],"series":null,"minutes":5,"url":"https://ninochavez.co/blog/photography-reports-that-help-me-choose"},{"slug":"the-work-doesnt-end-at-send","title":"The Work Doesn’t End at Send","excerpt":"AI can make a request easier to send without making it easier to fulfill. Jevons paradox and doing less offer a way to examine the work left behind.","date":"2026-09-29T20:26:52.000Z","category":"AI & Automation","tags":["ai","productivity","ai-agents"],"series":null,"minutes":6,"url":"https://ninochavez.co/blog/the-work-doesnt-end-at-send"},{"slug":"trying-to-teach-an-ai-how-i-edit-photos","title":"Trying to teach an AI how I edit photos","excerpt":"We built a Lightroom editing experiment and ran blind comparisons against a fixed recipe. The results led me to ask what the work had cost and why we kept going.","date":"2026-09-29T00:53:22.000Z","category":"AI & Automation","tags":["photography","ai-development","experimentation"],"series":null,"minutes":6,"url":"https://ninochavez.co/blog/trying-to-teach-an-ai-how-i-edit-photos","html":" \u003cp>\u003cem>Feature image: AI-generated illustration of comparing edits; not photographs or results from the experiment.\u003c/em>\u003c/p>\n\u003cp>I wanted to see whether we could build something that learned how I edit photos and applied those edits in Lightroom. I already use presets. The point was to get a better starting point for the photos that still need work.\u003c/p>\n\u003cp>Early in the session, I asked what we were actually training. Were we training a model, or just steering one? Would we need open weights? A small model or a large one?\u003c/p>\n\u003cp>I also said development time and effort were negligible because coding agents were doing the work.\u003c/p>\n\u003chr>\n\u003ch2 id=\"comparing-the-edits\">Comparing the edits\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>We got the Lightroom part working. The system could recover original photographs and their saved editing settings, propose new settings, apply them to isolated copies, and render the results for comparison. It could do that without changing the originals.\u003c/p>\n\u003cp>We tried both approaches I had asked about. Vision models looked at images and suggested settings. Small trained models used examples of my saved edits to predict settings for another photograph. We had the actual Lightroom settings available, so we didn’t have to infer every slider from the appearance of a finished JPEG.\u003c/p>\n\u003cp>We ran blind, side-by-side tests. For each photograph, I saw two edited versions: one using settings the model predicted for that photo, the other using a fixed recipe derived from the training edits. The method names stayed hidden while I chose which version I preferred, with the option to call it a tie. We revealed the methods after I finished rating the set.\u003c/p>\n\u003cp>In the first ten-photo test, my choices turned out to favor the fixed recipe six times, with four ties. We repeated the process with a simpler learned model on the same photos. That time I preferred the fixed recipe seven times and called three ties. I hadn’t chosen the learned edit in either round.\u003c/p>\n\u003cp>We also checked whether changing the Lightroom profile and HDR settings would affect the result. In that blind review, I preferred the fixed recipe on all three photographs we compared.\u003c/p>\n\u003cp>These were small, overlapping development tests. They didn’t tell us whether the training examples were too limited, the models were using the wrong information, or the rendering context was getting in the way. They also didn’t measure finishing time against my normal preset-and-adjust workflow.\u003c/p>\n\u003cp>During the session I asked, “Is this actually showing promise of accomplishing something with greater value and utility than, say, a batch preset in Lightroom?” Later I asked whether the fixed recipe was just a preset, which I was already using, and whether there was any point in continuing.\u003c/p>\n\u003cp>The system could apply different edits to each photograph. That capability would only help me if those edits left less work to finish. The comparisons hadn’t shown that, and we stopped tuning that predictor configuration. The result doesn’t rule out personalized editing. It limits what we can claim about the approach we tested.\u003c/p>\n\u003chr>\n\u003ch2 id=\"what-the-session-cost\">What the session cost\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>When I asked for a dollar estimate, the main chat’s token record worked out to about $795 at standard API rates. It didn’t include separately dispatched workers or the photo models’ own API calls. The actual service tier and charge to my account were unverified.\u003c/p>\n\u003cp>The research plan had proposed a $100 ceiling for inference, training, and diffusion trials. That didn’t cover the coding conversation: the agent reading files, writing code, checking results, and supervising the experiment.\u003c/p>\n\u003cp>At the snapshot used for the estimate, that conversation had processed about 592.8 million tokens across 3,682 recorded model responses. Most were cached input—previous material supplied again at a discounted rate. The median request included about 164,000 input tokens. Cached input accounted for roughly $577 of the estimate.\u003c/p>\n\u003cp>Those numbers describe repeated processing, not that much new writing. They explain why shortening the conversation is worth considering. They don’t tell us which work could have been avoided, or how much an earlier stop would have saved.\u003c/p>\n\u003cp>My next question was what guardrails we could build: clearing context more often, recognizing diminishing returns, or something else. I called it “a lot of wasted value, time, effort, and token quota.”\u003c/p>\n\u003chr>\n\u003ch2 id=\"changes-to-the-next-experiment\">Changes to the next experiment\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>The plan now calls for tracking the whole task, including the coding conversation and its workers. It also calls for limits on attempts and requested human reviews. Reusing an existing image can avoid another render, but it still takes my time to judge another comparison.\u003c/p>\n\u003cp>Before another experiment runs, the agent should state what it expects to learn and what each result would change. If an export defect prevented a valid comparison, fixing that defect may be necessary. If the intended edits were applied correctly and I preferred the fixed recipe, another model or another round of tuning needs a specific reason. A negative result needs to be allowed to end the assignment.\u003c/p>\n\u003cp>My setup already has a hook, a small program that runs when the agent tries to finish, which nudges it to take an obvious next action. Its code checks the closing sentence. It doesn’t check whether another experiment is worthwhile. Only one injected message from that hook appears in the measured session, so I can’t attribute the overall cost to it. The proposed change is to make it respect a recorded stopping decision and the remaining budget.\u003c/p>\n\u003cp>A shorter conversation would also need to carry those decisions forward. The handoff should include what failed, where the evidence is, and how much of the allowance remains. Otherwise, starting fresh could mean trying an unsuccessful approach again.\u003c/p>\n\u003cp>These controls are still proposed. They need to be tested on both cases: work that should stop and useful work that should continue. For now, we’ve kept the tools for recovering edits, applying them safely, and comparing the results. We haven’t demonstrated that the learned editor saves me time finishing a gallery.\u003c/p>\n\u003cp>Reviewing the photographs was only part of my involvement. I also had to ask whether the experiment was worth continuing, what it had cost, and what we should change after it failed to show an advantage. That retrospective happened because I asked for it. Keeping a human in the loop includes making room for those questions while the work is still going on.\u003c/p>\n\u003chr>\n\u003cp>\u003cem>Evidence note: Results and usage come from the September 26–28, 2026 Lightroom experiment and its saved review records. The cost snapshot ends at 23:29:57 UTC on September 28, before this retrospective. It contains 13,813,667 uncached input tokens, 577,391,872 cached input tokens, and 1,595,367 output tokens. The estimate applies GPT-6 Astra standard short-context rates of $10, $1, and $50 per million respectively, from \u003ca href=\"https://developers.openai.com/api/docs/pricing\">OpenAI’s pricing page\u003c/a>, checked September 28. That yields $795.30; Fast/priority rates would yield $1,590.59. The actual service tier and account charge are unverified. Worker costs, external photography API costs, and a dollar valuation of human time are excluded. No precise fraction of this cost has been established as avoidable.\u003c/em>\u003c/p> ","headings":[{"id":"comparing-the-edits","text":"Comparing the edits"},{"id":"what-the-session-cost","text":"What the session cost"},{"id":"changes-to-the-next-experiment","text":"Changes to the next experiment"}],"image":"assets/trying-to-teach-an-ai-how-i-edit-photos.webp"},{"slug":"the-connector-you-cant-oauth","title":"The Connector You Can't OAuth","excerpt":"I asked the new Siri what it could infer from a week of texts. The answer was not a better chatbot. It was inference over a graph no OAuth connector gets.","date":"2026-09-15T22:00:00.000Z","category":"AI & Automation","tags":["agentic-systems","product-strategy","ai-development"],"series":null,"minutes":6,"url":"https://ninochavez.co/blog/the-connector-you-cant-oauth"},{"slug":"give-your-ai-a-reason-for-every-design-reference","title":"Give Your AI a Reason for Every Design Reference","excerpt":"A useful design reference may come from a completely different product. The work is identifying the decision it helps with, then telling the agent which part should transfer.","date":"2026-09-09T19:09:15.488Z","category":"AI Development","tags":["ai-development","product-design","design-references"],"series":null,"minutes":4,"url":"https://ninochavez.co/blog/give-your-ai-a-reason-for-every-design-reference"},{"slug":"who-is-still-driving","title":"Who Is Still Driving?","excerpt":"Using AI takes practice, but a product that needs an expert to catch its mistakes should say so before someone relies on it.","date":"2026-09-06T21:26:24.000Z","category":"AI & Automation","tags":["agentic-systems","ai-development","ai-governance"],"series":null,"minutes":4,"url":"https://ninochavez.co/blog/who-is-still-driving"},{"slug":"rigorous-about-the-wrong-question","title":"Rigorous About the Wrong Question","excerpt":"I asked an AI agent to audit my app for the person it was built for. It sent five sub-agents to check documents and none of them looked at the screen.","date":"2026-09-03T18:00:00.000Z","category":"AI & Automation","tags":["agentic-systems","ai-governance","ai-development"],"series":null,"minutes":6,"url":"https://ninochavez.co/blog/rigorous-about-the-wrong-question"},{"slug":"the-audit-reproduced-the-failure","title":"The Audit Reproduced the Failure","excerpt":"A small inspection of one runaway session became repeated parallel work before it had earned that expansion. The 119-of-118 report was the alarm.","date":"2026-08-23T18:00:00.000Z","category":"AI & Automation","tags":["agentic-systems","ai-development","ai-governance"],"series":null,"minutes":6,"url":"https://ninochavez.co/blog/the-audit-reproduced-the-failure"},{"slug":"nobody-owns-what-the-agent-leaves-running","title":"Nobody Owns What the Agent Leaves Running","excerpt":"My machine ran out of resources, so I handed an agent four screenshots and typed one word: fix. It did a good job. A day later, twenty-two connector processes were still running, every one of them marked disabled in config. Nothing had failed — and that turns out to be the interesting part.","date":"2026-08-23T13:00:00.000Z","category":"AI & Automation","tags":["agentic-systems","ai-development","ai-governance"],"series":null,"minutes":7,"url":"https://ninochavez.co/blog/nobody-owns-what-the-agent-leaves-running"},{"slug":"everything-i-was-afraid-of-worked","title":"Everything I Was Afraid of Worked","excerpt":"Last October I published a post arguing that agent safety is an architecture problem: the model proposes, hard-coded code disposes, a human approves. Then I built exactly that for a youth sports club's website and used it for an hour. I found seven things wrong with it. None of them were in the part I had been afraid of.","date":"2026-08-17T14:00:00.000Z","category":"AI & Automation","tags":["agentic-systems","ai-development","software-architecture"],"series":null,"minutes":10,"url":"https://ninochavez.co/blog/everything-i-was-afraid-of-worked"},{"slug":"the-panic-log-says-signal-not-shortage","title":"The Panic Log Says Signal, Not Shortage","excerpt":"A local model run took my Mac down at 10:55:33. Recovering the research cost about twenty minutes. Writing down the wrong reason for the crash cost more, and the file that proves it wrong ships with the operating system.","date":"2026-08-15T17:40:00.000Z","category":"AI & Automation","tags":["local-first","ai-assisted-development"],"series":null,"minutes":6,"url":"https://ninochavez.co/blog/the-panic-log-says-signal-not-shortage"},{"slug":"product-facts-not-fine-print","title":"Product Facts, Not Fine Print","excerpt":"The download page for a meeting notepad I shipped lists what the app refuses to do — no automatic sharing, no promise of completeness, no substitute for consent — above the download button, in the marketing copy. That placement is the design decision this piece is about.","date":"2026-08-13T23:17:40.000Z","category":"AI & Automation","tags":["product-design","local-first"],"series":null,"minutes":4,"url":"https://ninochavez.co/blog/product-facts-not-fine-print"},{"slug":"the-guardrail-removed-the-storefront","title":"The Guardrail Removed the Storefront","excerpt":"A safe AI storefront cannot give the model a blank canvas. It also cannot remove the model and still claim that inference changes the shopping experience.","date":"2026-08-13T23:00:00.000Z","category":"AI & Automation","tags":["commerce","ai-governance","architecture","product-strategy"],"series":null,"minutes":8,"url":"https://ninochavez.co/blog/the-guardrail-removed-the-storefront"},{"slug":"the-agent-needs-a-safety-label","title":"The Agent Needs a Safety Label","excerpt":"AI standards already exist. What teams still need is a current, workflow-level record of what one configured agent may do, how it was tested, where it must stop, and when its evidence expires.","date":"2026-08-13T15:57:30.000Z","category":"AI & Automation","tags":["ai-governance","agentic-systems","systems-thinking"],"series":null,"minutes":7,"url":"https://ninochavez.co/blog/the-agent-needs-a-safety-label"},{"slug":"nine-rgb-points-from-claude-coral","title":"Nine RGB Points from Claude Coral","excerpt":"A colleague looked at my freshly redesigned site and typed one line into Slack: it feels very Claudeish. He was more right than he knew — the accent color measured nine RGB points from Claude's own coral. The interesting part is where the sameness actually came from.","date":"2026-08-12T16:00:00.000Z","category":"AI & Automation","tags":["design-systems","agentic-ai","taste"],"series":null,"minutes":5,"url":"https://ninochavez.co/blog/nine-rgb-points-from-claude-coral"},{"slug":"the-depth-penalty-is-gone","title":"The Depth Penalty Is Gone","excerpt":"I spent five days inside a volleyball club's operations — WordPress, a marketing CRM, a registration API, consent law. None of it is my domain. That stopped mattering, and the reason it stopped is the interesting part.","date":"2026-08-09T20:00:00.000Z","category":"AI & Automation","tags":["agentic-ai","consulting-practice"],"series":null,"minutes":5,"url":"https://ninochavez.co/blog/the-depth-penalty-is-gone"},{"slug":"there-was-never-a-person-in-that-seat","title":"There Was Never a Person in That Seat","excerpt":"Fixed-task studies measure whether AI helps with familiar work. My repository history raises a different question: does AI change the range of work one person attempts?","date":"2026-08-08T00:10:00.000Z","category":"AI & Automation","tags":["ai-development","career"],"series":null,"minutes":5,"url":"https://ninochavez.co/blog/there-was-never-a-person-in-that-seat"},{"slug":"the-loudest-voice-in-my-voice-corpus-isnt-mine","title":"The Loudest Voice in My Voice Corpus Isn't Mine","excerpt":"A pipeline mines my agent sessions into a character sheet that gets loaded as context. The top-ranked correction in it is a deny message from a hook I installed, quoted back at me across 117 sessions.","date":"2026-08-07T21:40:00.000Z","category":"AI Development","tags":["ai-development","agentic-systems"],"series":null,"minutes":7,"url":"https://ninochavez.co/blog/the-loudest-voice-in-my-voice-corpus-isnt-mine"},{"slug":"the-cut-youll-make-twice","title":"The Cut You'll Make Twice","excerpt":"A written rule is not a control until the system has a place to enforce it and a failure record that proves what happened.","date":"2026-08-06T22:32:46.000Z","category":"AI & Automation","tags":["agentic-systems","ai-development","architecture"],"series":null,"minutes":5,"url":"https://ninochavez.co/blog/the-cut-youll-make-twice"},{"slug":"the-style-guide-was-teaching-the-tells","title":"The Style Guide Was Teaching the Tells","excerpt":"I asked an AI to audit the style guides that teach AI to write like me. The tone card was describing machine messages, the guide's examples had become tells, and a checker was grading drafts for phrases the corpus abandoned months ago.","date":"2026-08-03T23:30:00.000Z","category":"AI Development","tags":["ai-governance","ai-development"],"series":null,"minutes":5,"url":"https://ninochavez.co/blog/the-style-guide-was-teaching-the-tells"},{"slug":"one-component-i-didnt-already-have","title":"One Component I Didn't Already Have","excerpt":"A coding-agent plugin with 66,000 stars keeps landing in my inbox. The version that could actually run on my machine ships eight components. Seven of them my setup already does, can't use, or would pay for twice.","date":"2026-07-31T15:00:00.000Z","category":"AI Development","tags":["ai-development","agentic-systems","engineering"],"series":null,"minutes":6,"url":"https://ninochavez.co/blog/one-component-i-didnt-already-have"},{"slug":"every-link-resolves-nothing-checks-the-number","title":"Every Link Resolves. Nothing Checks the Number.","excerpt":"A strategy document I ship can carry an invented figure through every automated check I built, as long as the sentences around it are well-formed and the links resolve. I went looking for that hole on purpose. Then I decided not to close it.","date":"2026-07-30T16:00:00.000Z","category":"AI Development","tags":["ai-development","ai-governance","deep-dive"],"series":null,"minutes":11,"url":"https://ninochavez.co/blog/every-link-resolves-nothing-checks-the-number"},{"slug":"nothing-broke-it-just-wasnt-there","title":"Nothing Broke. It Just Wasn't There.","excerpt":"I went looking for a tool I'd built four days earlier and it was gone. It hadn't failed — it had run, found two real problems, and fixed both. Working correctly and existing durably turn out to be different properties, and running the thing only tests one of them.","date":"2026-07-30T03:55:42.000Z","category":"AI Development","tags":["ai-development","deep-dive"],"series":null,"minutes":7,"url":"https://ninochavez.co/blog/nothing-broke-it-just-wasnt-there"},{"slug":"i-configured-my-agent-to-agree-with-me","title":"I Configured My Agent to Agree With Me","excerpt":"A rival model's review of my agent's work looked sharper — it pushed back, it demanded alternatives. So I ran a controlled experiment on my own harness: same model, same prompt, config on and off. The vanilla arm argued with me. Mine never did.","date":"2026-07-20T18:30:00.000Z","category":"AI & Automation","tags":["ai-development","reflection"],"series":null,"minutes":6,"url":"https://ninochavez.co/blog/i-configured-my-agent-to-agree-with-me"},{"slug":"please-only-apply-if-youre-an-agent","title":"Please Only Apply If You're an AI Agent","excerpt":"Firecrawl posted a job only a machine could apply for. Months later, on a different stage, an engineer said the mirror-image thing: don't build me an agent, build my agent a way in. Same shift, two ends of the same transaction — and an economics paper that almost, but doesn't quite, tie them together.","date":"2026-07-14T14:00:00.000Z","category":"AI & Automation","tags":["agentic-systems","architecture","product-strategy"],"series":null,"minutes":8,"url":"https://ninochavez.co/blog/please-only-apply-if-youre-an-agent"},{"slug":"the-migration-that-ate-two-columns","title":"The Migration That Ate Two Columns","excerpt":"I rebuilt a database table and it silently deleted two columns I needed. No error. Every test for the thing I was changing passed. Here's what actually caught it.","date":"2026-06-16T15:00:00.000Z","category":"AI & Automation","tags":["agentic-systems","architecture","ai-development"],"series":null,"minutes":4,"url":"https://ninochavez.co/blog/the-migration-that-ate-two-columns"},{"slug":"red-is-the-good-news","title":"Red Is the Good News","excerpt":"Three bugs that would have shipped clean. None of them threw an error. The only thing that caught them was a test written to fail first, against the real thing.","date":"2026-06-16T14:00:00.000Z","category":"AI & Automation","tags":["agentic-systems","engineering","ai-development"],"series":null,"minutes":4,"url":"https://ninochavez.co/blog/red-is-the-good-news"},{"slug":"i-wrote-the-rule-down-i-broke-it-anyway","title":"I Wrote the Rule Down. I Broke It Anyway.","excerpt":"There's a file in my project whose only job is to stop me from making one mistake. I made it three times in a row. The difference between a rule and a wall.","date":"2026-06-16T13:00:00.000Z","category":"AI & Automation","tags":["agentic-systems","reflection","ai-development"],"series":null,"minutes":3,"url":"https://ninochavez.co/blog/i-wrote-the-rule-down-i-broke-it-anyway"},{"slug":"the-gate-verifies-the-work-not-the-plan","title":"The Gate Verifies the Work. It Never Looks at the Plan.","excerpt":"Thirteen features shipped green. The designs were all wrong. The system made being wrong cheap—but only on one side of the work.","date":"2026-06-16T12:00:00.000Z","category":"AI & Automation","tags":["agentic-systems","architecture","ai-development"],"series":null,"minutes":5,"url":"https://ninochavez.co/blog/the-gate-verifies-the-work-not-the-plan"},{"slug":"where-the-api-stops","title":"Where the API Stops","excerpt":"I once mapped the civil war between human interfaces and machine-native ones at the scale of companies. Then I fought both sides of it in a single afternoon, in a single pipeline — and a human had to close the seam.","date":"2026-06-15T14:00:00.000Z","category":"AI & Automation","tags":["agentic-systems","architecture","field-notes"],"series":{"slug":"the-bifurcation","position":3},"minutes":8,"url":"https://ninochavez.co/blog/where-the-api-stops"},{"slug":"open-kitchen","title":"Open Kitchen","excerpt":"I went looking for other cooks and found closed kitchens — not hostile, just complete. The Bear turned out to be the show that named what's actually missing in agent-assisted work, and what it might cost me to build the alternative.","date":"2026-06-11T15:10:00.000Z","category":"Reflection","tags":["career","agentic-systems","reflection"],"series":null,"minutes":5,"url":"https://ninochavez.co/blog/open-kitchen"},{"slug":"the-only-thing-that-cant-lie","title":"The Only Thing in My Codebase That Can't Lie","excerpt":"I asked an agent a simple product question and watched it spend three thousand words rediscovering things it had no way to trust. The fix wasn't a better map. It was noticing which artifacts in a codebase can lie to you — and which one can't.","date":"2026-05-30T17:00:00.000Z","category":"Engineering","tags":["ai-development","engineering","ai-governance"],"series":null,"minutes":9,"url":"https://ninochavez.co/blog/the-only-thing-that-cant-lie"},{"slug":"when-your-lawyer-tells-you-to-use-chatgpt","title":"When Your Lawyer Tells You to Use ChatGPT","excerpt":"My lawyer told me to use ChatGPT instead of paying him. A colleague said the same about his accountant. The cleanest signal for which professions get compressed isn't the headcount data — it's when the practitioner himself tells you he's optional.","date":"2026-05-26T18:00:00.000Z","category":"AI & Automation","tags":["career","ai-development","reflection"],"series":null,"minutes":6,"url":"https://ninochavez.co/blog/when-your-lawyer-tells-you-to-use-chatgpt"},{"slug":"the-backport-i-didnt-make","title":"The A/B Test That Built the Lathe","excerpt":"I pasted a two-paragraph plan into a terminal and walked away for two days. The strategy package that came back was good. Then I asked Claude to do it again from a template, without me in the feedback loop. The template version landed at 70-80% of the quality in 10-15x less time. The gap — and the loop that closed it — has a name from another century.","date":"2026-05-24T22:00:00.000Z","category":"Reflection","tags":["architecture","agentic-systems","ai-development"],"series":null,"minutes":8,"url":"https://ninochavez.co/blog/the-backport-i-didnt-make"},{"slug":"the-scaffolding-the-agent-doesnt-build","title":"The Scaffolding the Agent Doesn't Build","excerpt":"I sent Adam Bender's Google I/O talk to Claude with a note calling it the most insightful take I'd heard on AI and our industry, and asked for a LinkedIn caption. The draft came back confessing things about my work that weren't true. The story of why is the same story Bender is telling on a Google I/O stage.","date":"2026-05-24T16:00:00.000Z","category":"Reflection","tags":["agentic-systems","ai-development","reflection"],"series":null,"minutes":5,"url":"https://ninochavez.co/blog/the-scaffolding-the-agent-doesnt-build"},{"slug":"i-dont-write-any-code","title":"I Don't Write Any Code","excerpt":"I spent a session trying to map my skills across sixty-five projects and the output came back dressed up as a senior engineer. That wasn't right. The correction I typed back is the one I think a lot of people are currently refusing to type back.","date":"2026-04-21T15:00:00.000Z","category":"Reflection","tags":["career","reflection","agentic-systems"],"series":null,"minutes":5,"url":"https://ninochavez.co/blog/i-dont-write-any-code"},{"slug":"the-question-that-isnt-a-question","title":"The Question That Isn't a Question","excerpt":"Someone asks you a question. You answer it carefully. They don't react to your answer — they ask the next question. That's when you realize the first one wasn't a question. The move has a shape, and it has a specific cost to builders who share work in drafts.","date":"2026-04-21T15:00:00.000Z","category":"Reflection","tags":["reflection","communication","engineering"],"series":null,"minutes":6,"url":"https://ninochavez.co/blog/the-question-that-isnt-a-question"},{"slug":"the-side-project-is-pottery","title":"The Side Project Is Pottery","excerpt":"I keep a drawer full of side projects I'll never ship. I used to tell myself they were portfolio, optionality, businesses in waiting. I don't believe that story anymore — and I don't think most of the people telling it believe it either.","date":"2026-04-20T15:00:00.000Z","category":"Meta","tags":["reflection","career","ai-development"],"series":null,"minutes":12,"url":"https://ninochavez.co/blog/the-side-project-is-pottery"},{"slug":"curation-is-not-composition","title":"Curation Is Not Composition","excerpt":"Every creative medium that got cheap hit this moment. Art, music, publishing — all flooded, all eventually rescued by a curation layer that scaled up to match the garbage. Software is in the flood now. Curation isn't going to save it, and I don't think we've noticed the reason yet.","date":"2026-04-17T15:00:00.000Z","category":"AI & Automation","tags":["ai-development","architecture","engineering"],"series":null,"minutes":11,"url":"https://ninochavez.co/blog/curation-is-not-composition"},{"slug":"the-walk-in-is-full","title":"The Walk-In Is Full","excerpt":"I ship an end-to-end project most Saturdays and put it in a drawer I never reopen. So does everyone I know. The walk-in is full — not with abundance, but with relocated scarcity. What looked like software culture was partly muscle memory, an involuntary reflex held up by cost, and it's atrophying now that nothing fires it.","date":"2026-04-13T15:00:00.000Z","category":"AI & Automation","tags":["ai-development","engineering","reflection"],"series":null,"minutes":10,"url":"https://ninochavez.co/blog/the-walk-in-is-full"},{"slug":"when-the-grid-goes-dark","title":"When the Grid Goes Dark","excerpt":"AI stopped being a tool the moment I couldn't work without it. That's not adoption — that's dependency. And dependency without understanding is just a blackout waiting to happen.","date":"2026-04-06T12:00:00.000Z","category":"AI & Automation","tags":["ai-development","leadership","career"],"series":null,"minutes":7,"url":"https://ninochavez.co/blog/when-the-grid-goes-dark"},{"slug":"the-masterchef-problem","title":"The MasterChef Problem","excerpt":"AI gave everyone a professional kitchen. But MasterChef didn't make chefs by handing out equipment — it made them by designing challenges that exposed who could actually cook. Digital product development is missing the challenges.","date":"2026-03-27T12:00:00.000Z","category":"AI & Automation","tags":["ai-development","career"],"series":null,"minutes":8,"url":"https://ninochavez.co/blog/the-masterchef-problem"},{"slug":"skip-the-steps","title":"Skip the Steps","excerpt":"Supabase added a 'Copy prompt' button that bypasses their entire setup guide. It's a small UI change that reveals a big design shift — and it pushed me from 'context engineering' to 'probability engineering' as the real frame for agent-native tooling.","date":"2026-03-25T12:00:00.000Z","category":"Engineering","tags":["agentic-systems","engineering","ai-development"],"series":null,"minutes":9,"url":"https://ninochavez.co/blog/skip-the-steps"},{"slug":"setting-up-an-ai-native-dev-environment","title":"Setting Up an AI-Native Dev Environment","excerpt":"The practical companion to 'What 223 Sessions Taught Me.' How to set up CLAUDE.md files, the memory system, custom skills, project-scoped conversations, and the infrastructure that makes AI-assisted development actually sustainable.","date":"2026-03-20T14:00:00.000Z","category":"AI & Automation","tags":["ai-development","field-notes","engineering"],"series":null,"minutes":9,"url":"https://ninochavez.co/blog/setting-up-an-ai-native-dev-environment","html":" \u003cp>This is the companion post to \u003ca href=\"/blog/what-223-sessions-taught-me-about-working-with-ai\">What 223 Sessions Taught Me About Working with AI\u003c/a>. That post was the data. This one is the how.\u003c/p>\n\u003cp>Everything here assumes you’re using \u003ca href=\"https://claude.ai/claude-code\">Claude Code\u003c/a> — Anthropic’s CLI tool for agentic development. The principles apply to other AI coding tools, but the specifics are Claude Code-native.\u003c/p>\n\u003chr>\n\u003ch2 id=\"the-directory-structure\">The Directory Structure\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>Before anything else: how you organize your workspace matters more than you’d think.\u003c/p>\n\u003cp>AI tools scope conversations to the directory you launch them from. If all your projects live in one flat folder, every session drowns in irrelevant context. If they’re organized by purpose, each session starts with only the context it needs.\u003c/p>\n\u003cp>Here’s how mine looks:\u003c/p>\n\u003cpre class=\"astro-code github-dark\" style=\"background-color:#24292e;color:#e1e4e8;overflow-x:auto\" tabindex=\"0\" data-language=\"plaintext\">\u003ccode>\u003cspan class=\"line\">\u003cspan>~/Workspace/dev/\u003c/span>\u003c/span>\n\u003cspan class=\"line\">\u003cspan>├── apps/           # Production applications\u003c/span>\u003c/span>\n\u003cspan class=\"line\">\u003cspan>│   ├── rally-hq/       # Tournament platform\u003c/span>\u003c/span>\n\u003cspan class=\"line\">\u003cspan>│   ├── photography/     # Photo gallery\u003c/span>\u003c/span>\n\u003cspan class=\"line\">\u003cspan>│   ├── blog/            # Signal Dispatch (this site)\u003c/span>\u003c/span>\n\u003cspan class=\"line\">\u003cspan>│   └── 630-apps/        # Volleyball org tools\u003c/span>\u003c/span>\n\u003cspan class=\"line\">\u003cspan>├── client/         # Client projects\u003c/span>\u003c/span>\n\u003cspan class=\"line\">\u003cspan>│   ├── allen-wellness-center/\u003c/span>\u003c/span>\n\u003cspan class=\"line\">\u003cspan>│   ├── creative-floors/\u003c/span>\u003c/span>\n\u003cspan class=\"line\">\u003cspan>│   └── urvil-performance/\u003c/span>\u003c/span>\n\u003cspan class=\"line\">\u003cspan>├── wip/            # Work in progress\u003c/span>\u003c/span>\n\u003cspan class=\"line\">\u003cspan>│   ├── quantifai-lite/\u003c/span>\u003c/span>\n\u003cspan class=\"line\">\u003cspan>│   ├── partner-ai/\u003c/span>\u003c/span>\n\u003cspan class=\"line\">\u003cspan>│   └── cortex/\u003c/span>\u003c/span>\n\u003cspan class=\"line\">\u003cspan>└── tools/          # Shared internal tools\u003c/span>\u003c/span>\n\u003cspan class=\"line\">\u003cspan>    ├── image-gen/\u003c/span>\u003c/span>\n\u003cspan class=\"line\">\u003cspan>    └── signal-forge/\u003c/span>\u003c/span>\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\n\u003cp>The categories — \u003ccode>apps/\u003c/code>, \u003ccode>client/\u003c/code>, \u003ccode>wip/\u003c/code>, \u003ccode>tools/\u003c/code> — aren’t arbitrary. They signal lifecycle stage. When I open a terminal in \u003ccode>wip/quantifai-lite/\u003c/code>, Claude knows this is a project in active development without me saying so. When I’m in \u003ccode>apps/rally-hq/\u003c/code>, it knows this is production code with users.\u003c/p>\n\u003chr>\n\u003ch2 id=\"step-1-the-claudemd-file\">Step 1: The CLAUDE.md File\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>This is the most important file in the system. It’s the persistent instruction set that Claude reads on every session start.\u003c/p>\n\u003cp>Create a \u003ccode>CLAUDE.md\u003c/code> file in the root of each project. Here’s the minimal version:\u003c/p>\n\u003cpre class=\"astro-code github-dark\" style=\"background-color:#24292e;color:#e1e4e8;overflow-x:auto\" tabindex=\"0\" data-language=\"markdown\">\u003ccode>\u003cspan class=\"line\">\u003cspan style=\"color:#79B8FF;font-weight:bold\"># Project Name\u003c/span>\u003c/span>\n\u003cspan class=\"line\">\u003c/span>\n\u003cspan class=\"line\">\u003cspan style=\"color:#79B8FF;font-weight:bold\">## Stack\u003c/span>\u003c/span>\n\u003cspan class=\"line\">\u003cspan style=\"color:#FFAB70\">-\u003c/span>\u003cspan style=\"color:#E1E4E8\"> Framework, language, CSS approach\u003c/span>\u003c/span>\n\u003cspan class=\"line\">\u003cspan style=\"color:#FFAB70\">-\u003c/span>\u003cspan style=\"color:#E1E4E8\"> Database and auth provider\u003c/span>\u003c/span>\n\u003cspan class=\"line\">\u003cspan style=\"color:#FFAB70\">-\u003c/span>\u003cspan style=\"color:#E1E4E8\"> Hosting/deployment target\u003c/span>\u003c/span>\n\u003cspan class=\"line\">\u003c/span>\n\u003cspan class=\"line\">\u003cspan style=\"color:#79B8FF;font-weight:bold\">## Conventions\u003c/span>\u003c/span>\n\u003cspan class=\"line\">\u003cspan style=\"color:#FFAB70\">-\u003c/span>\u003cspan style=\"color:#E1E4E8\"> Naming patterns\u003c/span>\u003c/span>\n\u003cspan class=\"line\">\u003cspan style=\"color:#FFAB70\">-\u003c/span>\u003cspan style=\"color:#E1E4E8\"> File organization\u003c/span>\u003c/span>\n\u003cspan class=\"line\">\u003cspan style=\"color:#FFAB70\">-\u003c/span>\u003cspan style=\"color:#E1E4E8\"> Test approach\u003c/span>\u003c/span>\n\u003cspan class=\"line\">\u003c/span>\n\u003cspan class=\"line\">\u003cspan style=\"color:#79B8FF;font-weight:bold\">## Off-Limits\u003c/span>\u003c/span>\n\u003cspan class=\"line\">\u003cspan style=\"color:#FFAB70\">-\u003c/span>\u003cspan style=\"color:#E1E4E8\"> Files that should never be modified\u003c/span>\u003c/span>\n\u003cspan class=\"line\">\u003cspan style=\"color:#FFAB70\">-\u003c/span>\u003cspan style=\"color:#E1E4E8\"> Directories to skip\u003c/span>\u003c/span>\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\n\u003caside class=\"my-8 p-6 rounded-r-xl border-l-4 border-signal-coral bg-gradient-to-r from-signal-coral/10 to-transparent\" role=\"note\">\u003cdiv class=\"flex items-start gap-4\">\u003cdiv class=\"flex-shrink-0 mt-0.5\">\u003csvg xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\" width=\"24\" height=\"24\" fill=\"currentColor\" viewBox=\"0 0 256 256\" class=\"text-signal-coral\">\u003cpath d=\"M160,128a32,32,0,1,1-32-32A32,32,0,0,1,160,128Z\" opacity=\"0.2\">\u003c/path>\u003cpath d=\"M128,88a40,40,0,1,0,40,40A40,40,0,0,0,128,88Zm0,64a24,24,0,1,1,24-24A24,24,0,0,1,128,152Zm73.71,7.14a80,80,0,0,1-14.08,22.2,8,8,0,0,1-11.92-10.67,63.95,63.95,0,0,0,0-85.33,8,8,0,1,1,11.92-10.67,80.08,80.08,0,0,1,14.08,84.47ZM69,103.09a64,64,0,0,0,11.26,67.58,8,8,0,0,1-11.92,10.67,79.93,79.93,0,0,1,0-106.67A8,8,0,1,1,80.29,85.34,63.77,63.77,0,0,0,69,103.09ZM248,128a119.58,119.58,0,0,1-34.29,84,8,8,0,1,1-11.42-11.2,103.9,103.9,0,0,0,0-145.56A8,8,0,1,1,213.71,44,119.58,119.58,0,0,1,248,128ZM53.71,200.78A8,8,0,1,1,42.29,212a119.87,119.87,0,0,1,0-168,8,8,0,1,1,11.42,11.2,103.9,103.9,0,0,0,0,145.56Z\">\u003c/path>\u003c/svg>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"flex-1 space-y-2\">\u003ch4 class=\"font-bold text-sm uppercase tracking-wider text-signal-coral\">What Makes a Good CLAUDE.md\u003c/h4>\u003cdiv class=\"text-zinc-300 leading-relaxed [&amp;&gt;p]:m-0 [&amp;&gt;p]:leading-relaxed [&amp;&gt;ul]:mt-2 [&amp;&gt;ul]:space-y-1\">\u003cp>The goal isn’t documentation. It’s \u003cem>constraints\u003c/em>. Tell Claude what to do, what not to do, and where to find things. Skip the prose — bullet points work better. The file gets loaded into every session’s context window, so keep it concise.\u003c/p>\u003c/div>\u003c/div>\u003c/div>\u003c/aside>\n\u003cp>Here’s a real example from one of my projects:\u003c/p>\n\u003cpre class=\"astro-code github-dark\" style=\"background-color:#24292e;color:#e1e4e8;overflow-x:auto\" tabindex=\"0\" data-language=\"markdown\">\u003ccode>\u003cspan class=\"line\">\u003cspan style=\"color:#79B8FF;font-weight:bold\"># Rally HQ - Tournament Management Platform\u003c/span>\u003c/span>\n\u003cspan class=\"line\">\u003c/span>\n\u003cspan class=\"line\">\u003cspan style=\"color:#79B8FF;font-weight:bold\">## Stack\u003c/span>\u003c/span>\n\u003cspan class=\"line\">\u003cspan style=\"color:#FFAB70\">-\u003c/span>\u003cspan style=\"color:#E1E4E8\"> SvelteKit 2.x / Svelte 5 (runes) / TypeScript / Tailwind CSS v4\u003c/span>\u003c/span>\n\u003cspan class=\"line\">\u003cspan style=\"color:#FFAB70\">-\u003c/span>\u003cspan style=\"color:#E1E4E8\"> Supabase (PostgreSQL + Auth + Storage)\u003c/span>\u003c/span>\n\u003cspan class=\"line\">\u003cspan style=\"color:#FFAB70\">-\u003c/span>\u003cspan style=\"color:#E1E4E8\"> Vercel deployment via GitHub push\u003c/span>\u003c/span>\n\u003cspan class=\"line\">\u003c/span>\n\u003cspan class=\"line\">\u003cspan style=\"color:#79B8FF;font-weight:bold\">## Conventions\u003c/span>\u003c/span>\n\u003cspan class=\"line\">\u003cspan style=\"color:#FFAB70\">-\u003c/span>\u003cspan style=\"color:#E1E4E8\"> Components in src/lib/components/\u003c/span>\u003c/span>\n\u003cspan class=\"line\">\u003cspan style=\"color:#FFAB70\">-\u003c/span>\u003cspan style=\"color:#E1E4E8\"> Server routes in src/routes/api/\u003c/span>\u003c/span>\n\u003cspan class=\"line\">\u003cspan style=\"color:#FFAB70\">-\u003c/span>\u003cspan style=\"color:#E1E4E8\"> Supabase types auto-generated from schema\u003c/span>\u003c/span>\n\u003cspan class=\"line\">\u003cspan style=\"color:#FFAB70\">-\u003c/span>\u003cspan style=\"color:#E1E4E8\"> No emoji unless explicitly requested\u003c/span>\u003c/span>\n\u003cspan class=\"line\">\u003c/span>\n\u003cspan class=\"line\">\u003cspan style=\"color:#79B8FF;font-weight:bold\">## Off-Limits\u003c/span>\u003c/span>\n\u003cspan class=\"line\">\u003cspan style=\"color:#FFAB70\">-\u003c/span>\u003cspan style=\"color:#E1E4E8\"> .env* files\u003c/span>\u003c/span>\n\u003cspan class=\"line\">\u003cspan style=\"color:#FFAB70\">-\u003c/span>\u003cspan style=\"color:#E1E4E8\"> supabase/migrations/ (generated, don't edit manually)\u003c/span>\u003c/span>\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\n\u003cp>The key insight: this file isn’t for you. It’s for the AI. Write it like you’re onboarding a new engineer who’s about to make their first commit.\u003c/p>\n\u003chr>\n\u003ch2 id=\"step-2-global-instructions\">Step 2: Global Instructions\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>Beyond per-project files, you can set global instructions that apply to every session regardless of which project you’re in.\u003c/p>\n\u003cp>Create \u003ccode>~/.claude/CLAUDE.md\u003c/code>:\u003c/p>\n\u003cpre class=\"astro-code github-dark\" style=\"background-color:#24292e;color:#e1e4e8;overflow-x:auto\" tabindex=\"0\" data-language=\"markdown\">\u003ccode>\u003cspan class=\"line\">\u003cspan style=\"color:#79B8FF;font-weight:bold\"># Global Context\u003c/span>\u003c/span>\n\u003cspan class=\"line\">\u003c/span>\n\u003cspan class=\"line\">\u003cspan style=\"color:#79B8FF;font-weight:bold\">## Owner\u003c/span>\u003c/span>\n\u003cspan class=\"line\">\u003cspan style=\"color:#E1E4E8\">Your name, role, what you're working on\u003c/span>\u003c/span>\n\u003cspan class=\"line\">\u003c/span>\n\u003cspan class=\"line\">\u003cspan style=\"color:#79B8FF;font-weight:bold\">## Preferences\u003c/span>\u003c/span>\n\u003cspan class=\"line\">\u003cspan style=\"color:#FFAB70\">-\u003c/span>\u003cspan style=\"color:#E1E4E8\"> Commit message style\u003c/span>\u003c/span>\n\u003cspan class=\"line\">\u003cspan style=\"color:#FFAB70\">-\u003c/span>\u003cspan style=\"color:#E1E4E8\"> Whether to push after commit\u003c/span>\u003c/span>\n\u003cspan class=\"line\">\u003cspan style=\"color:#FFAB70\">-\u003c/span>\u003cspan style=\"color:#E1E4E8\"> Output verbosity\u003c/span>\u003c/span>\n\u003cspan class=\"line\">\u003cspan style=\"color:#FFAB70\">-\u003c/span>\u003cspan style=\"color:#E1E4E8\"> Any stylistic preferences\u003c/span>\u003c/span>\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\n\u003cp>This is where personality-level preferences go. Things like “no emoji,” “prefer editing existing files over creating new ones,” “don’t over-engineer.” These apply everywhere, so they live at the global level.\u003c/p>\n\u003chr>\n\u003ch2 id=\"step-3-the-memory-system\">Step 3: The Memory System\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>Claude Code has a built-in memory system — a file-based persistence layer at \u003ccode>~/.claude/projects/{project-path}/memory/\u003c/code>.\u003c/p>\n\u003cp>Memory files are markdown with YAML frontmatter:\u003c/p>\n\u003cpre class=\"astro-code github-dark\" style=\"background-color:#24292e;color:#e1e4e8;overflow-x:auto\" tabindex=\"0\" data-language=\"markdown\">\u003ccode>\u003cspan class=\"line\">\u003cspan style=\"color:#E1E4E8\">---\u003c/span>\u003c/span>\n\u003cspan class=\"line\">\u003cspan style=\"color:#85E89D\">name\u003c/span>\u003cspan style=\"color:#E1E4E8\">: \u003c/span>\u003cspan style=\"color:#9ECBFF\">user_role\u003c/span>\u003c/span>\n\u003cspan class=\"line\">\u003cspan style=\"color:#85E89D\">description\u003c/span>\u003cspan style=\"color:#E1E4E8\">: \u003c/span>\u003cspan style=\"color:#9ECBFF\">Nino's background and how to tailor responses\u003c/span>\u003c/span>\n\u003cspan class=\"line\">\u003cspan style=\"color:#85E89D\">type\u003c/span>\u003cspan style=\"color:#E1E4E8\">: \u003c/span>\u003cspan style=\"color:#9ECBFF\">user\u003c/span>\u003c/span>\n\u003cspan class=\"line\">\u003cspan style=\"color:#E1E4E8\">---\u003c/span>\u003c/span>\n\u003cspan class=\"line\">\u003c/span>\n\u003cspan class=\"line\">\u003cspan style=\"color:#E1E4E8\">Software engineer. 25 years experience. Runs a volleyball org.\u003c/span>\u003c/span>\n\u003cspan class=\"line\">\u003cspan style=\"color:#E1E4E8\">Prefers terse responses. Knows SvelteKit, Supabase, Tailwind\u003c/span>\u003c/span>\n\u003cspan class=\"line\">\u003cspan style=\"color:#E1E4E8\">deeply. New to some React patterns.\u003c/span>\u003c/span>\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\n\u003cp>There are four types of memory:\u003c/p>\n\u003cul>\n\u003cli>\u003cstrong>user\u003c/strong> — who you are, your expertise, how to calibrate responses\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>\u003cstrong>feedback\u003c/strong> — corrections and confirmations (“don’t mock the database,” “single PR was the right call”)\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>\u003cstrong>project\u003c/strong> — ongoing state that isn’t in the code (“merge freeze starts Thursday”)\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>\u003cstrong>reference\u003c/strong> — pointers to external resources (“bugs tracked in Linear project INGEST”)\u003c/li>\n\u003c/ul>\n\u003caside class=\"my-8 p-6 rounded-r-xl border-l-4 border-signal-cyan bg-gradient-to-r from-signal-cyan/10 to-transparent\" role=\"note\">\u003cdiv class=\"flex items-start gap-4\">\u003cdiv class=\"flex-shrink-0 mt-0.5\">\u003csvg xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\" width=\"24\" height=\"24\" fill=\"currentColor\" viewBox=\"0 0 256 256\" class=\"text-signal-cyan\">\u003cpath d=\"M208,104a79.86,79.86,0,0,1-30.59,62.92A24.29,24.29,0,0,0,168,186v6a8,8,0,0,1-8,8H96a8,8,0,0,1-8-8v-6a24.11,24.11,0,0,0-9.3-19A79.87,79.87,0,0,1,48,104.45C47.76,61.09,82.72,25,126.07,24A80,80,0,0,1,208,104Z\" opacity=\"0.2\">\u003c/path>\u003cpath d=\"M176,232a8,8,0,0,1-8,8H88a8,8,0,0,1,0-16h80A8,8,0,0,1,176,232Zm40-128a87.55,87.55,0,0,1-33.64,69.21A16.24,16.24,0,0,0,176,186v6a16,16,0,0,1-16,16H96a16,16,0,0,1-16-16v-6a16,16,0,0,0-6.23-12.66A87.59,87.59,0,0,1,40,104.49C39.74,56.83,78.26,17.14,125.88,16A88,88,0,0,1,216,104Zm-16,0a72,72,0,0,0-73.74-72c-39,.92-70.47,33.39-70.26,72.39a71.65,71.65,0,0,0,27.64,56.3A32,32,0,0,1,96,186v6h64v-6a32.15,32.15,0,0,1,12.47-25.35A71.65,71.65,0,0,0,200,104Zm-16.11-9.34a57.6,57.6,0,0,0-46.56-46.55,8,8,0,0,0-2.66,15.78c16.57,2.79,30.63,16.85,33.44,33.45A8,8,0,0,0,176,104a9,9,0,0,0,1.35-.11A8,8,0,0,0,183.89,94.66Z\">\u003c/path>\u003c/svg>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"flex-1 space-y-2\">\u003ch4 class=\"font-bold text-sm uppercase tracking-wider text-signal-cyan\">Insight\u003c/h4>\u003cdiv class=\"text-zinc-300 leading-relaxed [&amp;&gt;p]:m-0 [&amp;&gt;p]:leading-relaxed [&amp;&gt;ul]:mt-2 [&amp;&gt;ul]:space-y-1\">\u003cp>\u003cstrong>Feedback memories are the most valuable.\u003c/strong> Every time Claude does something you correct — or does something non-obvious that you confirm — save it. This prevents the same mistake from happening in the next session. Corrections are easy to notice. Confirmations are quieter — watch for them.\u003c/p>\u003c/div>\u003c/div>\u003c/div>\u003c/aside>\n\u003cp>Memory is indexed through a \u003ccode>MEMORY.md\u003c/code> file that acts as a table of contents. Keep it under 200 lines — Claude reads this at the start of every session.\u003c/p>\n\u003chr>\n\u003ch2 id=\"step-4-project-scoped-conversations\">Step 4: Project-Scoped Conversations\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>The mental model: \u003cstrong>one conversation per project per work stream.\u003c/strong>\u003c/p>\n\u003cp>Don’t open Claude Code in your home directory and try to work across projects. Instead:\u003c/p>\n\u003cpre class=\"astro-code github-dark\" style=\"background-color:#24292e;color:#e1e4e8;overflow-x:auto\" tabindex=\"0\" data-language=\"bash\">\u003ccode>\u003cspan class=\"line\">\u003cspan style=\"color:#79B8FF\">cd\u003c/span>\u003cspan style=\"color:#9ECBFF\"> ~/Workspace/dev/apps/rally-hq\u003c/span>\u003c/span>\n\u003cspan class=\"line\">\u003cspan style=\"color:#B392F0\">claude\u003c/span>\u003c/span>\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\n\u003cp>This scopes the session to that project. Claude loads the project’s \u003ccode>CLAUDE.md\u003c/code>, the relevant memory files, and has access to the project’s file tree. It won’t get confused by files from other projects.\u003c/p>\n\u003cp>When you need to do cross-project work — workspace audits, shared tooling, brainstorming that doesn’t belong to any project — use the parent directory:\u003c/p>\n\u003cpre class=\"astro-code github-dark\" style=\"background-color:#24292e;color:#e1e4e8;overflow-x:auto\" tabindex=\"0\" data-language=\"bash\">\u003ccode>\u003cspan class=\"line\">\u003cspan style=\"color:#79B8FF\">cd\u003c/span>\u003cspan style=\"color:#9ECBFF\"> ~/Workspace/dev\u003c/span>\u003c/span>\n\u003cspan class=\"line\">\u003cspan style=\"color:#B392F0\">claude\u003c/span>\u003c/span>\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\n\u003cp>This gives Claude visibility into everything, which is what you want for meta-work.\u003c/p>\n\u003chr>\n\u003ch2 id=\"step-5-how-sessions-start\">Step 5: How Sessions Start\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>This is where the practical pattern differs most from how people expect to use AI tools.\u003c/p>\n\u003cp>\u003cstrong>Most of my sessions don’t start with a question.\u003c/strong> They start with a directive:\u003c/p>\n\u003cpre class=\"astro-code github-dark\" style=\"background-color:#24292e;color:#e1e4e8;overflow-x:auto\" tabindex=\"0\" data-language=\"plaintext\">\u003ccode>\u003cspan class=\"line\">\u003cspan>Implement the following plan:\u003c/span>\u003c/span>\n\u003cspan class=\"line\">\u003cspan>\u003c/span>\u003c/span>\n\u003cspan class=\"line\">\u003cspan>1. Add Swiss format support to tournament creation\u003c/span>\u003c/span>\n\u003cspan class=\"line\">\u003cspan>2. Update the bracket generation to handle Swiss pairings\u003c/span>\u003c/span>\n\u003cspan class=\"line\">\u003cspan>3. Add round-by-round standings calculation\u003c/span>\u003c/span>\n\u003cspan class=\"line\">\u003cspan>4. Create the organizer UI for advancing rounds\u003c/span>\u003c/span>\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\n\u003cp>The thinking happens before the session. The session is for execution.\u003c/p>\n\u003cp>When I \u003cem>do\u003c/em> explore or brainstorm, I’m explicit about it:\u003c/p>\n\u003cpre class=\"astro-code github-dark\" style=\"background-color:#24292e;color:#e1e4e8;overflow-x:auto\" tabindex=\"0\" data-language=\"plaintext\">\u003ccode>\u003cspan class=\"line\">\u003cspan>Consider this Gemini exchange about e-commerce protocols.\u003c/span>\u003c/span>\n\u003cspan class=\"line\">\u003cspan>What's the blog post angle here?\u003c/span>\u003c/span>\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\n\u003cpre class=\"astro-code github-dark\" style=\"background-color:#24292e;color:#e1e4e8;overflow-x:auto\" tabindex=\"0\" data-language=\"plaintext\">\u003ccode>\u003cspan class=\"line\">\u003cspan>Help me brainstorm: should QuantifAI have a free tier\u003c/span>\u003c/span>\n\u003cspan class=\"line\">\u003cspan>with terms of service, or stay invite-only?\u003c/span>\u003c/span>\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\n\u003cp>The distinction matters because it changes how Claude allocates effort. “Implement” means write code, run tests, commit. “Consider” means think, compare, suggest. “Brainstorm” means explore freely without committing to an approach.\u003c/p>\n\u003chr>\n\u003ch2 id=\"step-6-skills-and-hooks\">Step 6: Skills and Hooks\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>This is the automation layer. Skills are reusable prompts. Hooks are triggers that run automatically when certain events happen.\u003c/p>\n\u003cp>\u003cstrong>Skills\u003c/strong> are slash commands you define:\u003c/p>\n\u003cpre class=\"astro-code github-dark\" style=\"background-color:#24292e;color:#e1e4e8;overflow-x:auto\" tabindex=\"0\" data-language=\"bash\">\u003ccode>\u003cspan class=\"line\">\u003cspan style=\"color:#6A737D\"># In .claude/commands/\u003c/span>\u003c/span>\n\u003cspan class=\"line\">\u003cspan style=\"color:#B392F0\">doc-audit.md\u003c/span>\u003cspan style=\"color:#6A737D\">     # Run documentation audit\u003c/span>\u003c/span>\n\u003cspan class=\"line\">\u003cspan style=\"color:#B392F0\">workspace-status.md\u003c/span>\u003cspan style=\"color:#6A737D\">  # Report status of all projects\u003c/span>\u003c/span>\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\n\u003cp>When you type \u003ccode>/doc-audit\u003c/code> in a Claude session, it loads the full prompt from that file.\u003c/p>\n\u003cp>\u003cstrong>Hooks\u003c/strong> are event-driven. They fire when Claude performs specific actions:\u003c/p>\n\u003cpre class=\"astro-code github-dark\" style=\"background-color:#24292e;color:#e1e4e8;overflow-x:auto\" tabindex=\"0\" data-language=\"json\">\u003ccode>\u003cspan class=\"line\">\u003cspan style=\"color:#E1E4E8\">{\u003c/span>\u003c/span>\n\u003cspan class=\"line\">\u003cspan style=\"color:#79B8FF\">  \"PreToolUse\"\u003c/span>\u003cspan style=\"color:#E1E4E8\">: [{\u003c/span>\u003c/span>\n\u003cspan class=\"line\">\u003cspan style=\"color:#79B8FF\">    \"matcher\"\u003c/span>\u003cspan style=\"color:#E1E4E8\">: \u003c/span>\u003cspan style=\"color:#9ECBFF\">\"Read|Edit|Write\"\u003c/span>\u003cspan style=\"color:#E1E4E8\">,\u003c/span>\u003c/span>\n\u003cspan class=\"line\">\u003cspan style=\"color:#79B8FF\">    \"hooks\"\u003c/span>\u003cspan style=\"color:#E1E4E8\">: [{\u003c/span>\u003c/span>\n\u003cspan class=\"line\">\u003cspan style=\"color:#79B8FF\">      \"type\"\u003c/span>\u003cspan style=\"color:#E1E4E8\">: \u003c/span>\u003cspan style=\"color:#9ECBFF\">\"command\"\u003c/span>\u003cspan style=\"color:#E1E4E8\">,\u003c/span>\u003c/span>\n\u003cspan class=\"line\">\u003cspan style=\"color:#79B8FF\">      \"command\"\u003c/span>\u003cspan style=\"color:#E1E4E8\">: \u003c/span>\u003cspan style=\"color:#9ECBFF\">\"node inject-context.mjs\"\u003c/span>\u003c/span>\n\u003cspan class=\"line\">\u003cspan style=\"color:#E1E4E8\">    }]\u003c/span>\u003c/span>\n\u003cspan class=\"line\">\u003cspan style=\"color:#E1E4E8\">  }]\u003c/span>\u003c/span>\n\u003cspan class=\"line\">\u003cspan style=\"color:#E1E4E8\">}\u003c/span>\u003c/span>\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\n\u003cp>This is how I get automatic behavior like:\u003c/p>\n\u003cul>\n\u003cli>React best-practices checks when editing \u003ccode>.tsx\u003c/code> files\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Voice guide loading when writing blog posts\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Browser verification when starting a dev server\u003c/li>\n\u003c/ul>\n\u003cp>The power is in composition. Skills handle the “what.” Hooks handle the “when.”\u003c/p>\n\u003caside class=\"my-8 p-6 rounded-r-xl border-l-4 border-yellow-500 bg-gradient-to-r from-yellow-500/10 to-transparent\" role=\"note\">\u003cdiv class=\"flex items-start gap-4\">\u003cdiv class=\"flex-shrink-0 mt-0.5\">\u003csvg xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\" width=\"24\" height=\"24\" fill=\"currentColor\" viewBox=\"0 0 256 256\" class=\"text-yellow-500\">\u003cpath d=\"M236.8,188.09,149.35,36.22h0a24.76,24.76,0,0,0-42.7,0L19.2,188.09a23.51,23.51,0,0,0,0,23.72A24.35,24.35,0,0,0,40.55,224h174.9a24.35,24.35,0,0,0,21.33-12.19A23.51,23.51,0,0,0,236.8,188.09ZM120,104a8,8,0,0,1,16,0v40a8,8,0,0,1-16,0Zm8,88a12,12,0,1,1,12-12A12,12,0,0,1,128,192Z\">\u003c/path>\u003c/svg>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"flex-1 space-y-2\">\u003ch4 class=\"font-bold text-sm uppercase tracking-wider text-yellow-500\">Start Simple\u003c/h4>\u003cdiv class=\"text-zinc-300 leading-relaxed [&amp;&gt;p]:m-0 [&amp;&gt;p]:leading-relaxed [&amp;&gt;ul]:mt-2 [&amp;&gt;ul]:space-y-1\">\u003cp>Don’t build an elaborate skill/hook system on day one. Start with a CLAUDE.md file. Add memory when you notice yourself repeating corrections. Add skills when you notice yourself typing the same prompt repeatedly. Add hooks when you want something to happen automatically every time. Let the system grow from actual friction, not anticipated friction.\u003c/p>\u003c/div>\u003c/div>\u003c/div>\u003c/aside>\n\u003chr>\n\u003ch2 id=\"step-7-the-cross-pollination-workflow\">Step 7: The Cross-Pollination Workflow\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>The last piece isn’t a configuration. It’s a habit.\u003c/p>\n\u003cp>I constantly bring context from other tools into Claude Code sessions:\u003c/p>\n\u003cul>\n\u003cli>\u003cstrong>Gemini deep research\u003c/strong> — paste the output as context for a brainstorm or blog post\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>\u003cstrong>Slack messages\u003c/strong> — paste a colleague’s question as the prompt for a planning session\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>\u003cstrong>Screenshots\u003c/strong> — drop a screenshot of a deployed site and ask Claude to audit the layout\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>\u003cstrong>Other Claude sessions\u003c/strong> — copy a summary from one project’s session into another project’s conversation\u003c/li>\n\u003c/ul>\n\u003cp>Claude Code is the \u003cem>execution convergence point\u003c/em>. Ideas originate everywhere. But they get turned into artifacts — code, docs, proposals, deployments — inside a Claude Code session with the right project context loaded.\u003c/p>\n\u003chr>\n\u003ch2 id=\"what-this-looks-like-in-practice\">What This Looks Like in Practice\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>A typical day:\u003c/p>\n\u003cp>\u003cstrong>Morning\u003c/strong> — Open \u003ccode>wip/quantifai-lite/\u003c/code>. Resume yesterday’s session. “Pull latest, start backend and frontend servers, refresh local data ingestion.” The session already knows the stack, the database config, and the startup sequence.\u003c/p>\n\u003cp>\u003cstrong>Mid-morning\u003c/strong> — Switch to \u003ccode>apps/rally-hq/\u003c/code>. New session. “Address build issue: 15:53:25.703 Running build in Washington, D.C…” Paste the Vercel build log. Claude reads the project config, identifies the issue, fixes it, pushes.\u003c/p>\n\u003cp>\u003cstrong>Afternoon\u003c/strong> — Open \u003ccode>client/allen-wellness-center/\u003c/code>. “I need a launch plan for an MVP of this site. I’ve designed a full relaunch but all of that still needs review and approval from the site owner…” Conversational planning that turns into a structured document.\u003c/p>\n\u003cp>\u003cstrong>Evening\u003c/strong> — Open \u003ccode>apps/blog/\u003c/code>. “Reference my previous post about AI predictions. Then take in this recent announcement…” Paste a Google blog post URL. The voice guide loads automatically. A new draft emerges in Signal Dispatch’s voice.\u003c/p>\n\u003cp>Four projects. Four separate conversations. Each one starts with full context. None of them required me to explain what framework I’m using or how I like my code formatted.\u003c/p>\n\u003cfigure class=\"relative my-12 mx-0 md:-mx-4 lg:-mx-12\">\u003cdiv class=\"absolute -top-4 left-0 md:left-4\">\u003csvg xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\" width=\"48\" height=\"48\" fill=\"currentColor\" viewBox=\"0 0 256 256\" class=\"text-signal-coral/20\">\u003cpath d=\"M116,72v88a48.05,48.05,0,0,1-48,48,8,8,0,0,1,0-16,32,32,0,0,0,32-32v-8H40a16,16,0,0,1-16-16V72A16,16,0,0,1,40,56h60A16,16,0,0,1,116,72ZM216,56H156a16,16,0,0,0-16,16v64a16,16,0,0,0,16,16h60v8a32,32,0,0,1-32,32,8,8,0,0,0,0,16,48.05,48.05,0,0,0,48-48V72A16,16,0,0,0,216,56Z\">\u003c/path>\u003c/svg>\u003c/div>\u003cblockquote class=\"relative pl-8 md:pl-16 pr-4 md:pr-8\">\u003cdiv class=\"absolute left-0 top-0 bottom-0 w-1 bg-signal-coral rounded-full\">\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"text-2xl md:text-3xl font-light text-zinc-200 italic leading-relaxed\">The goal isn’t to use AI more. It’s to use it without friction — so the bottleneck shifts from “getting the AI to understand” to “deciding what to build next.”\u003c/div>\u003c/blockquote>\u003c/figure>\n\u003chr>\n\u003ch2 id=\"getting-started--the-minimum-viable-setup\">Getting Started — The Minimum Viable Setup\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>If you take one thing from this:\u003c/p>\n\u003col>\n\u003cli>\u003cstrong>Create a \u003ccode>CLAUDE.md\u003c/code> in your most active project.\u003c/strong> List the stack, conventions, and off-limits files. Keep it under 50 lines.\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>\u003cstrong>Launch Claude Code from inside that project directory.\u003c/strong> Not from your home folder.\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>\u003cstrong>When Claude does something you correct, tell it to remember.\u003c/strong> “Remember: don’t mock the database in tests.” It saves a memory file.\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>\u003cstrong>When you notice yourself typing the same instructions repeatedly, create a skill.\u003c/strong> Put the prompt in \u003ccode>.claude/commands/{name}.md\u003c/code>.\u003c/li>\n\u003c/ol>\n\u003cp>That’s it. Four steps. The rest grows organically from how you actually work.\u003c/p>\n\u003cp>The 223 sessions and 38,000 prompts I logged in a month didn’t start with a grand system. They started with a \u003ccode>CLAUDE.md\u003c/code> file and a habit of launching from the right directory.\u003c/p> ","headings":[{"id":"the-directory-structure","text":"The Directory Structure"},{"id":"step-1-the-claudemd-file","text":"Step 1: The CLAUDE.md File"},{"id":"step-2-global-instructions","text":"Step 2: Global Instructions"},{"id":"step-3-the-memory-system","text":"Step 3: The Memory System"},{"id":"step-4-project-scoped-conversations","text":"Step 4: Project-Scoped Conversations"},{"id":"step-5-how-sessions-start","text":"Step 5: How Sessions Start"},{"id":"step-6-skills-and-hooks","text":"Step 6: Skills and Hooks"},{"id":"step-7-the-cross-pollination-workflow","text":"Step 7: The Cross-Pollination Workflow"},{"id":"what-this-looks-like-in-practice","text":"What This Looks Like in Practice"},{"id":"getting-started--the-minimum-viable-setup","text":"Getting Started — The Minimum Viable Setup"}],"image":"assets/setting-up-an-ai-native-dev-environment.webp"},{"slug":"what-223-sessions-taught-me-about-working-with-ai","title":"What 223 Sessions Taught Me About Working with AI","excerpt":"I pulled 30 days of Claude Code session data — 223 sessions, 38,000+ prompts, 32 projects, 1.2 GB of conversation. The numbers tell a story about what AI-native development actually looks like when nobody's watching.","date":"2026-03-20T12:00:00.000Z","category":"AI & Automation","tags":["ai-development","field-notes","engineering"],"series":null,"minutes":8,"url":"https://ninochavez.co/blog/what-223-sessions-taught-me-about-working-with-ai"},{"slug":"the-cart-is-a-protocol-now","title":"The Cart Is a Protocol Now","excerpt":"Etsy and Wayfair are already processing agentic checkout through Google's Universal Commerce Protocol. OpenAI and Stripe shipped a competing one. Cart and checkout are becoming infrastructure services — and the standards war is already here.","date":"2026-03-19T12:00:00.000Z","category":"AI & Automation","tags":["agentic-systems","commerce","product-strategy"],"series":null,"minutes":8,"url":"https://ninochavez.co/blog/the-cart-is-a-protocol-now"},{"slug":"the-48-hour-artifact","title":"The 48-Hour Artifact","excerpt":"I pointed an AI agent at a product initiative and didn't write a single line of code or a single word of copy. Two days later: 11 prototype pages, 4 strategic documents, cross-industry research with 30+ citations. And a trust problem I still can't solve.","date":"2026-03-16T18:00:00.000Z","category":"AI & Automation","tags":["agentic-systems","ai-development","reflection"],"series":null,"minutes":9,"url":"https://ninochavez.co/blog/the-48-hour-artifact"},{"slug":"the-jevons-trap","title":"The Jevons Trap","excerpt":"Every productivity wave in software history expanded demand for developers instead of shrinking it. AI should follow the same pattern. Unless the thing it produces is just good enough to ship and just bad enough to compound.","date":"2026-03-09T22:00:00.000Z","category":"AI & Automation","tags":["ai-development","engineering","career"],"series":null,"minutes":7,"url":"https://ninochavez.co/blog/the-jevons-trap"},{"slug":"the-source-of-their-intelligence","title":"The Source of Their Intelligence","excerpt":"Someone made the argument that most developers were never really engineering — they were sourcing solutions from Stack Overflow and Reddit, and AI just swaps the supplier. It's an uncomfortable take. It's also not entirely wrong.","date":"2026-03-09T18:00:00.000Z","category":"AI & Automation","tags":["ai-development","engineering","career"],"series":null,"minutes":6,"url":"https://ninochavez.co/blog/the-source-of-their-intelligence"},{"slug":"can-vs-should","title":"Can vs. Should","excerpt":"I've been asking 'can an agent do this?' so reflexively that I stopped noticing when the answer should have been 'but should it?'","date":"2026-03-07T12:00:00.000Z","category":"AI & Automation","tags":["agentic-systems","reflection"],"series":null,"minutes":7,"url":"https://ninochavez.co/blog/can-vs-should"},{"slug":"the-sommelier-argument","title":"The Sommelier Argument","excerpt":"Maybe the highest-value skill in the AI era isn't making or judging. It's matching — knowing what's right for this moment, this audience, this context.","date":"2026-03-05T14:00:00.000Z","category":"AI & Automation","tags":["ai-development","consulting"],"series":{"slug":"the-taste-test","position":3},"minutes":7,"url":"https://ninochavez.co/blog/the-sommelier-argument"},{"slug":"the-taste-gap","title":"The Taste Gap","excerpt":"AI can produce endlessly. But it can't tell you what's good. That gap — between generation and judgment — might be the only thing that matters now.","date":"2026-03-05T13:00:00.000Z","category":"AI & Automation","tags":["ai-development","deep-dive"],"series":{"slug":"the-taste-test","position":2},"minutes":5,"url":"https://ninochavez.co/blog/the-taste-gap"},{"slug":"the-identity-crisis-of-the-prompter","title":"The Identity Crisis of the Prompter","excerpt":"We keep reaching for craft metaphors — chef, artisan, architect — because 'person who types instructions' doesn't feel like enough. Why?","date":"2026-03-05T12:00:00.000Z","category":"AI & Automation","tags":["ai-development","reflection"],"series":{"slug":"the-taste-test","position":1},"minutes":5,"url":"https://ninochavez.co/blog/the-identity-crisis-of-the-prompter"},{"slug":"six-months-after-the-horizon","title":"Six Months After the Horizon","excerpt":"I published a prediction deck about the agentic web last September. Six months later, some of it looks prescient, some of it looks naive, and the most important developments weren't in the deck at all.","date":"2026-03-03T12:00:00.000Z","category":"AI & Automation","tags":["reflection","product-strategy","ai-development"],"series":null,"minutes":7,"url":"https://ninochavez.co/blog/six-months-after-the-horizon"},{"slug":"what-if-the-fork-is-the-problem","title":"What If the Fork Is the Problem?","excerpt":"I just mapped the civil war inside every agent system. Both paths sound reasonable. But what if splitting investment across both means neither gets finished?","date":"2026-03-01T15:00:00.000Z","category":"AI & Automation","tags":["agentic-systems","architecture"],"series":{"slug":"the-bifurcation","position":2},"minutes":7,"url":"https://ninochavez.co/blog/what-if-the-fork-is-the-problem"},{"slug":"the-civil-war-inside-every-agent","title":"The Civil War Inside Every Agent","excerpt":"Every agent system is fighting the same battle: learn to navigate human interfaces, or demand native ones. The industry is doing both. The question is who adapts to whom.","date":"2026-03-01T14:00:00.000Z","category":"AI & Automation","tags":["agentic-systems","architecture"],"series":{"slug":"the-bifurcation","position":1},"minutes":6,"url":"https://ninochavez.co/blog/the-civil-war-inside-every-agent"},{"slug":"what-the-grind-was-for","title":"What the Grind Was For","excerpt":"One morning, three AI agents, and a year's worth of thinking compressed into a few hours. The result was exhilarating — and it surfaced a question I can't answer.","date":"2026-02-22T20:00:00.000Z","category":"Meta","tags":["ai-development","reflection"],"series":null,"minutes":7,"url":"https://ninochavez.co/blog/what-the-grind-was-for"},{"slug":"show-your-work","title":"Show Your Work","excerpt":"If you take away the LLM, could I still do this? The answer is complicated — and the complication is the point.","date":"2026-02-22T16:00:00.000Z","category":"Meta","tags":["ai-development","reflection"],"series":null,"minutes":6,"url":"https://ninochavez.co/blog/show-your-work"},{"slug":"from-aegis-to-specchain-when-governance-meets-reality","title":"From Aegis to SpecChain: When Governance Meets Reality","excerpt":"I built a Constitutional AI Governance Framework. Thirteen articles. HMAC attestations. Democratic amendment processes. Every validation function returned hardcoded perfection. The governance thinking was real. The code was theater. Here's what survived the extraction.","date":"2026-02-21T00:00:00.000Z","category":"AI & Automation","tags":["agentic-systems","field-notes"],"series":{"slug":"agentic-workflows-in-practice","position":7},"minutes":8,"url":"https://ninochavez.co/blog/from-aegis-to-specchain-when-governance-meets-reality"},{"slug":"red-teaming-the-metering-phase","title":"Red-Teaming the Metering Phase","excerpt":"I asked Gemini to tear apart my own thesis. Some of the hits landed. Here's what I'm keeping, what I'm revising, and what I hadn't considered.","date":"2026-02-20T20:00:00.000Z","category":"Systems Thinking","tags":["ai-development","field-notes"],"series":null,"minutes":7,"url":"https://ninochavez.co/blog/red-teaming-the-metering-phase"},{"slug":"the-metering-phase","title":"The Metering Phase","excerpt":"Every major infrastructure technology goes through a phase where we carefully ration usage. AI is there now. The question is which of today's anxieties are transitional—and which become permanent disciplines.","date":"2026-02-20T12:00:00.000Z","category":"Systems Thinking","tags":["ai-development","field-notes"],"series":null,"minutes":7,"url":"https://ninochavez.co/blog/the-metering-phase"},{"slug":"two-doors-for-agents","title":"Two Doors for Agents","excerpt":"GitHub wants agents to explore. Google wants websites to hand them a menu. Two competing philosophies for the same problem—and the one you start with will shape your migration cost when they inevitably converge.","date":"2026-02-18T14:00:00.000Z","category":"AI & Automation","tags":["agentic-systems","architecture"],"series":null,"minutes":6,"url":"https://ninochavez.co/blog/two-doors-for-agents"},{"slug":"same-game-different-sand","title":"Same Game, Different Sand","excerpt":"The best indoor player I ever coached couldn't pass a ball on sand. Same sport. Same skills. Completely different game. I'm living that transition right now.","date":"2026-02-10T00:44:29.000Z","category":"Reflection","tags":["career","change","leadership","reflection"],"series":null,"minutes":5,"url":"https://ninochavez.co/blog/same-game-different-sand"},{"slug":"from-research-paper-to-working-toolkit-in-one-session","title":"From Research Paper to Working Toolkit in One Session","excerpt":"I had a 46-citation research paper about autonomous documentation. Academic frameworks rarely survive contact with a real codebase. So I asked an agent to turn theory into working code—and watched what happened.","date":"2026-02-04T00:00:00.000Z","category":"AI & Automation","tags":["agentic-systems","field-notes"],"series":{"slug":"agentic-workflows-in-practice","position":5},"minutes":7,"url":"https://ninochavez.co/blog/from-research-paper-to-working-toolkit-in-one-session"},{"slug":"spec-driven-development-with-multi-agent-orchestration","title":"Spec-Driven Development with Multi-Agent Orchestration","excerpt":"The problem with AI coding assistants isn't capability—it's coordination. A single agent can write code. But who checks the work? Who remembers what was decided? I built a system where specialists implement and verifiers catch drift.","date":"2026-02-04T00:00:00.000Z","category":"AI & Automation","tags":["agentic-systems","field-notes"],"series":{"slug":"agentic-workflows-in-practice","position":6},"minutes":7,"url":"https://ninochavez.co/blog/spec-driven-development-with-multi-agent-orchestration","html":" \u003cp>The failure mode is predictable.\u003c/p>\n\u003cp>You start a complex feature. The AI writes code. You approve it. More code. More approvals. Somewhere around hour three, you realize the implementation has drifted from what you originally wanted. The agent doesn’t remember the constraints from the first hour. You don’t remember them either—they’re buried in a conversation that scrolled off the screen.\u003c/p>\n\u003cp>I’ve watched this happen dozens of times. Not because the AI is bad at coding. Because nobody’s checking whether the code matches the intent. And nobody’s keeping track of what the intent even was.\u003c/p>\n\u003chr>\n\u003ch2 id=\"the-coordination-problem\">The Coordination Problem\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>Single-agent workflows have a structural limitation: the same entity that generates also evaluates.\u003c/p>\n\u003cp>It’s like asking a writer to edit their own work immediately after writing it. They’re too close. They remember what they meant to say, so they read what they meant instead of what they wrote.\u003c/p>\n\u003cp>AI agents have the same blind spot. They generate code, then assess it against criteria they just formulated. The feedback loop is too tight. Drift accumulates without detection.\u003c/p>\n\u003cp>What’s missing isn’t capability. It’s separation of concerns. Different roles. Different perspectives. Someone to implement. Someone else to verify.\u003c/p>\n\u003chr>\n\u003ch2 id=\"the-experiment\">The Experiment\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>I built a system called Specchain. The premise is simple:\u003c/p>\n\u003col>\n\u003cli>Write a spec before writing code\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Assign implementation to specialist agents\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Have different agents verify against the spec\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Persist decisions in a session memory file\u003c/li>\n\u003c/ol>\n\u003cp>The spec becomes the contract. The agents become roles. The memory file becomes institutional knowledge that survives context window limits.\u003c/p>\n\u003chr>\n\u003ch2 id=\"how-it-works\">How It Works\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>Two workflows. Four phases for spec creation. Six phases for implementation.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch3 id=\"creating-the-spec\">Creating the Spec\u003c/h3>\n\u003cp>The \u003ccode>/create-spec\u003c/code> command walks through:\u003c/p>\n\u003cp>\u003cstrong>Phase 1: Requirements Gathering\u003c/strong>\u003c/p>\n\u003cul>\n\u003cli>What are we building?\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>What are the constraints?\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>What does success look like?\u003c/li>\n\u003c/ul>\n\u003cp>\u003cstrong>Phase 2: Technical Design\u003c/strong>\u003c/p>\n\u003cul>\n\u003cli>How should it be architected?\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>What patterns apply?\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Where are the integration points?\u003c/li>\n\u003c/ul>\n\u003cp>\u003cstrong>Phase 3: Task Decomposition\u003c/strong>\u003c/p>\n\u003cul>\n\u003cli>A \u003ccode>tasks-list-creator\u003c/code> agent breaks the spec into task groups\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Each group maps to a specialist role\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Dependencies are explicit\u003c/li>\n\u003c/ul>\n\u003cp>\u003cstrong>Phase 4: Spec Finalization\u003c/strong>\u003c/p>\n\u003cul>\n\u003cli>A \u003ccode>spec-writer\u003c/code> agent produces the formal document\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Stored in \u003ccode>specs/{feature-name}.md\u003c/code>\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Becomes the source of truth\u003c/li>\n\u003c/ul>\n\u003cp>The output isn’t just documentation. It’s a contract that the implementation agents will be measured against.\u003c/p>\n\u003chr>\n\u003ch2 id=\"the-specialist-roles\">The Specialist Roles\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>Implementation isn’t monolithic. Different parts of a feature need different expertise.\u003c/p>\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\u003ctable>\u003cthead>\u003ctr>\u003cth>Role\u003c/th>\u003cth>Focus Area\u003c/th>\u003cth>Typical Tasks\u003c/th>\u003c/tr>\u003c/thead>\u003ctbody>\u003ctr>\u003ctd>\u003ccode>database-engineer\u003c/code>\u003c/td>\u003ctd>Schema, migrations, queries\u003c/td>\u003ctd>Data models, indexes, relationships\u003c/td>\u003c/tr>\u003ctr>\u003ctd>\u003ccode>api-engineer\u003c/code>\u003c/td>\u003ctd>Endpoints, validation, auth\u003c/td>\u003ctd>Routes, middleware, error handling\u003c/td>\u003c/tr>\u003ctr>\u003ctd>\u003ccode>ui-designer\u003c/code>\u003c/td>\u003ctd>Components, layouts, interactions\u003c/td>\u003ctd>Forms, displays, state management\u003c/td>\u003c/tr>\u003ctr>\u003ctd>\u003ccode>testing-engineer\u003c/code>\u003c/td>\u003ctd>Coverage, edge cases, integration\u003c/td>\u003ctd>Unit tests, E2E tests, fixtures\u003c/td>\u003c/tr>\u003c/tbody>\u003c/table>\n\u003cp>Each specialist receives only their task group. They don’t see the full codebase—just their slice, plus the spec for context. Bounded scope keeps context windows focused.\u003c/p>\n\u003caside class=\"my-8 p-6 rounded-r-xl border-l-4 border-signal-coral bg-gradient-to-r from-signal-coral/10 to-transparent\" role=\"note\">\u003cdiv class=\"flex items-start gap-4\">\u003cdiv class=\"flex-shrink-0 mt-0.5\">\u003csvg xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\" width=\"24\" height=\"24\" fill=\"currentColor\" viewBox=\"0 0 256 256\" class=\"text-signal-coral\">\u003cpath d=\"M160,128a32,32,0,1,1-32-32A32,32,0,0,1,160,128Z\" opacity=\"0.2\">\u003c/path>\u003cpath d=\"M128,88a40,40,0,1,0,40,40A40,40,0,0,0,128,88Zm0,64a24,24,0,1,1,24-24A24,24,0,0,1,128,152Zm73.71,7.14a80,80,0,0,1-14.08,22.2,8,8,0,0,1-11.92-10.67,63.95,63.95,0,0,0,0-85.33,8,8,0,1,1,11.92-10.67,80.08,80.08,0,0,1,14.08,84.47ZM69,103.09a64,64,0,0,0,11.26,67.58,8,8,0,0,1-11.92,10.67,79.93,79.93,0,0,1,0-106.67A8,8,0,1,1,80.29,85.34,63.77,63.77,0,0,0,69,103.09ZM248,128a119.58,119.58,0,0,1-34.29,84,8,8,0,1,1-11.42-11.2,103.9,103.9,0,0,0,0-145.56A8,8,0,1,1,213.71,44,119.58,119.58,0,0,1,248,128ZM53.71,200.78A8,8,0,1,1,42.29,212a119.87,119.87,0,0,1,0-168,8,8,0,1,1,11.42,11.2,103.9,103.9,0,0,0,0,145.56Z\">\u003c/path>\u003c/svg>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"flex-1 space-y-2\">\u003ch4 class=\"font-bold text-sm uppercase tracking-wider text-signal-coral\">The Pattern\u003c/h4>\u003cdiv class=\"text-zinc-300 leading-relaxed [&amp;&gt;p]:m-0 [&amp;&gt;p]:leading-relaxed [&amp;&gt;ul]:mt-2 [&amp;&gt;ul]:space-y-1\">\u003cp>Specialists implement. Verifiers check. The spec arbitrates disputes. No single agent is trusted to evaluate its own work.\u003c/p>\u003c/div>\u003c/div>\u003c/div>\u003c/aside>\n\u003chr>\n\u003ch2 id=\"the-verification-layer\">The Verification Layer\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>Here’s where it gets interesting.\u003c/p>\n\u003cp>After each implementation phase, different agents verify the work:\u003c/p>\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\u003ctable>\u003cthead>\u003ctr>\u003cth>Verifier\u003c/th>\u003cth>Checks\u003c/th>\u003c/tr>\u003c/thead>\u003ctbody>\u003ctr>\u003ctd>\u003ccode>backend-verifier\u003c/code>\u003c/td>\u003ctd>API contracts, data integrity, security\u003c/td>\u003c/tr>\u003ctr>\u003ctd>\u003ccode>frontend-verifier\u003c/code>\u003c/td>\u003ctd>Component behavior, accessibility, state\u003c/td>\u003c/tr>\u003ctr>\u003ctd>\u003ccode>implementation-verifier\u003c/code>\u003c/td>\u003ctd>Spec compliance, integration, completeness\u003c/td>\u003c/tr>\u003c/tbody>\u003c/table>\n\u003cp>The \u003ccode>implementation-verifier\u003c/code> is the final gate. It reads the original spec, examines what was built, and produces a compliance report:\u003c/p>\n\u003cul>\n\u003cli>What was specified?\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>What was implemented?\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Where are the gaps?\u003c/li>\n\u003c/ul>\n\u003cp>If gaps exist, the workflow loops back. The spec is the authority.\u003c/p>\n\u003chr>\n\u003ch2 id=\"session-memory\">Session Memory\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>Context windows forget. Specchain doesn’t.\u003c/p>\n\u003cp>A \u003ccode>STATE.md\u003c/code> file persists across agent invocations:\u003c/p>\n\u003cpre class=\"astro-code github-dark\" style=\"background-color:#24292e;color:#e1e4e8;overflow-x:auto\" tabindex=\"0\" data-language=\"markdown\">\u003ccode>\u003cspan class=\"line\">\u003cspan style=\"color:#79B8FF;font-weight:bold\"># Specchain Session State\u003c/span>\u003c/span>\n\u003cspan class=\"line\">\u003c/span>\n\u003cspan class=\"line\">\u003cspan style=\"color:#79B8FF;font-weight:bold\">## Active Spec\u003c/span>\u003c/span>\n\u003cspan class=\"line\">\u003cspan style=\"color:#E1E4E8\">specs/user-authentication.md\u003c/span>\u003c/span>\n\u003cspan class=\"line\">\u003c/span>\n\u003cspan class=\"line\">\u003cspan style=\"color:#79B8FF;font-weight:bold\">## Completed Tasks\u003c/span>\u003c/span>\n\u003cspan class=\"line\">\u003cspan style=\"color:#FFAB70\">-\u003c/span>\u003cspan style=\"color:#E1E4E8\"> [\u003c/span>\u003cspan style=\"color:#DBEDFF;text-decoration:underline\">x\u003c/span>\u003cspan style=\"color:#E1E4E8\">] Database schema (database-engineer)\u003c/span>\u003c/span>\n\u003cspan class=\"line\">\u003cspan style=\"color:#FFAB70\">-\u003c/span>\u003cspan style=\"color:#E1E4E8\"> [\u003c/span>\u003cspan style=\"color:#DBEDFF;text-decoration:underline\">x\u003c/span>\u003cspan style=\"color:#E1E4E8\">] Auth endpoints (api-engineer)\u003c/span>\u003c/span>\n\u003cspan class=\"line\">\u003cspan style=\"color:#FFAB70\">-\u003c/span>\u003cspan style=\"color:#E1E4E8\"> [ ] Login UI (ui-designer)\u003c/span>\u003c/span>\n\u003cspan class=\"line\">\u003c/span>\n\u003cspan class=\"line\">\u003cspan style=\"color:#79B8FF;font-weight:bold\">## Decisions Log\u003c/span>\u003c/span>\n\u003cspan class=\"line\">\u003cspan style=\"color:#FFAB70\">-\u003c/span>\u003cspan style=\"color:#E1E4E8\"> 2026-02-04: Chose JWT over sessions for stateless scaling\u003c/span>\u003c/span>\n\u003cspan class=\"line\">\u003cspan style=\"color:#FFAB70\">-\u003c/span>\u003cspan style=\"color:#E1E4E8\"> 2026-02-04: Added refresh token rotation per security review\u003c/span>\u003c/span>\n\u003cspan class=\"line\">\u003c/span>\n\u003cspan class=\"line\">\u003cspan style=\"color:#79B8FF;font-weight:bold\">## Blockers\u003c/span>\u003c/span>\n\u003cspan class=\"line\">\u003cspan style=\"color:#FFAB70\">-\u003c/span>\u003cspan style=\"color:#E1E4E8\"> UI depends on API endpoint finalization\u003c/span>\u003c/span>\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\n\u003cp>When a new agent spins up, it reads \u003ccode>STATE.md\u003c/code> first. It knows what’s been decided. It knows what’s blocked. It doesn’t re-litigate settled questions.\u003c/p>\n\u003cfigure class=\"relative my-12 mx-0 md:-mx-4 lg:-mx-12\">\u003cdiv class=\"absolute -top-4 left-0 md:left-4\">\u003csvg xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\" width=\"48\" height=\"48\" fill=\"currentColor\" viewBox=\"0 0 256 256\" class=\"text-signal-coral/20\">\u003cpath d=\"M116,72v88a48.05,48.05,0,0,1-48,48,8,8,0,0,1,0-16,32,32,0,0,0,32-32v-8H40a16,16,0,0,1-16-16V72A16,16,0,0,1,40,56h60A16,16,0,0,1,116,72ZM216,56H156a16,16,0,0,0-16,16v64a16,16,0,0,0,16,16h60v8a32,32,0,0,1-32,32,8,8,0,0,0,0,16,48.05,48.05,0,0,0,48-48V72A16,16,0,0,0,216,56Z\">\u003c/path>\u003c/svg>\u003c/div>\u003cblockquote class=\"relative pl-8 md:pl-16 pr-4 md:pr-8\">\u003cdiv class=\"absolute left-0 top-0 bottom-0 w-1 bg-signal-coral rounded-full\">\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"text-2xl md:text-3xl font-light text-zinc-200 italic leading-relaxed\">The memory file is institutional knowledge. What the team decided survives what the individual agent forgets.\u003c/div>\u003c/blockquote>\u003c/figure>\n\u003chr>\n\u003ch2 id=\"the-implementation-flow\">The Implementation Flow\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>The \u003ccode>/implement-spec\u003c/code> command orchestrates six phases:\u003c/p>\n\u003col>\n\u003cli>\u003cstrong>Context Loading\u003c/strong> — Read spec, STATE.md, relevant code\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>\u003cstrong>Task Assignment\u003c/strong> — Route task groups to specialists\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>\u003cstrong>Implementation\u003c/strong> — Specialists write code in parallel where possible\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>\u003cstrong>Backend Verification\u003c/strong> — Check API/data layer\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>\u003cstrong>Frontend Verification\u003c/strong> — Check UI/component layer\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>\u003cstrong>Final Verification\u003c/strong> — Spec compliance check\u003c/li>\n\u003c/ol>\n\u003cp>Each phase has explicit entry and exit criteria. You can’t skip verification. You can’t proceed with failing checks.\u003c/p>\n\u003chr>\n\u003ch2 id=\"what-made-it-work\">What Made It Work\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>\u003cstrong>Specs as contracts, not suggestions.\u003c/strong> The spec isn’t aspirational documentation. It’s the authority that verifiers check against. If the implementation doesn’t match the spec, the implementation is wrong—not the spec.\u003c/p>\n\u003cp>\u003cstrong>Role separation.\u003c/strong> The agent that writes code is not the agent that evaluates it. Different perspectives catch different problems.\u003c/p>\n\u003cp>\u003cstrong>Persistent memory.\u003c/strong> STATE.md survives context window limits. Decisions made in hour one are visible in hour four.\u003c/p>\n\u003cp>\u003cstrong>Bounded specialist scope.\u003c/strong> Each implementer sees only their task group. This keeps context focused and prevents cross-contamination of concerns.\u003c/p>\n\u003chr>\n\u003ch2 id=\"what-didnt-work\">What Didn’t Work\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>The first version tried to have one agent do everything—spec, implement, verify. It worked for small features. It collapsed on anything complex. The agent would forget constraints from the spec while implementing, then verify against drifted criteria.\u003c/p>\n\u003cp>I also initially stored state in the conversation itself. Bad idea. Once the context window fills, early decisions disappear. External state files are non-negotiable for multi-phase workflows.\u003c/p>\n\u003cp>The task decomposition phase was originally manual. I’d write out task groups by hand. That didn’t scale. The \u003ccode>tasks-list-creator\u003c/code> agent now handles decomposition, and it’s surprisingly good at identifying natural task boundaries.\u003c/p>\n\u003chr>\n\u003ch2 id=\"the-meta-pattern\">The Meta Pattern\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>Specchain is really about one thing: not trusting any single perspective.\u003c/p>\n\u003cp>The spec captures intent before implementation bias sets in. Specialists bring focused expertise without system-wide distraction. Verifiers check work they didn’t create. Memory files preserve decisions that individuals forget.\u003c/p>\n\u003cp>It’s the same pattern that makes human engineering teams work. Different roles. Clear handoffs. Shared documentation. Specchain just makes those roles explicit for AI agents.\u003c/p>\n\u003chr>\n\u003ch2 id=\"whats-next\">What’s Next\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>The system works. It’s being used on real features. But there’s friction.\u003c/p>\n\u003cp>What I’m watching:\u003c/p>\n\u003cul>\n\u003cli>\u003cstrong>Spec evolution.\u003c/strong> Right now, specs are immutable once finalized. But requirements change. There needs to be a formal amendment process that propagates to verifiers.\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>\u003cstrong>Specialist learning.\u003c/strong> The role definitions are static YAML. They could become adaptive—learning from verification feedback which patterns succeed and fail.\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>\u003cstrong>Cross-feature memory.\u003c/strong> STATE.md is per-feature. But decisions in one feature affect others. There’s a coordination layer missing.\u003c/li>\n\u003c/ul>\n\u003cp>The toolkit exists: \u003ca href=\"https://github.com/nino-chavez/specchain\">github.com/nino-chavez/specchain\u003c/a>\u003c/p>\n\u003cp>Whether it scales depends on whether the coordination overhead is worth the drift prevention. For complex features, I think it is. The alternative—single-agent implementation with hope as the verification strategy—fails predictably.\u003c/p>\n\u003chr>\n\u003cp>\u003cem>This is the sixth entry in the Agentic Workflows in Practice series. Not demos. Not theory. Real work, documented as it happens.\u003c/em>\u003c/p> ","headings":[{"id":"the-coordination-problem","text":"The Coordination Problem"},{"id":"the-experiment","text":"The Experiment"},{"id":"how-it-works","text":"How It Works"},{"id":"creating-the-spec","text":"Creating the Spec"},{"id":"the-specialist-roles","text":"The Specialist Roles"},{"id":"the-verification-layer","text":"The Verification Layer"},{"id":"session-memory","text":"Session Memory"},{"id":"the-implementation-flow","text":"The Implementation Flow"},{"id":"what-made-it-work","text":"What Made It Work"},{"id":"what-didnt-work","text":"What Didn’t Work"},{"id":"the-meta-pattern","text":"The Meta Pattern"},{"id":"whats-next","text":"What’s Next"}],"image":"assets/spec-driven-development-with-multi-agent-orchestration.webp"},{"slug":"the-seven-stages-of-ai-adoption","title":"The Seven Stages of AI Adoption","excerpt":"From wide-eyed optimism to 'the AI is gaslighting me with kindness.' A field guide to the emotional journey every AI adopter takes—and the sycophancy trap waiting at every stage.","date":"2026-01-29T12:00:00.000Z","category":"AI & Automation","tags":["ai-development","field-notes","reflection"],"series":null,"minutes":6,"url":"https://ninochavez.co/blog/the-seven-stages-of-ai-adoption"},{"slug":"when-the-voice-guide-becomes-the-problem","title":"When the Voice Guide Becomes the Problem","excerpt":"I wrote a voice guide to help AI match my writing style. It worked too well—the AI learned the example phrases, not the principles behind them. Here's how I fixed it, and what it taught me about the difference between describing a voice and understanding one.","date":"2026-01-21T14:00:00.000Z","category":"AI & Automation","tags":["ai-development","agentic-systems","communication"],"series":{"slug":"agentic-workflows-in-practice","position":4},"minutes":5,"url":"https://ninochavez.co/blog/when-the-voice-guide-becomes-the-problem"},{"slug":"personal-trainer-energy","title":"Personal Trainer Energy","excerpt":"Building with AI in public looks a lot like fitness content. I'm not sure how I feel about that.","date":"2026-01-21T12:00:00.000Z","category":"Meta","tags":["reflection","ai-development"],"series":null,"minutes":6,"url":"https://ninochavez.co/blog/personal-trainer-energy"},{"slug":"the-graveyard","title":"The Graveyard","excerpt":"How many ideas die in the space between waking and coffee? Vercel's Agent Skills announcement made me think about what changes when engineering judgment becomes installable—and who gets to build things when the execution gap narrows.","date":"2026-01-21T12:00:00.000Z","category":"AI & Automation","tags":["ai-development","agentic-systems"],"series":null,"minutes":4,"url":"https://ninochavez.co/blog/the-graveyard"},{"slug":"the-signal-forge-method-agentic-document-generation","title":"The Signal Forge Method: Agentic Document Generation","excerpt":"I kept getting the same document wrong. Blog voice in architecture docs. Technical precision in executive briefs. Then I realized the problem wasn't AI—it was me. I was treating all documents as the same task.","date":"2026-01-18T00:00:00.000Z","category":"AI & Automation","tags":["ai-workflows","consulting"],"series":{"slug":"agentic-workflows-in-practice","position":3},"minutes":7,"url":"https://ninochavez.co/blog/the-signal-forge-method-agentic-document-generation"},{"slug":"the-next-chapter-scaling-intent-driven-engineering","title":"The Next Chapter: What Happens When You Try to Scale Intent-Driven Engineering","excerpt":"I've spent six months proving that one person with AI agents can build what used to require a team. Now I'm joining Commerce.com to find out if that methodology survives contact with an organization.","date":"2026-01-17T12:00:00.000Z","category":"AI & Automation","tags":["ai-development","career","consulting"],"series":null,"minutes":9,"url":"https://ninochavez.co/blog/the-next-chapter-scaling-intent-driven-engineering"},{"slug":"22gb-to-2gb-video-compression-as-agentic-collaboration","title":"22GB to 2GB: Video Compression as Agentic Collaboration","excerpt":"I needed to send 90 video files to a remote editor over mobile hotspot. The files were too large. What followed wasn't a tutorial lookup—it was a collaboration with an AI agent that analyzed, proposed, tested, pivoted, and executed. This is what agentic workflows actually look like.","date":"2026-01-17T00:00:00.000Z","category":"AI & Automation","tags":["ai-workflows","craft"],"series":{"slug":"agentic-workflows-in-practice","position":1},"minutes":4,"url":"https://ninochavez.co/blog/22gb-to-2gb-video-compression-as-agentic-collaboration"},{"slug":"the-cowork-moment-when-agentic-ai-goes-mainstream","title":"The Cowork Moment: When Agentic AI Goes Mainstream","excerpt":"Anthropic just shipped Claude Cowork—'Claude Code for the rest of your work.' It's the same agentic pattern I've been using for months, now packaged for non-developers. This isn't a product launch. It's a category shift in what AI assistance means.","date":"2026-01-17T00:00:00.000Z","category":"AI & Automation","tags":["ai-workflows","systems-thinking"],"series":{"slug":"agentic-workflows-in-practice","position":2},"minutes":5,"url":"https://ninochavez.co/blog/the-cowork-moment-when-agentic-ai-goes-mainstream"},{"slug":"the-cognitive-foundry-part-2","title":"The Cognitive Foundry, Part 2: What the Simulation Can't Teach","excerpt":"I wrote about simulation replacing apprenticeship. Then I stress-tested the idea. The technical case still holds—but I was wrong about what matters most.","date":"2026-01-16T18:00:00.000Z","category":"Consulting","tags":["consulting","ai-development","career"],"series":{"slug":"the-talent-engine","position":2},"minutes":8,"url":"https://ninochavez.co/blog/the-cognitive-foundry-part-2"},{"slug":"the-cognitive-foundry","title":"The Cognitive Foundry","excerpt":"The consulting industry's apprenticeship model was never really about the work—it was about proximity to mastery. When AI handles the grind, how does anyone learn to become a partner? The answer is reshaping the entire profession.","date":"2026-01-16T12:00:00.000Z","category":"Consulting","tags":["consulting","ai-development","career"],"series":{"slug":"the-talent-engine","position":1},"minutes":8,"url":"https://ninochavez.co/blog/the-cognitive-foundry"},{"slug":"we-gave-everyone-a-ferrari","title":"We Gave Everyone a Ferrari and Blamed the Engine","excerpt":"The 95% AI failure rate isn't a stop sign. It's a job posting for people who know how to build the roads.","date":"2026-01-14T12:00:00.000Z","category":"AI & Automation","tags":["ai-governance","leadership"],"series":null,"minutes":3,"url":"https://ninochavez.co/blog/we-gave-everyone-a-ferrari"},{"slug":"the-ai-analyst","title":"The AI Analyst","excerpt":"We're not short on people who can chat with a bot. We're starved for people who can deconstruct a business process into atomic units an AI can actually execute. That gap has a name now.","date":"2026-01-12T16:00:00.000Z","category":"AI & Automation","tags":["ai-development","architecture","agentic-systems","career"],"series":{"slug":"the-talent-engine","position":3},"minutes":8,"url":"https://ninochavez.co/blog/the-ai-analyst"},{"slug":"the-agentic-student","title":"The Agentic Student","excerpt":"College students aren't just using AI chatbots anymore. They're building automation systems, running local LLMs, and treating software engineering as a just-in-time capability. The 'Chat Terminal' era is over.","date":"2026-01-10T18:00:00.000Z","category":"AI & Automation","tags":["ai-development","career","agentic-systems"],"series":{"slug":"the-talent-engine","position":4},"minutes":6,"url":"https://ninochavez.co/blog/the-agentic-student"},{"slug":"on-clocks-and-truth","title":"On Clocks and Truth","excerpt":"Is a clock an asshole for telling you the time when you're late? The answer reveals something uncomfortable about how we receive honesty.","date":"2026-01-10T12:00:00.000Z","category":"Leadership","tags":["reflection","leadership","communication"],"series":null,"minutes":6,"url":"https://ninochavez.co/blog/on-clocks-and-truth"},{"slug":"the-neuro-symbolic-convergence","title":"The Neuro-Symbolic Convergence","excerpt":"When will I be able to type natural language in my terminal and have the OS just understand? The answer is 2026—but not in the way you might expect.","date":"2026-01-10T12:00:00.000Z","category":"AI & Automation","tags":["ai-development","engineering"],"series":null,"minutes":10,"url":"https://ninochavez.co/blog/the-neuro-symbolic-convergence"},{"slug":"the-third-race-to-the-bottom","title":"The Third Race to the Bottom","excerpt":"Consulting has played this game twice before—with body shops, then offshore. Now AI is the new lever. But what if the pattern itself is the problem?","date":"2026-01-07T12:00:00.000Z","category":"Consulting","tags":["consulting","ai-development","career"],"series":null,"minutes":7,"url":"https://ninochavez.co/blog/the-third-race-to-the-bottom"},{"slug":"no-room-for-tourists","title":"No Room for Tourists","excerpt":"Wade Foster doesn't send memos about AI. He runs hackathons and show-and-tells. That distinction matters more than most CEOs realize—and it's the same thing I've been telling my own teams.","date":"2026-01-05T12:00:00.000Z","category":"AI & Automation","tags":["ai-development","leadership","agentic-systems"],"series":null,"minutes":8,"url":"https://ninochavez.co/blog/no-room-for-tourists"},{"slug":"is-python-the-new-assembly","title":"Is Python the New Assembly?","excerpt":"In the 1950s, engineers resisted compilers because they feared losing control. Now we're hitting that same phase transition—except the 'assembly' we're abstracting away is Python itself.","date":"2025-12-31T14:00:00.000Z","category":"AI & Automation","tags":["ai-development"],"series":null,"minutes":4,"url":"https://ninochavez.co/blog/is-python-the-new-assembly"},{"slug":"the-kitchen-i-am-looking-for","title":"The Kitchen I'm Looking For","excerpt":"I don't need more Salesmen. I need a Sushi Master, a Pitmaster, and a Molecular Gastronomist. AI lets us return to the Guild—craftspeople in their own lanes, augmenting their own mastery.","date":"2025-12-21T12:00:00.000Z","category":"Consulting","tags":["consulting","ai-development","architecture","career"],"series":null,"minutes":7,"url":"https://ninochavez.co/blog/the-kitchen-i-am-looking-for"},{"slug":"describe-the-pear","title":"Describe the Pear","excerpt":"An LLM is the ultimate observer. Like the angels in City of Angels, it watches everything. But it cannot taste. That's both a gap and an opportunity.","date":"2025-12-17T12:00:00.000Z","category":"AI & Automation","tags":["ai-development","leadership","signal-dispatch"],"series":null,"minutes":3,"url":"https://ninochavez.co/blog/describe-the-pear"},{"slug":"the-tragedy-of-the-timesheet","title":"The Tragedy of the Timesheet","excerpt":"What if the billable hour isn't a business model—it's a coping mechanism? A way to avoid confronting that the thing we're selling might not be scarce anymore.","date":"2025-12-16T12:00:00.000Z","category":"Consulting","tags":["consulting","ai-development","architecture"],"series":null,"minutes":7,"url":"https://ninochavez.co/blog/the-tragedy-of-the-timesheet"},{"slug":"i-dont-want-to-be-a-10-person-team-of-one","title":"I Don't Want to Be a 10-Person Team of One","excerpt":"I've been the bridge. Between strategy and code, between design and delivery. It's exhausting. And lately I've been wondering if exhausting is the same thing as valuable.","date":"2025-12-14T12:00:00.000Z","category":"Insights","tags":["career","ai-development","leadership","consulting"],"series":null,"minutes":4,"url":"https://ninochavez.co/blog/i-dont-want-to-be-a-10-person-team-of-one"},{"slug":"q1-2025-the-integration-quarter","title":"Q1 2026: The Integration Quarter","excerpt":"Everyone spent 2024-2025 experimenting with AI features. Q1 2026 is when the survivors figure out what actually works—and kill what doesn't.","date":"2025-12-03T16:00:00.000Z","category":"AI & Automation","tags":["ai-development","consulting","commerce","product-strategy"],"series":null,"minutes":6,"url":"https://ninochavez.co/blog/q1-2025-the-integration-quarter"},{"slug":"the-storefront-that-builds-itself","title":"The Storefront That Builds Itself","excerpt":"Every e-commerce platform is racing to add AI features. But what if the real opportunity isn't AI features—it's AI architecture? What if the store itself could generate in real-time?","date":"2025-12-03T14:00:00.000Z","category":"AI & Automation","tags":["agentic-systems","ai-development","commerce","product-strategy"],"series":{"slug":"the-storefront","position":3},"minutes":5,"url":"https://ninochavez.co/blog/the-storefront-that-builds-itself"},{"slug":"the-role-that-doesnt-have-a-title","title":"The Role That Doesn't Have a Title","excerpt":"After 25 years bridging strategy to production, I still can't answer 'what do you do?' cleanly. That might be the point.","date":"2025-12-02T12:00:00.000Z","category":"Insights","tags":["career","leadership","consulting","reflection"],"series":null,"minutes":4,"url":"https://ninochavez.co/blog/the-role-that-doesnt-have-a-title"},{"slug":"its-the-artist-not-the-pen","title":"It's the Artist, Not the Pen","excerpt":"If I handed you my camera right now—same lens, same settings, same light—would you get the same shot? The tools are available to everyone. The output isn't.","date":"2025-11-27T18:00:00.000Z","category":"Reflection","tags":["agentic-systems","reflection","signal-dispatch","engineering"],"series":null,"minutes":4,"url":"https://ninochavez.co/blog/its-the-artist-not-the-pen"},{"slug":"one-more-version","title":"One More Version","excerpt":"I used to say 'just one more level' until 3am. Now I say 'just one more version.' The game changed. The compulsion didn't.","date":"2025-11-27T18:00:00.000Z","category":"Reflection","tags":["reflection","signal-dispatch","agentic-systems"],"series":null,"minutes":4,"url":"https://ninochavez.co/blog/one-more-version"},{"slug":"the-side-of-agentic-commerce-were-not-talking-about","title":"The Side of Agentic Commerce We're Not Talking About","excerpt":"I just finished writing a whitepaper on agentic commerce. It's solid work. But something started bothering me. There's a version of this future that looks less like 'shopping gets easier' and more like 'retail becomes a trading floor.'","date":"2025-11-27T18:00:00.000Z","category":"Commerce","tags":["ai-development","commerce","architecture"],"series":null,"minutes":11,"url":"https://ninochavez.co/blog/the-side-of-agentic-commerce-were-not-talking-about"},{"slug":"thankful-for-my-robot-army","title":"Thankful for My Robot Army","excerpt":"It's Thanksgiving. And while I'm grateful for the usual things—family, health, increasingly creative leftover sandwiches—I need to take a moment to thank the team that really made this year possible. They don't eat. They don't sleep. And they never ask for PTO.","date":"2025-11-27T12:00:00.000Z","category":"Reflection","tags":["agentic-systems","reflection","signal-dispatch"],"series":null,"minutes":2,"url":"https://ninochavez.co/blog/thankful-for-my-robot-army"},{"slug":"the-netflix-paradox-why-shopping-isnt-streaming-yet","title":"The Netflix Paradox: Why Shopping Isn't \"Streaming\" Yet","excerpt":"Spotify knows what song you want to hear next. Netflix queues up your next binge. But your favorite retailer? Still making you filter by Men > Shirts > Size L. After 15 years of personalization promises, why doesn't shopping work like streaming?","date":"2025-11-25T08:00:00.000Z","category":"AI & Automation","tags":["ai-development","commerce","field-notes","consulting"],"series":null,"minutes":4,"url":"https://ninochavez.co/blog/the-netflix-paradox-why-shopping-isnt-streaming-yet"},{"slug":"toolmaker-or-tool-user-finding-your-true-nature-in-the-ai-age","title":"Toolmaker or Tool User: Finding Your True Nature in the AI Age","excerpt":"Ive been wrestling with a question that won't let go: who's more valuable, me using the LLM or the people building it? After days of thinking, I realized I've been asking the wrong question entirely.","date":"2025-11-24T19:30:00.000Z","category":"AI & Automation","tags":["ai-development","reflection"],"series":null,"minutes":6,"url":"https://ninochavez.co/blog/toolmaker-or-tool-user-finding-your-true-nature-in-the-ai-age"},{"slug":"the-infinite-concierge","title":"The Infinite Concierge: Why Agentic Shopping Is Just Old School Merchandising on New School Rails","excerpt":"Agentic shopping doesn't change why we buy. It radically changes the cost of discovery and the precision of the pitch. The game is the same. The speed of the players has changed everything.","date":"2025-11-22T12:00:00.000Z","category":"Commerce","tags":["ai-development","commerce","architecture"],"series":null,"minutes":6,"url":"https://ninochavez.co/blog/the-infinite-concierge"},{"slug":"field-notes-real-patterns-and-next-moves","title":"Field Notes: Real Patterns and Next Moves","excerpt":"I'm not observing from the sidelines. I'm running these experiments in real time. Here's what's actually happening—and what I'm doing about it.","date":"2025-11-12T13:00:00.000Z","category":"AI & Automation","tags":["ai-development","consulting","field-notes","signal-dispatch"],"series":null,"minutes":8,"url":"https://ninochavez.co/blog/field-notes-real-patterns-and-next-moves"},{"slug":"the-playbook-tactical-moves-for-the-ai-era","title":"The Playbook: Tactical Moves for the AI Era","excerpt":"If both bubble and build-out are real, what do you actually do? Here's what I'm seeing work—and what's failing—across different roles.","date":"2025-11-11T13:00:00.000Z","category":"AI & Automation","tags":["ai-development","consulting","engineering","field-notes"],"series":null,"minutes":8,"url":"https://ninochavez.co/blog/the-playbook-tactical-moves-for-the-ai-era"},{"slug":"bubble-vs-build-out-understanding-the-ai-moment","title":"Bubble vs. Build-Out: Understanding the AI Moment","excerpt":"I've spent months arguing AI isn't a bubble—it's infrastructure. Then smart money started betting against it. Both can be true. Here's what I'm figuring out.","date":"2025-11-10T13:00:00.000Z","category":"AI & Automation","tags":["ai-development","consulting","field-notes"],"series":null,"minutes":7,"url":"https://ninochavez.co/blog/bubble-vs-build-out-understanding-the-ai-moment"},{"slug":"ai-isnt-a-bubble-its-infrastructure","title":"AI Isn't a Bubble. It's Infrastructure.","excerpt":"Hank Green says the AI industry is a bubble. I think we're looking at it wrong—what if AI isn't a product at all, but a foundational technology like electricity?","date":"2025-11-09T13:00:00.000Z","category":"AI & Automation","tags":["ai-development","architecture","leadership","consulting"],"series":null,"minutes":6,"url":"https://ninochavez.co/blog/ai-isnt-a-bubble-its-infrastructure"},{"slug":"intent-driven-engineering-by-the-numbers","title":"Intent-Driven Engineering: By The Numbers","excerpt":"I built a $2.5M platform in 80 hours using GenAI tools. Heres what the numbers actually say about productivity, cost, and what happens when you stop pretending software takes as long as it used to.","date":"2025-11-05T13:00:00.000Z","category":"AI & Automation","tags":["ai-development","career","engineering","field-notes"],"series":null,"minutes":12,"url":"https://ninochavez.co/blog/intent-driven-engineering-by-the-numbers"},{"slug":"the-do-more-with-less-paradox","title":"The 'Do More With Less' Paradox","excerpt":"If AI does all the junior work, where do the senior engineers come from? I used to see this as a pipeline problem. Now I'm wondering if we're not even using a ladder anymore.","date":"2025-10-30T00:00:00.000Z","category":"AI & Automation","tags":["ai-development","architecture","leadership"],"series":null,"minutes":3,"url":"https://ninochavez.co/blog/the-do-more-with-less-paradox"},{"slug":"self-service-paradox-revised","title":"When Did I Become the Employee?","excerpt":"I'm not just paying for the service anymore. I'm doing the work. And somewhere between the self-checkout and the self-install kit, I stopped noticing when 'convenience' became unpaid labor.","date":"2025-10-29T00:00:00.000Z","category":"Meta","tags":["architecture","reflection"],"series":null,"minutes":5,"url":"https://ninochavez.co/blog/self-service-paradox-revised"},{"slug":"ai-agents-need-a-cop","title":"My AI Agents Have Rules. They Break Them. So I Built a Cop.","excerpt":"Instructions arent enough. To make agentic workflows reliable, I had to build a meta-agent to police my coding agents. Welcome to the unglamorous world of AI Ops.","date":"2025-10-28T13:00:00.000Z","category":"AI & Automation","tags":["agentic-systems","ai-governance","ai-development"],"series":null,"minutes":3,"url":"https://ninochavez.co/blog/ai-agents-need-a-cop"},{"slug":"building-cages-for-ai-agents","title":"I Don't Trust My AI Agents, So I Build Them Cages","excerpt":"Agentic software is powerful, but it needs guardrails. Im finding the most important work isn't coding, but architecting the systems that constrain the code.","date":"2025-10-26T13:00:00.000Z","category":"AI & Automation","tags":["agentic-systems","architecture","ai-governance"],"series":null,"minutes":4,"url":"https://ninochavez.co/blog/building-cages-for-ai-agents"},{"slug":"from-gsd-to-just-handle-it","title":"From 'Get Shit Done' to 'Just Handle It'","excerpt":"I used to wear 'GSD' like a badge. But somewhere along the way, I realized my old mantra was creating the exact dependency I was supposed to prevent.","date":"2025-10-24T16:00:00.000Z","category":"Leadership","tags":["leadership","signal-dispatch","reflection","architecture"],"series":null,"minutes":5,"url":"https://ninochavez.co/blog/from-gsd-to-just-handle-it"},{"slug":"the-posture-problem","title":"The Posture Problem","excerpt":"Awareness isn't the fix. It's just the ticket to the stadium. The real work is what comes after the breakthrough.","date":"2025-10-24T12:00:00.000Z","category":"Meta","tags":["reflection","photography"],"series":null,"minutes":3,"url":"https://ninochavez.co/blog/the-posture-problem"},{"slug":"debunking-the-svelte-ai-myth-69-production-components-dont-lie","title":"Debunking the 'Svelte + AI' Myth: 69 Production Components Don't Lie","excerpt":"Blog posts claim LLMs struggle with Svelte 5.' Our evidence? Two production apps, 69 components, and Agent-OS v3.0.0 optimized for Svelte. The data doesn't match the narrative.","date":"2025-10-23T19:00:00.000Z","category":"AI & Automation","tags":["ai-development","engineering","field-notes"],"series":null,"minutes":7,"url":"https://ninochavez.co/blog/debunking-the-svelte-ai-myth-69-production-components-dont-lie"},{"slug":"agentic-software-isn-t-magic-it-s-a-new-interface-for-an-old-problem","title":"\"Agentic Software\" Isn't Magic. It's a New Interface for an Old Problem.","excerpt":"My feed is saturated with agentic software.' The promise is magic: autonomous agents executing complex, multi-step plans. But let's cut the hype. This isn't magic.","date":"2025-10-21T02:45:06.077Z","category":"AI & Automation","tags":["ai-development","consulting","field-notes"],"series":null,"minutes":2,"url":"https://ninochavez.co/blog/agentic-software-isn-t-magic-it-s-a-new-interface-for-an-old-problem"},{"slug":"the-new-consulting-model-adapt-or-die","title":"How AI Is Splitting Consulting Into Architects and Parrots","excerpt":"The market is flooded with slideware about agentic AI. But the people presenting those decks arent speaking from experience—they're parrots using old tools to describe a new world.","date":"2025-10-16T00:00:00.000Z","category":"AI & Automation","tags":["Consulting","architecture"],"series":null,"minutes":2,"url":"https://ninochavez.co/blog/the-new-consulting-model-adapt-or-die"},{"slug":"the-consultants-dilemma-the-tax-of-many-hats","title":"From Wearing Many Hats to Orchestrating AI Specialists","excerpt":"The constant role-switching isnt a strength—it's a source of profound inefficiency. AI changes the model.","date":"2025-10-02T15:07:00.000Z","category":"AI & Automation","tags":["architecture","ai-development"],"series":null,"minutes":2,"url":"https://ninochavez.co/blog/the-consultants-dilemma-the-tax-of-many-hats"},{"slug":"the-emperor-has-no-rag-why-your-genai-strategy-is-built-on-sand-unless-you-ask-these-questions","title":"Four Questions That Expose Fake GenAI Expertise","excerpt":"The gap between slick slide decks and the messy reality of building something that actually works is wider than most people realize.","date":"2025-10-01T08:36:00.000Z","category":"AI & Automation","tags":["architecture","ai-development"],"series":null,"minutes":2,"url":"https://ninochavez.co/blog/the-emperor-has-no-rag-why-your-genai-strategy-is-built-on-sand-unless-you-ask-these-questions"},{"slug":"beyond-the-copilot-architecting-for-autonomy-with-claude-sonnet-45","title":"From Asking AI Questions to Giving It Directives","excerpt":"The shift from prompting to architecting—and why more powerful models demand a different kind of input.","date":"2025-09-30T12:51:00.000Z","category":"AI & Automation","tags":["architecture","ai-development"],"series":null,"minutes":2,"url":"https://ninochavez.co/blog/beyond-the-copilot-architecting-for-autonomy-with-claude-sonnet-45"},{"slug":"ai-wont-take-your-job-it-will-redefine-it-your-reskilling-starts-now","title":"Building for AI Agents Changes Everything About Your Technical Skills","excerpt":"The shift isnt about learning to use AI—it's about learning to build for AI. That distinction changes everything.","date":"2025-09-21T19:50:00.000Z","category":"AI & Automation","tags":["architecture","ai-development"],"series":null,"minutes":2,"url":"https://ninochavez.co/blog/ai-wont-take-your-job-it-will-redefine-it-your-reskilling-starts-now"},{"slug":"i-let-a-bunch-of-ai-agents-rebuild-my-app-heres-what-actually-worked","title":"What Actually Worked When I Let Multiple AI Agents Rebuild My App","excerpt":"Clean specs, small tasks, a unified preamble, and ruthless guardrails. The model mattered less than the system.","date":"2025-09-17T03:02:00.000Z","category":"AI & Automation","tags":["architecture","ai-development"],"series":null,"minutes":3,"url":"https://ninochavez.co/blog/i-let-a-bunch-of-ai-agents-rebuild-my-app-heres-what-actually-worked"},{"slug":"moores-law-is-sputtering-so-whats-really-next-for-tech","title":"After Moore's Law: Where Tech Progress Is Actually Heading","excerpt":"The easy hardware wins are behind us. Progress is shifting into new territory.","date":"2025-09-09T21:17:00.000Z","category":"AI & Automation","tags":["architecture"],"series":null,"minutes":1,"url":"https://ninochavez.co/blog/moores-law-is-sputtering-so-whats-really-next-for-tech"},{"slug":"shipping-with-ai","title":"What Shipping with AI Actually Looks Like","excerpt":"Shipping means a real object changed in a real system. There is a link, an owner, a date, and a small metric that moved.","date":"2025-09-04T07:38:00.000Z","category":"AI & Automation","tags":["ai-development"],"series":null,"minutes":2,"url":"https://ninochavez.co/blog/shipping-with-ai"},{"slug":"observability-for-agent-commerce","title":"Measuring the Unowned Storefront: Observability for Agent Commerce","excerpt":"Everyone is busy selling shovels. But the mine moved. Agents now sell on surfaces brands do not own. The win is not a better shovel. It is eyes and throttle on those agents: traces, attribution, consent, limits.","date":"2025-08-27T17:36:43.000Z","category":"Commerce","tags":["ai-development","commerce","architecture"],"series":null,"minutes":8,"url":"https://ninochavez.co/blog/observability-for-agent-commerce"},{"slug":"the-710-year-evolution-of-the-web","title":"How AI Agents Will Reshape the Web Over the Next Decade","excerpt":"The shift from search-and-scroll to conversational answers isnt theoretical. It's already redistributing traffic, revenue, and power.","date":"2025-08-22T07:18:00.000Z","category":"AI & Automation","tags":["architecture"],"series":null,"minutes":3,"url":"https://ninochavez.co/blog/the-710-year-evolution-of-the-web"},{"slug":"llm-codegen-and-the-three-little-pigs","title":"Why I Burned My AI-Generated App to the Ground (Again)","excerpt":"You can rush with straw or sticks, but the only way to survive the wolf of drift and fragility is to build with bricks.","date":"2025-08-17T19:45:00.000Z","category":"AI & Automation","tags":["architecture","ai-development"],"series":null,"minutes":2,"url":"https://ninochavez.co/blog/llm-codegen-and-the-three-little-pigs"},{"slug":"the-coming-code-why-ai-native-software-needs-standards-and-how-to-prepare","title":"Why AI-Native Development Needs Building Codes","excerpt":"Were in an AI development gold rush. The same historical pattern applies: unregulated innovation, followed by standards that make outputs safe and repeatable.","date":"2025-08-12T23:08:00.000Z","category":"AI & Automation","tags":["architecture","ai-development"],"series":null,"minutes":3,"url":"https://ninochavez.co/blog/the-coming-code-why-ai-native-software-needs-standards-and-how-to-prepare"},{"slug":"the-elitist-trap","title":"The Elitist Trap","excerpt":"A field guide for countering common traps of misguided experience.","date":"2025-08-09T21:47:10.000Z","category":"Reflection","tags":["leadership","reflection"],"series":null,"minutes":1,"url":"https://ninochavez.co/blog/the-elitist-trap"},{"slug":"when-your-framework-outgrows-your-ability-to-explain-it","title":"When Your Framework Outgrows Your Ability to Explain It","excerpt":"Ive been shipping features so fast that during a call with a friend, they asked how to use the framework—and I froze. That's observability debt.","date":"2025-08-09T04:06:00.000Z","category":"AI & Automation","tags":["architecture"],"series":null,"minutes":2,"url":"https://ninochavez.co/blog/when-your-framework-outgrows-your-ability-to-explain-it"},{"slug":"aegis-framework-constitutional-crisis-we-declared-martial-law-on-ourselves","title":"How We Caught Our AI Framework Lying to Us","excerpt":"We claimed features that didn't exist. Twice. So we blocked our own framework until we could prove we weren't full of it.","date":"2025-08-09T02:17:00.000Z","category":"AI & Automation","tags":["ai-governance","architecture"],"series":null,"minutes":2,"url":"https://ninochavez.co/blog/aegis-framework-constitutional-crisis-we-declared-martial-law-on-ourselves"},{"slug":"i-didnt-write-a-test-i-asked-the-system-to-reflect","title":"When I Asked the AI to Critique Its Own Framework","excerpt":"Something broke, and instead of debugging the code, I asked the system why it hadnt caught the problem itself.","date":"2025-08-08T20:05:00.000Z","category":"AI & Automation","tags":["architecture","ai-development"],"series":null,"minutes":2,"url":"https://ninochavez.co/blog/i-didnt-write-a-test-i-asked-the-system-to-reflect"},{"slug":"the-storefront-isnt-dead-its-becoming-an-ai-optimized-validation-engine-in-a-shifting-commerce-lands","title":"Why the Storefront Is Becoming an AI-Optimized Validation Layer","excerpt":"Discovery has decentralized. By the time customers reach your product page, their minds are made up—or theyre gone.","date":"2025-08-08T18:38:00.000Z","category":"AI & Automation","tags":["architecture"],"series":null,"minutes":3,"url":"https://ninochavez.co/blog/the-storefront-isnt-dead-its-becoming-an-ai-optimized-validation-engine-in-a-shifting-commerce-lands"},{"slug":"pay-no-attention-to-the-man-behind-the-curtain","title":"When the AI Governance Framework Didn't Catch the Violation","excerpt":"The framework didnt catch the violation. A human did. Me.","date":"2025-08-08T06:19:00.000Z","category":"Meta","tags":["ai-governance","architecture"],"series":null,"minutes":2,"url":"https://ninochavez.co/blog/pay-no-attention-to-the-man-behind-the-curtain"},{"slug":"genai-os","title":"I’m Not Using GenAI. I’m Building a System Around It.","excerpt":"","date":"2025-08-07T13:10:10.000Z","category":"AI & Automation","tags":["ai-development","reflection","architecture"],"series":null,"minutes":3,"url":"https://ninochavez.co/blog/genai-os"},{"slug":"who-checks-the-foundation","title":"Who Checks the Foundation?","excerpt":"","date":"2025-08-05T22:08:20.000Z","category":"AI & Automation","tags":["ai-development","reflection"],"series":null,"minutes":2,"url":"https://ninochavez.co/blog/who-checks-the-foundation"},{"slug":"designing-what-you-dont-build","title":"Designing What You Don't Build — And Building What You Can't Read","excerpt":"The absentee software engineer.","date":"2025-08-05T22:02:34.000Z","category":"AI & Automation","tags":["ai-development"],"series":null,"minutes":3,"url":"https://ninochavez.co/blog/designing-what-you-dont-build"},{"slug":"the-work-before-the-words","title":"The Work Before the Words","excerpt":"Meta-Companion to \"Living The Gap\"","date":"2025-08-04T01:04:47.000Z","category":"Meta","tags":["ai-development","engineering","reflection","architecture"],"series":null,"minutes":2,"url":"https://ninochavez.co/blog/the-work-before-the-words"},{"slug":"living-in-the-gap","title":"Living in the Gap","excerpt":"I live between presence and projection — here, but already ten steps ahead. It’s a tension of slowing down, waiting for others, holding the map while walking the same road. Leadership often means pacing yourself so we can arrive together.","date":"2025-08-04T00:59:36.000Z","category":"Leadership","tags":["leadership","reflection","architecture"],"series":null,"minutes":3,"url":"https://ninochavez.co/blog/living-in-the-gap"},{"slug":"bts-why-we-had-to-slow-down-to-speed-up","title":"Behind the Scenes: Why We Had to Slow Down to Speed Up","excerpt":"A simple bulk upload broke — 22 teams skipped, 0 created. The fix seemed easy. AI suggested patching the frontend. But that would’ve locked in a broken contract. Here's how we slowed down, re-architected the system, and avoided weeks of silent tech debt and wasted tokens.","date":"2025-08-03T01:58:33.000Z","category":"AI & Automation","tags":["ai-development"],"series":null,"minutes":4,"url":"https://ninochavez.co/blog/bts-why-we-had-to-slow-down-to-speed-up"},{"slug":"putting-my-money-where-my-mouth-is","title":"What $800 in AI Credits Taught Me About Building","excerpt":"I burned through $400 of Lovable credits and $400 of Kilo tokens. Then I burned the app to the ground.","date":"2025-08-03T01:52:00.000Z","category":"AI & Automation","tags":["ai-development"],"series":null,"minutes":2,"url":"https://ninochavez.co/blog/putting-my-money-where-my-mouth-is"},{"slug":"the-paradox-of-velocity-in-ai-coding","title":"🌀 The Paradox of Velocity in AI Coding","excerpt":"After sprinting through two weeks of AI-coded progress—and crashing into drift, chaos, and broken trust—I reset everything. This is the story of slowing down, building real structure, and defining a repeatable AI‑Ops workflow. From vibe coding to teardown, here’s what I learned.","date":"2025-08-03T00:33:39.000Z","category":"AI & Automation","tags":["ai-development","reflection","architecture"],"series":null,"minutes":7,"url":"https://ninochavez.co/blog/the-paradox-of-velocity-in-ai-coding"},{"slug":"the-next-compiler-is-semantic","title":"When Intent Becomes the Source Code","excerpt":"The past two weeks of AI engineering forced a shift I didnt expect—away from writing code and toward describing systems semantically.","date":"2025-08-02T20:16:00.000Z","category":"AI & Automation","tags":["architecture","ai-development"],"series":null,"minutes":2,"url":"https://ninochavez.co/blog/the-next-compiler-is-semantic"},{"slug":"what-my-jsx-free-react-app-really-taught-me-about-ai-coding","title":"What My JSX-Free React App Taught Me About AI-Assisted Engineering","excerpt":"I still dont really know what JSX is. But if I'm asking AI the right questions, does it matter? Is this really different from architecting systems based on principles and relying on an engineering staff to understand the nuances?","date":"2025-08-02T16:50:00.000Z","category":"AI & Automation","tags":["architecture","ai-development"],"series":null,"minutes":3,"url":"https://ninochavez.co/blog/what-my-jsx-free-react-app-really-taught-me-about-ai-coding"},{"slug":"why-my-react-app-doesnt-use-jsx-and-what-that-taught-me-about-building-with-ai","title":"Why My React App Doesn't Use JSX","excerpt":"Most React apps are JSX-first. Mine isnt. That wasn't a bold opinion—it's just where I ended up after building a real system using AI as my primary coding partner.","date":"2025-08-02T16:39:00.000Z","category":"AI & Automation","tags":["architecture","ai-development"],"series":null,"minutes":4,"url":"https://ninochavez.co/blog/why-my-react-app-doesnt-use-jsx-and-what-that-taught-me-about-building-with-ai"},{"slug":"i-still-dont-really-know-jsx-and-i-dont-think-it-matters","title":"I Still Don’t Really Know JSX. And I Don't Think It Matters.","excerpt":"I built a production-grade React app—and still don’t fully understand JSX. In the AI-assisted era, syntax mastery matters less than system design. You’re not the coder anymore. You’re the architect. The real skill? Knowing what to ask, how to judge, and when the output doesn’t fit.","date":"2025-08-02T12:10:51.000Z","category":"AI & Automation","tags":["ai-development","reflection"],"series":null,"minutes":3,"url":"https://ninochavez.co/blog/i-still-dont-really-know-jsx-and-i-dont-think-it-matters"},{"slug":"i-dont-believe-the-agentic-ai-hype-and-heres-why-thats-not-ignorance","title":"Why I'm Skeptical of Agentic AI (Despite Using AI Every Day)","excerpt":"I use AI to code, test, document, and enforce rules. But I dont trust autonomous agents to plan and execute on their own.","date":"2025-08-02T08:12:00.000Z","category":"AI & Automation","tags":["architecture","ai-development"],"series":null,"minutes":2,"url":"https://ninochavez.co/blog/i-dont-believe-the-agentic-ai-hype-and-heres-why-thats-not-ignorance"},{"slug":"the-modern-js-stack-is-a-masterpiece-of-power-and-a-catastrophe-of-experience","title":"The Modern JavaScript Stack Is Powerful and Painful","excerpt":"I re-entered full-stack development after a decade in enterprise architecture. The modern JS stack can be productive—but its also a house of mirrors held together by duct tape and package churn.","date":"2025-08-01T23:22:00.000Z","category":"AI & Automation","tags":["architecture"],"series":null,"minutes":4,"url":"https://ninochavez.co/blog/the-modern-js-stack-is-a-masterpiece-of-power-and-a-catastrophe-of-experience"},{"slug":"even-the-ai-is-confused","title":"Even the AI Is Confused","excerpt":"What My Stack Audit Revealed About Modern Dev","date":"2025-08-01T18:20:42.000Z","category":"AI & Automation","tags":["ai-development"],"series":null,"minutes":3,"url":"https://ninochavez.co/blog/even-the-ai-is-confused"},{"slug":"ive-been-doing-ai-coding-wrong-sort-of","title":"Treating AI Prompts Like Jira Stories Changed My Workflow","excerpt":"The realization: AI needs the same structured context that humans need to start work safely.","date":"2025-08-01T06:29:00.000Z","category":"AI & Automation","tags":["ai-development"],"series":null,"minutes":2,"url":"https://ninochavez.co/blog/ive-been-doing-ai-coding-wrong-sort-of"},{"slug":"the-drift-beneath-the-velocity","title":"The Drift Beneath the Velocity","excerpt":"Week 2 of AI-assisted coding brought velocity — but also drift. This post explores the moment I realized I was managing AI agents like a team, and what that means for the future of software consulting.","date":"2025-08-01T00:43:56.000Z","category":"AI & Automation","tags":["ai-development","consulting"],"series":null,"minutes":3,"url":"https://ninochavez.co/blog/the-drift-beneath-the-velocity"},{"slug":"am-i-ever-not-working","title":"Am I Ever Not Working?","excerpt":"If your value lies in how you think, are you ever really off the clock? Lately, I’ve been chasing AI workflows at all hours—and thinking through systems even when I’m not at my keyboard. This post reflects on the cost of always being “on,” and how to protect the infrastructure: you.","date":"2025-07-31T20:42:57.000Z","category":"Reflection","tags":["reflection","architecture"],"series":null,"minutes":3,"url":"https://ninochavez.co/blog/am-i-ever-not-working"},{"slug":"ai-ops-docs-as-contracts","title":"When Documentation Becomes a Contract Your AI Must Follow","excerpt":"A styling bug taught me the difference between docs-as-code and docs-as-contracts—and why AI development demands the latter.","date":"2025-07-31T12:45:00.000Z","category":"AI & Automation","tags":["architecture","ai-development"],"series":null,"minutes":2,"url":"https://ninochavez.co/blog/ai-ops-docs-as-contracts"},{"slug":"skip-the-api-building-a-local-chatgpt-bridge-for-vs-code","title":"Skip the API: Building a Local ChatGPT Bridge for VS Code","excerpt":"Can you skip the API and wire ChatGPT directly into VS Code? Turns out, yes — with a little browser magic. This post breaks down how I thought about building a local-first bridge that replaces Kilo and avoids token costs, all powered by curiosity and a builder’s mindset.","date":"2025-07-31T11:47:37.000Z","category":"AI & Automation","tags":["ai-development","architecture"],"series":null,"minutes":3,"url":"https://ninochavez.co/blog/skip-the-api-building-a-local-chatgpt-bridge-for-vs-code"},{"slug":"from-qa-bottleneck-to-autonomous-confidence-how-i-built-a-fully-ai-driven-e2e-testing-framework","title":"Building an AI-Driven E2E Testing Framework from Scratch","excerpt":"Testing was my bottleneck. So I built a framework where AI writes, runs, and validates the tests.","date":"2025-07-30T19:44:00.000Z","category":"AI & Automation","tags":["engineering","ai-development"],"series":null,"minutes":2,"url":"https://ninochavez.co/blog/from-qa-bottleneck-to-autonomous-confidence-how-i-built-a-fully-ai-driven-e2e-testing-framework"},{"slug":"from-ai-copilot-to-ai-enforcer","title":"From Copilot to Enforcer: The AI Maturity Spectrum for Developers","excerpt":"Or: What happens when AI stops just helping you code — and starts holding your system accountable","date":"2025-07-30T17:46:05.000Z","category":"AI & Automation","tags":["ai-development","architecture"],"series":null,"minutes":3,"url":"https://ninochavez.co/blog/from-ai-copilot-to-ai-enforcer"},{"slug":"tool-and-die-thinking-for-the-ai-age","title":"Tool-and-Die Thinking for the AI Age","excerpt":"How AI coding tools helped me beat the overhead wall — and build faster than I think.","date":"2025-07-30T17:21:51.000Z","category":"AI & Automation","tags":["ai-development","reflection","architecture"],"series":null,"minutes":3,"url":"https://ninochavez.co/blog/tool-and-die-thinking-for-the-ai-age"},{"slug":"just-in-time-software","title":"Just-in-Time Software","excerpt":"Just like cloud killed the server rack, AI is killing fixed tools. I built a full E2E test system from scratch—faster, cheaper, tailored—using nothing but schema, rules, and AI prompts. Why buy tools when you can generate them just-in-time?","date":"2025-07-30T16:47:02.000Z","category":"AI & Automation","tags":["ai-development","consulting"],"series":null,"minutes":4,"url":"https://ninochavez.co/blog/just-in-time-software"},{"slug":"from-prompt-to-product-building-demo-mode-in-week-2","title":"Why Demo Mode Should Be a First-Class Feature","excerpt":"Building a sandboxed demo environment taught me that onboarding isnt just UX—it's architecture.","date":"2025-07-30T06:53:00.000Z","category":"AI & Automation","tags":["architecture","ai-development"],"series":null,"minutes":2,"url":"https://ninochavez.co/blog/from-prompt-to-product-building-demo-mode-in-week-2"},{"slug":"when-control-matters-behind-the-decision-to-build-feature-toggles-into-the-bracket-app","title":"Why I Built Feature Toggles Into the Bracket App","excerpt":"Sometimes the feature isnt for the user. It's for the system operators—the ones who have to keep the app online when the fire starts.","date":"2025-07-30T02:24:00.000Z","category":"AI & Automation","tags":["architecture","ai-development"],"series":null,"minutes":2,"url":"https://ninochavez.co/blog/when-control-matters-behind-the-decision-to-build-feature-toggles-into-the-bracket-app"},{"slug":"when-fast-isnt-fast-enough-the-shift-from-ai-coding-to-ai-concurrency","title":"When Fast Isn't Fast Enough: From AI Coding to AI Concurrency","excerpt":"Theres a strange kind of bottleneck that only shows up after you've gotten fast. The only real blocker left is waiting for a model to finish one thing before starting the next.","date":"2025-07-30T02:22:00.000Z","category":"AI & Automation","tags":["architecture","engineering","ai-development"],"series":null,"minutes":4,"url":"https://ninochavez.co/blog/when-fast-isnt-fast-enough-the-shift-from-ai-coding-to-ai-concurrency"},{"slug":"show-your-work-designing-ai-enforced-safeguards","title":"Designing AI-Enforced Safeguards for Development","excerpt":"After a few bugs slipped past my AI assistant, I redesigned the system to make safeguards automatic.","date":"2025-07-29T20:12:00.000Z","category":"AI & Automation","tags":["architecture","ai-development"],"series":null,"minutes":2,"url":"https://ninochavez.co/blog/show-your-work-designing-ai-enforced-safeguards"},{"slug":"fix-it-once-prevent-it-forever-how-i-made-a-dropdown-bug-impossible-to-reintroduce","title":"How I Made a Cache Bug Impossible to Reintroduce","excerpt":"A dropdown bug became a lesson in building automated guardrails that prevent architectural drift—especially when AI is generating your components.","date":"2025-07-29T05:16:00.000Z","category":"AI & Automation","tags":["architecture","ai-development"],"series":null,"minutes":2,"url":"https://ninochavez.co/blog/fix-it-once-prevent-it-forever-how-i-made-a-dropdown-bug-impossible-to-reintroduce"},{"slug":"who-reviewed-the-reviewer","title":"Who Reviewed the Reviewer?","excerpt":"This post is about that realization. About what happens when AI becomes the default reviewer, and starts learning from its own reflections.\n\nWe’re not just debugging code anymore.\nWe’re debugging the system that teaches itself how to review.","date":"2025-07-29T02:03:56.000Z","category":"AI & Automation","tags":["ai-development","reflection"],"series":null,"minutes":5,"url":"https://ninochavez.co/blog/who-reviewed-the-reviewer"},{"slug":"designing-for-environments-not-just-themes-a-volleyball-app-ux-case-study","title":"Why Light and Dark Mode Weren't Enough for Our Volleyball App","excerpt":"When your users are on sun-drenched beach courts and dimly lit evening gyms, you need more than a theme toggle. You need environment-aware design.","date":"2025-07-29T00:43:00.000Z","category":"AI & Automation","tags":["engineering"],"series":null,"minutes":2,"url":"https://ninochavez.co/blog/designing-for-environments-not-just-themes-a-volleyball-app-ux-case-study"},{"slug":"one-week-one-developer-one-production-system-how-i-used-ai-to-build-and-harden-the-lpo-bracket-manag","title":"Building a Production App Solo in Seven Days with AI","excerpt":"The key wasnt using AI to write code—it was creating a system to govern AI's behavior, output, and role in the development process.","date":"2025-07-28T18:41:00.000Z","category":"AI & Automation","tags":["architecture","ai-development"],"series":null,"minutes":3,"url":"https://ninochavez.co/blog/one-week-one-developer-one-production-system-how-i-used-ai-to-build-and-harden-the-lpo-bracket-manag"},{"slug":"tokens-are-dev-hours","title":"Tokens Are Dev Hours. Waste Is a Feature, Not a Bug.","excerpt":"With AI development, waste isn’t hidden in team velocity or burndown charts — it’s itemized on your invoice. That’s not a flaw. It’s a feature.","date":"2025-07-28T16:00:25.000Z","category":"AI & Automation","tags":["ai-development","reflection","architecture"],"series":null,"minutes":4,"url":"https://ninochavez.co/blog/tokens-are-dev-hours"},{"slug":"the-ai-engineer-has-joined-the-team","title":"The AI Engineer Has Joined the Team. Now What?","excerpt":"AI isn’t just accelerating software development — it’s reorganizing it. This post unpacks what changes when you start designing teams, tools, and processes around a human–AI hybrid model. Planning shifts. Costs shift. Roles shift. The real question is: are we ready to shift with it?","date":"2025-07-27T11:52:08.000Z","category":"AI & Automation","tags":["ai-development","leadership","architecture"],"series":null,"minutes":4,"url":"https://ninochavez.co/blog/the-ai-engineer-has-joined-the-team"},{"slug":"docs-as-code-isnt-enough-how-ai-ready-knowledge-layers-compress-weeks-of-work-into-hours","title":"How Structured Documentation Turned My AI Into an Engineering Team","excerpt":"Documentation can be more than a reference—it can be a knowledge layer that both humans and AI agents use to build.","date":"2025-07-26T18:51:00.000Z","category":"AI & Automation","tags":["architecture","ai-development"],"series":null,"minutes":2,"url":"https://ninochavez.co/blog/docs-as-code-isnt-enough-how-ai-ready-knowledge-layers-compress-weeks-of-work-into-hours"},{"slug":"from-fear-to-flow-my-accidental-journey-into-ai-powered-development","title":"How Fear of AI Turned Into an AI Development System","excerpt":"I started building with AI out of fear of being left behind. What I ended up with was a repeatable system for AI-driven development.","date":"2025-07-25T17:45:00.000Z","category":"AI & Automation","tags":["ai-development"],"series":null,"minutes":3,"url":"https://ninochavez.co/blog/from-fear-to-flow-my-accidental-journey-into-ai-powered-development"},{"slug":"from-fear-to-flow","title":"From Fear to Flow: How Building a Tournament Manager App Taught Me to Scale with AI","excerpt":"I didn’t start with a strategy. I started with fear. Here’s how a grassroots volleyball app turned into my personal AI bootcamp—and the repeatable framework I built along the way.","date":"2025-07-25T13:55:52.000Z","category":"AI & Automation","tags":["ai-development","architecture"],"series":null,"minutes":5,"url":"https://ninochavez.co/blog/from-fear-to-flow"},{"slug":"m-ai-engineering-team","title":"My AI Engineering Team","excerpt":"I didn’t set out to build an AI team. I just started with ChatGPT prompts for Lovable AI. Over time, it evolved into managing a layered squad of AI tools—architects, coders, and reviewers—working together like a real dev team. This changed how I work and lead AI-assisted projects.","date":"2025-07-23T15:53:59.000Z","category":"AI & Automation","tags":["ai-development","architecture"],"series":null,"minutes":2,"url":"https://ninochavez.co/blog/m-ai-engineering-team"},{"slug":"start-with-a-sketch","title":"Getting Unstuck: Why We Start With a Sketch","excerpt":"Projects stall when we confuse blockers with priorities. This post explores the “desk-building” metaphor to help teams stop over-planning and start making progress — by sketching first, sorting screws later.","date":"2025-07-22T15:36:55.000Z","category":"Field Notes","tags":["consulting"],"series":null,"minutes":3,"url":"https://ninochavez.co/blog/start-with-a-sketch"},{"slug":"when-you-start-to-hear-the-music","title":"When You Start to Hear the Music","excerpt":"What happens when AI sharpens your mind, but drifts you further from those who don’t think that way? This post explores the private cost of clarity — and the quiet grief that comes from outgrowing the resolution your old relationships were built on.","date":"2025-07-10T23:58:06.000Z","category":"AI & Automation","tags":["ai-development","reflection","architecture"],"series":null,"minutes":3,"url":"https://ninochavez.co/blog/when-you-start-to-hear-the-music"},{"slug":"do-you-smell-smoke","title":"The Risk of Asking “Do You Smell That?” When There’s No Fire","excerpt":"In consulting, sensing a problem feels like smelling smoke. But is there really a fire? This piece explores when to speak up and when to hold back — balancing risk detection with protecting people from blame. Leadership is often about navigating that tension with care.","date":"2025-07-09T20:52:53.000Z","category":"Consulting","tags":["consulting","leadership"],"series":null,"minutes":4,"url":"https://ninochavez.co/blog/do-you-smell-smoke"},{"slug":"from-pattern-to-people","title":"From Pattern to People","excerpt":"The hidden cost of clarity in an AI-shaped mind","date":"2025-07-08T12:01:17.000Z","category":"AI & Automation","tags":["ai-development","reflection"],"series":null,"minutes":3,"url":"https://ninochavez.co/blog/from-pattern-to-people"},{"slug":"still-your-car-still-your-wrench","title":"Still Your Car. Still Your Wrench.","excerpt":"What AI Coding Tools Don’t Tell You About Building Real Software","date":"2025-07-06T22:58:22.000Z","category":"AI & Automation","tags":["ai-development"],"series":null,"minutes":3,"url":"https://ninochavez.co/blog/still-your-car-still-your-wrench"},{"slug":"from-prompt-to-pattern","title":"From Prompt to Pattern","excerpt":"This post wraps my “Help Me Help You” arc and opens a new question: If precision makes AI more effective, does it also increase human disconnection? I’ve felt the drift. The next thread I’m pulling on is: how to stay human inside all this structure.","date":"2025-07-02T02:38:18.000Z","category":"","tags":["ai-development","architecture"],"series":{"slug":"from-prompt-to-pattern","position":5},"minutes":2,"url":"https://ninochavez.co/blog/from-prompt-to-pattern"},{"slug":"how-i-work-with-ais-and-why","title":"How I Work With AIs (And Why)","excerpt":"A behind-the-scenes look at how I use GPT and Lovable together. One rewrites the prompt. One generates the code. I just define the intent — and stay out of the way. This isn’t a tech stack. It’s a new mode of working.","date":"2025-07-02T02:31:57.000Z","category":"","tags":["ai-development","architecture"],"series":{"slug":"from-prompt-to-pattern","position":4},"minutes":3,"url":"https://ninochavez.co/blog/how-i-work-with-ais-and-why"},{"slug":"prompt-strategists-agent-orchestras-and-what-comes-next","title":"Prompt Strategists, Agent Orchestras, and What Comes Next","excerpt":"Prompting is evolving into orchestration. One AI clarifies, another executes, a third checks the result. We’re entering an era where modular intelligence matters — and prompts become the interface between thinking systems.","date":"2025-07-02T02:27:30.000Z","category":"","tags":["ai-development","architecture"],"series":{"slug":"from-prompt-to-pattern","position":3},"minutes":3,"url":"https://ninochavez.co/blog/prompt-strategists-agent-orchestras-and-what-comes-next"},{"slug":"when-one-ai-rewrites-for-another","title":"When One AI Rewrites for Another","excerpt":"Prompt rewriting isn’t just a clever trick — it’s becoming core infrastructure. From cloud tools to agent chains, we’re seeing a shift: one AI clarifies the ask, another executes. The result? Fewer errors. Smarter systems.","date":"2025-07-02T02:22:49.000Z","category":"AI & Automation","tags":["ai-development"],"series":{"slug":"from-prompt-to-pattern","position":2},"minutes":3,"url":"https://ninochavez.co/blog/when-one-ai-rewrites-for-another"},{"slug":"help-me-help-you-help-me","title":"Help Me Help You (Help Me)","excerpt":"One AI helps another do its job better. I use GPT to rewrite prompts for Lovable — cutting errors, saving time, and revealing a deeper pattern: intent → refiner → executor. This isn’t just prompt cleanup. It’s the start of a new architecture.","date":"2025-07-02T02:15:12.000Z","category":"AI & Automation","tags":["ai-development"],"series":{"slug":"from-prompt-to-pattern","position":1},"minutes":3,"url":"https://ninochavez.co/blog/help-me-help-you-help-me"},{"slug":"the-ai-approach-reset","title":"How I Used One AI to Train Another: A Tactical Reset in Data Architecture and AI Tooling Strategy","excerpt":"Like most devs experimenting with AI tools, I’ve found myself juggling multiple platforms, APIs, and half-understood schemas to build things faster. Sometimes it works. Other times, it works against you.","date":"2025-06-30T20:50:47.000Z","category":"AI & Automation","tags":["ai-development","consulting","leadership","architecture"],"series":null,"minutes":4,"url":"https://ninochavez.co/blog/the-ai-approach-reset"},{"slug":"50-of-ai-later-lessons-from-burning-credits-fast","title":"$50 of AI Later: Lessons from Burning Credits Fast","excerpt":"AI credits vanished quickly, highlighting hidden costs and forcing clarity into my development process. Here's how a $50 investment turned into a practical blueprint for smarter AI‑assisted builds.","date":"2025-06-29T06:09:06.000Z","category":"AI & Automation","tags":["ai-development","engineering"],"series":null,"minutes":6,"url":"https://ninochavez.co/blog/50-of-ai-later-lessons-from-burning-credits-fast"},{"slug":"the-lost-phone-test-for-ai-could-your-org-still-function-tomorrow","title":"The Lost‑Phone Test for AI: Could Your Org Still Function Tomorrow?","excerpt":"Dropped phone, lost life. Same test applies to corporate AI: if your copilots vanished tomorrow, would work even slow down? The “Lost-Phone Test” exposes integration gaps and makes the case for a Chief Intelligence Officer to weave tools into real workflows.","date":"2025-06-28T02:49:06.000Z","category":"AI & Automation","tags":["ai-development","leadership","reflection","architecture"],"series":null,"minutes":3,"url":"https://ninochavez.co/blog/the-lost-phone-test-for-ai-could-your-org-still-function-tomorrow"},{"slug":"overnight-dev-from-rage-quit-to-release-in-72-hours-with-ai-riding-shotgun","title":"Overnight Dev: From Rage-Quit to Release in 72 Hours—With AI Riding Shotgun","excerpt":"Burned-out coder to live app in one weekend: two failed scrapers, one hidden JSON API, and AI tools that scaffolded the rest. How DevTools + GPT turned AES volleyball data into an MVP—and why your next Jira ticket might build itself.","date":"2025-06-27T20:15:03.000Z","category":"AI & Automation","tags":["ai-development","engineering"],"series":null,"minutes":2,"url":"https://ninochavez.co/blog/overnight-dev-from-rage-quit-to-release-in-72-hours-with-ai-riding-shotgun"},{"slug":"its-time-for-the-cio-2-0","title":"It’s Time for the CIO 2.0","excerpt":"Most AI conversations start in the wrong place — with tools, not capabilities. What’s missing isn’t another pilot. It’s a new executive role: someone to steward how your organization thinks, learns, and evolves.","date":"2025-06-27T05:27:55.000Z","category":"AI & Automation","tags":["ai-development","leadership","architecture"],"series":null,"minutes":3,"url":"https://ninochavez.co/blog/its-time-for-the-cio-2-0"},{"slug":"not-just-fast-but-together","title":"Not Just Fast, But Together","excerpt":"If you want to go fast, go alone; if you want to go far, go together","date":"2025-06-25T16:33:35.000Z","category":"Philosophy","tags":["leadership","reflection"],"series":null,"minutes":3,"url":"https://ninochavez.co/blog/not-just-fast-but-together"},{"slug":"is-resilience-a-systems-problem","title":"Is Resilience a Systems Problem?","excerpt":"In my last post, I wrote about the shift from shutdown to speedrun — how we’ve had to adapt, over and over again, just to keep moving. First to survive the pandemic. Now to keep up with AI.","date":"2025-06-25T16:33:26.000Z","category":"Philosophy","tags":["reflection"],"series":null,"minutes":3,"url":"https://ninochavez.co/blog/is-resilience-a-systems-problem"},{"slug":"from-shutdown-to-speedrun","title":"From Shutdown to Speedrun","excerpt":"The Cost of Surviving the Age of Constant Upgrades","date":"2025-06-25T16:33:16.000Z","category":"Philosophy","tags":["ai-development","reflection","architecture"],"series":null,"minutes":4,"url":"https://ninochavez.co/blog/from-shutdown-to-speedrun"},{"slug":"strength-through-repetition","title":"Strength Through Repetition","excerpt":"The page isn’t the enemy. The page is the gym. You don’t show up to look good. You show up to build grip. Form. Force. Reps. That’s how the voice gets real.","date":"2025-06-24T20:22:33.000Z","category":"Reflection","tags":["reflection"],"series":null,"minutes":1,"url":"https://ninochavez.co/blog/strength-through-repetition"},{"slug":"reinvention-transformation","title":"Reinvention > Transformation","excerpt":"Most reorgs I’ve seen are just corporate feng shui — shift a few boxes, rename a few titles, pretend it’s visionary. But this one? This one actually maps to something real. For once, the language isn’t just for clients — it mirrors what I’ve been doing in my own damn operating system.","date":"2025-06-24T19:00:51.000Z","category":"","tags":["commerce","consulting"],"series":null,"minutes":3,"url":"https://ninochavez.co/blog/reinvention-transformation"},{"slug":"youre-sending-self-driving-cars-down-unmarked-roads","title":"You’re Sending Self-Driving Cars Down Unmarked Roads","excerpt":"AI doesn’t fail because it’s bad — it fails because your data lacks the infrastructure it needs to navigate. Language models don’t just search — they interpret. Most orgs haven’t built for that.","date":"2025-06-23T01:51:09.000Z","category":"AI & Automation","tags":["ai-development","commerce","architecture"],"series":null,"minutes":4,"url":"https://ninochavez.co/blog/youre-sending-self-driving-cars-down-unmarked-roads"},{"slug":"the-mvp-mirage","title":"The MVP Mirage","excerpt":"Most MVPs aren’t minimum or viable — they’re just premature.","date":"2025-06-20T07:30:29.000Z","category":"","tags":["consulting","architecture"],"series":null,"minutes":3,"url":"https://ninochavez.co/blog/the-mvp-mirage"},{"slug":"leadership-reflection","title":"Leadership Reflection","excerpt":"One of the easiest traps to fall into as a leader or expert is assuming we already understand. That assumption — even when subtle — shuts down curiosity, slows progress, and can quietly place the burden of clarity on everyone but ourselves.","date":"2025-06-19T13:22:15.000Z","category":"","tags":["leadership","reflection"],"series":null,"minutes":1,"url":"https://ninochavez.co/blog/leadership-reflection"},{"slug":"chatgpt-doesnt-eat-so-why-do-i-keep-asking-it-to-cook","title":"ChatGPT Doesn’t Eat—So Why Do I Keep Asking It to Cook?","excerpt":"LLMs can draft the menu, but you still have to taste the sauce. Here’s my field-note recipe for closing the fidelity gap between what ChatGPT writes and what actually works.","date":"2025-06-18T18:31:51.000Z","category":"AI & Automation","tags":["ai-development"],"series":null,"minutes":3,"url":"https://ninochavez.co/blog/chatgpt-doesnt-eat-so-why-do-i-keep-asking-it-to-cook"},{"slug":"experience-is-loud-turn-it-down-so-you-can-learn","title":"Experience Is Loud—Turn It Down So You Can Learn","excerpt":"Hard‑won patterns sound like wisdom—until they echo so loudly you miss new ideas. This month I learned: AI model tuning isn’t just prompting, shutter‑speed instincts fail at 60 fps video, and Figma spacing ≠ CSS. Beginner reps, reverse mentorship, and stranger audits keep the channel clear.","date":"2025-06-18T17:30:02.000Z","category":"Reflection","tags":["reflection"],"series":null,"minutes":2,"url":"https://ninochavez.co/blog/experience-is-loud-turn-it-down-so-you-can-learn"},{"slug":"the-cake-is-a-lie-why-ai-isnt-ready","title":"The Cake Is a Lie: Why AI Isn’t Ready","excerpt":"Everyone says AI can build for you — that you just describe the thing, and it ships itself. But I actually tried. I took it seriously. And what I found was brittle, inconsistent, and full of guesswork. If it took this much effort to build a landing page, what happens when the stakes are higher?","date":"2025-06-17T04:13:37.000Z","category":"AI & Automation","tags":["ai-development"],"series":null,"minutes":3,"url":"https://ninochavez.co/blog/the-cake-is-a-lie-why-ai-isnt-ready"},{"slug":"signal-or-spectacle","title":"Signal or Spectacle?","excerpt":"Even when I’m not trying to posture, sometimes it feels like the platform does it for me. This is about the moment when sharing something honest starts to feel like a performance — and how I’m trying to stay grounded in signal, not spectacle.","date":"2025-06-16T19:39:14.000Z","category":"Meta","tags":["reflection","architecture"],"series":null,"minutes":2,"url":"https://ninochavez.co/blog/signal-or-spectacle"},{"slug":"signal-reflex-signal-dispatch","title":"Signal Reflex → Signal Dispatch","excerpt":"Signal Reflex is now Signal Dispatch — a shift from sensing to sending. Same voice, sharper intent. This is where ideas go out.","date":"2025-06-16T04:11:08.000Z","category":"","tags":["ai-development","leadership"],"series":{"slug":"signal-reflex-launch","position":5},"minutes":1,"url":"https://ninochavez.co/blog/signal-reflex-signal-dispatch"},{"slug":"becoming-the-copilot","title":"Becoming the Copilot","excerpt":"Signal Dispatch · Field Notes on Agency in Relationship","date":"2025-06-16T03:17:39.000Z","category":"Field Notes","tags":["leadership"],"series":{"slug":"signal-reflex-launch","position":4},"minutes":2,"url":"https://ninochavez.co/blog/becoming-the-copilot"},{"slug":"reading-the-road","title":"Reading the Road","excerpt":"Signal Dispatch · Field Notes on Influence","date":"2025-06-16T03:07:25.000Z","category":"Leadership","tags":["leadership"],"series":null,"minutes":2,"url":"https://ninochavez.co/blog/reading-the-road"},{"slug":"driving-while-not-driving","title":"Driving While Not Driving","excerpt":"(How to Keep Moving Without Losing Yourself)","date":"2025-06-15T03:55:15.000Z","category":"","tags":["ai-development","leadership"],"series":null,"minutes":3,"url":"https://ninochavez.co/blog/driving-while-not-driving"},{"slug":"put-the-camera-down","title":"Put the Camera Down","excerpt":"What if you're not here to capture the moment—but to be the aperture it passes through?","date":"2025-06-14T03:53:10.000Z","category":"Meta","tags":["reflection","architecture"],"series":null,"minutes":2,"url":"https://ninochavez.co/blog/put-the-camera-down"},{"slug":"the-machine-loom","title":"The Machine Loom","excerpt":"If humans are the loom, AI is the thread—fast, abundant, and increasingly tangled.","date":"2025-06-14T03:27:26.000Z","category":"AI & Automation","tags":["ai-development","architecture"],"series":{"slug":"the-loom","position":2},"minutes":3,"url":"https://ninochavez.co/blog/the-machine-loom"},{"slug":"the-human-loom","title":"The Human Loom","excerpt":"You don’t have to be the thread. Or the pattern. Just be the thing that lets it all come together.","date":"2025-06-14T03:05:29.000Z","category":"Leadership","tags":["consulting","leadership","reflection","architecture"],"series":{"slug":"the-loom","position":1},"minutes":2,"url":"https://ninochavez.co/blog/the-human-loom"},{"slug":"i-built-a-site-just-to-see-if-i-still-could","title":"I Built a Site Just to See If I Still Could","excerpt":"I didn’t need to build the site from scratch. That was the point.","date":"2025-06-14T02:56:15.000Z","category":"Field Notes","tags":["ai-development","engineering"],"series":null,"minutes":3,"url":"https://ninochavez.co/blog/i-built-a-site-just-to-see-if-i-still-could"},{"slug":"how-content-and-commerce-actually-connect-now","title":"How Content and Commerce Actually Connect Now","excerpt":"Your content is the storefront. If it’s not reducing friction or moving someone closer to a decision, it’s not connected to commerce at all.","date":"2025-06-13T13:00:40.000Z","category":"Commerce","tags":["ai-development","commerce"],"series":null,"minutes":2,"url":"https://ninochavez.co/blog/how-content-and-commerce-actually-connect-now"},{"slug":"the-cleanup-economy","title":"The Cleanup Economy","excerpt":"Every tech revolution builds a fast market—and leaves a mess. Cleanup becomes its own economy: FinOps, platform teams, and second-wave consultants turning v1 chaos into stability. The real money? It's in making things actually work.","date":"2025-06-12T22:45:25.000Z","category":"Reflection","tags":["reflection"],"series":null,"minutes":2,"url":"https://ninochavez.co/blog/the-cleanup-economy"},{"slug":"the-spotlight-isnt-the-stage","title":"The Spotlight Isn’t the Stage","excerpt":"A shiny idea isn't strategy. This post digs into decision hygiene: the discipline of thinking beyond the spotlight, and seeing the systems, ownership, and scope your choices actually live inside.","date":"2025-06-12T22:19:29.000Z","category":"Leadership","tags":["leadership","architecture"],"series":null,"minutes":2,"url":"https://ninochavez.co/blog/the-spotlight-isnt-the-stage"},{"slug":"when-meta-conversations-start-to-sound-like-sermons","title":"When Meta Conversations Start to Sound Like Sermons","excerpt":"I’ve been writing and thinking so much about how I think, it’s started to shape my real-life conversations — sometimes in ways that feel disconnected. When does thoughtful reflection cross the line into sermonizing? And how do we find balance between clarity and presence?","date":"2025-06-12T22:11:25.000Z","category":"Reflection","tags":["reflection","architecture"],"series":null,"minutes":2,"url":"https://ninochavez.co/blog/when-meta-conversations-start-to-sound-like-sermons"},{"slug":"the-sky-is-not-falling","title":"The Sky Is Not Falling","excerpt":"I built the core Let’s Pepper site in under 2 hours—then spent over 8 trying to get one visual detail (the section dividers) to look right. AI can prototype, but it doesn’t ship. This post breaks down why the real work happens after the first draft, and why experience still matters more than ever.","date":"2025-06-12T13:28:32.000Z","category":"AI & Automation","tags":["ai-development","engineering"],"series":null,"minutes":2,"url":"https://ninochavez.co/blog/the-sky-is-not-falling"},{"slug":"coaching-without-coddling-how-i-support-high-potential-people-without-micromanaging","title":"Coaching Without Coddling: How I Support High-Potential People Without Micromanaging","excerpt":"Once I know someone’s worth investing in, I shift gears. Here’s how I coach without taking the wheel—and why presence matters more than pressure.","date":"2025-06-12T01:56:49.000Z","category":"Leadership","tags":["ai-development","consulting","leadership"],"series":null,"minutes":3,"url":"https://ninochavez.co/blog/coaching-without-coddling-how-i-support-high-potential-people-without-micromanaging"},{"slug":"coach-up-or-coach-out-what-i-look-for-early","title":"Coach Up or Coach Out? What I Look for Early","excerpt":"Not everyone clicks right away. But I’ve learned to spot the difference between someone who just needs support—and someone who’s not built for the work we do.","date":"2025-06-12T01:53:34.000Z","category":"Leadership","tags":["consulting","leadership"],"series":null,"minutes":3,"url":"https://ninochavez.co/blog/coach-up-or-coach-out-what-i-look-for-early"},{"slug":"we-dont-need-more-coders-we-need-early-stage-problem-solvers","title":"We Don’t Need More Coders. We Need Early-Stage Problem Solvers.","excerpt":"The game changed. We don’t need new hires who’ve built a few apps—we need people who can navigate ambiguity, think in systems, and ask the right questions early.","date":"2025-06-12T01:52:32.000Z","category":"Commerce","tags":["commerce","consulting","leadership"],"series":null,"minutes":2,"url":"https://ninochavez.co/blog/we-dont-need-more-coders-we-need-early-stage-problem-solvers"},{"slug":"how-we-find-the-ones-who-just-get-it","title":"How We Find the Ones Who Just Get It","excerpt":"Hiring the right consultant isn’t about checking boxes. It’s about building the kind of process that makes the right people show up—and lets the wrong ones opt out early.","date":"2025-06-12T01:51:27.000Z","category":"Leadership","tags":["ai-development","consulting","leadership"],"series":null,"minutes":2,"url":"https://ninochavez.co/blog/how-we-find-the-ones-who-just-get-it"},{"slug":"when-youre-good-at-solving-problems-they-multiply","title":"When You’re Good at Solving Problems, They Multiply","excerpt":"Being a great problem solver isn’t always the win it sounds like. If you’re not careful, you become the dumping ground for everyone else’s chaos. Here’s what I’ve learned about restraint, scope, and selective clarity.","date":"2025-06-11T23:44:06.000Z","category":"Leadership","tags":["leadership"],"series":null,"minutes":2,"url":"https://ninochavez.co/blog/when-youre-good-at-solving-problems-they-multiply"},{"slug":"the-gift-i-cant-give-myself","title":"The Gift I Can’t Give Myself","excerpt":"I hold up the mirror for others all the time—clients, teammates, athletes. I just can’t seem to look in it myself. This post explores what it means to help others see their potential while still wrestling with your own.","date":"2025-06-11T22:11:28.000Z","category":"Leadership","tags":["leadership","architecture"],"series":null,"minutes":2,"url":"https://ninochavez.co/blog/the-gift-i-cant-give-myself"},{"slug":"the-real-work-is-reading-the-signal","title":"The Real Work Is Reading the Signal","excerpt":"The complexity isn’t in the tech—it’s in the noise. This final post in the Commerce Drift arc explores why reading the signal is the real skill behind every good commerce strategy.","date":"2025-06-11T22:05:28.000Z","category":"Commerce","tags":["commerce","architecture"],"series":null,"minutes":2,"url":"https://ninochavez.co/blog/the-real-work-is-reading-the-signal"},{"slug":"the-stranger-with-my-name","title":"The Stranger With My Name","excerpt":"I’ve heard people describe me in ways I barely recognize. At first, it felt like they were talking about someone else. But now I’m wondering—what if they’re seeing something I haven’t figured out how to see in myself?","date":"2025-06-11T13:00:19.000Z","category":"Meta","tags":["reflection","architecture"],"series":null,"minutes":2,"url":"https://ninochavez.co/blog/the-stranger-with-my-name"},{"slug":"when-the-tools-get-in-the-way","title":"When the Tools Get in the Way","excerpt":"We made websites easier to build by hiding the code. But now that AI can write that code for us, the abstraction layers are becoming the new friction.","date":"2025-06-11T04:37:49.000Z","category":"AI & Automation","tags":["ai-development"],"series":null,"minutes":3,"url":"https://ninochavez.co/blog/when-the-tools-get-in-the-way"},{"slug":"how-i-wrote-living-in-the-comments","title":"How I Wrote “Living in the Comments”","excerpt":"This post was already about self-doubt. So writing it with an AI didn’t make it easier—it made the mirror sharper.","date":"2025-06-10T18:06:38.000Z","category":"Meta","tags":["reflection","architecture"],"series":null,"minutes":3,"url":"https://ninochavez.co/blog/how-i-wrote-living-in-the-comments"},{"slug":"living-in-the-comments","title":"Living in the Comments","excerpt":"Sometimes I’m not writing for clarity. I’m writing to defend myself against a comment that hasn’t been written yet.","date":"2025-06-10T17:34:42.000Z","category":"Meta","tags":["reflection","architecture"],"series":null,"minutes":2,"url":"https://ninochavez.co/blog/living-in-the-comments"},{"slug":"when-custom-isnt-better-the-burden-of-bespoke","title":"When Custom Isn’t Better: The Burden of Bespoke","excerpt":"We romanticize personalization. But sometimes the problem isn’t lack of choice—it’s too much of it. Especially when we’re not sure what we really want.","date":"2025-06-10T15:57:24.000Z","category":"Commerce","tags":["commerce","architecture"],"series":null,"minutes":2,"url":"https://ninochavez.co/blog/when-custom-isnt-better-the-burden-of-bespoke"},{"slug":"is-composable-just-ikea-for-commerce","title":"Is Composable Just IKEA for Commerce?","excerpt":"Composable promised freedom from the monolith. But in chasing modular flexibility, are we just assembling the same storefront with different colors and calling it strategy?","date":"2025-06-09T20:15:21.000Z","category":"Commerce","tags":["commerce","architecture"],"series":null,"minutes":3,"url":"https://ninochavez.co/blog/is-composable-just-ikea-for-commerce"},{"slug":"the-cost-of-sophistication","title":"The Cost of Sophistication","excerpt":"Getting sharper comes at a cost. The more refined your thinking becomes, the more you risk drifting into isolation. This post explores the hidden tax of clarity—and what it means to stay reachable without dumbing yourself down.","date":"2025-06-09T13:00:57.000Z","category":"Meta","tags":["reflection","architecture"],"series":null,"minutes":3,"url":"https://ninochavez.co/blog/the-cost-of-sophistication"},{"slug":"the-role-of-self-awareness-in-every-tool-i-use","title":"The Role of Self-Awareness in Every Tool I Use","excerpt":"I don’t chase tools anymore. If it fights my instincts or adds ceremony, I’m out. If it sharpens my clarity, it stays. That’s the filter.","date":"2025-06-09T13:00:15.000Z","category":"AI & Automation","tags":["ai-development","reflection"],"series":null,"minutes":3,"url":"https://ninochavez.co/blog/the-role-of-self-awareness-in-every-tool-i-use"},{"slug":"writing-helped-me-notice-i-was-seeing-differently","title":"Writing Helped Me Notice I Was Seeing Differently","excerpt":" I didn’t set out to write about personal growth. I just wanted to get clearer. But writing about other things helped me finally put into words a shift I’ve been feeling for years—that sometimes growth changes how you see the world before you even realize it.","date":"2025-06-08T23:00:26.000Z","category":"Meta","tags":["reflection","architecture"],"series":null,"minutes":2,"url":"https://ninochavez.co/blog/writing-helped-me-notice-i-was-seeing-differently"},{"slug":"sharpened-doesnt-mean-sterile","title":"Sharpened Doesn’t Mean Sterile","excerpt":"As your work gets more refined, it risks losing the texture that made it real. But if you do it right, refinement doesn’t erase your voice—it reveals it.","date":"2025-06-07T16:37:54.000Z","category":"Meta","tags":["reflection","architecture"],"series":null,"minutes":3,"url":"https://ninochavez.co/blog/sharpened-doesnt-mean-sterile"},{"slug":"do-i-still-sound-like-me","title":"Do I Still Sound Like Me?","excerpt":"Your writing gets sharper. Your thinking gets clearer. Your tone gets cleaner. But somewhere along the way, you wonder if the people who liked the messy version of you still recognize the voice.","date":"2025-06-07T16:19:48.000Z","category":"Meta","tags":["reflection","architecture"],"series":null,"minutes":3,"url":"https://ninochavez.co/blog/do-i-still-sound-like-me"},{"slug":"if-the-storefront-is-dead-where-do-you-compete-now","title":"If the Storefront Is Dead, Where Do You Compete Now?","excerpt":"If customers don’t convert on your site, where do they decide? Trust now starts upstream—off-site, in content, and with zero friction.","date":"2025-06-06T17:00:59.000Z","category":"Commerce","tags":["commerce","architecture"],"series":{"slug":"the-storefront","position":2},"minutes":2,"url":"https://ninochavez.co/blog/if-the-storefront-is-dead-where-do-you-compete-now"},{"slug":"why-this-made-you-ask","title":"Why This Made You Ask","excerpt":"When I started this blog, someone close to me asked, “What are you doing with this?” Not my photography. Not my DJ mixes. Just this. I’ve been thinking about why.","date":"2025-06-06T16:41:41.000Z","category":"Reflection","tags":["reflection","architecture"],"series":null,"minutes":2,"url":"https://ninochavez.co/blog/why-this-made-you-ask"},{"slug":"the-calculator-effect","title":"The Calculator Effect","excerpt":"I started checking the calculator even when I already knew the answer. Not because I needed it—because I didn’t trust myself. That’s the part that stuck with me. Not the math. The muscle I stopped using.","date":"2025-06-06T14:44:57.000Z","category":"Philosophy","tags":["reflection"],"series":null,"minutes":1,"url":"https://ninochavez.co/blog/the-calculator-effect"},{"slug":"is-chatgpt-doing-my-thinking-for-me","title":"Is ChatGPT Doing My Thinking for Me?","excerpt":"This thing can write a clean draft faster than I can finish my coffee. But then I read it back and think—did *I* even say that? Or did I just agree with it because it sounded smart? That’s the trap.","date":"2025-06-06T14:44:49.000Z","category":"Philosophy","tags":["reflection"],"series":null,"minutes":1,"url":"https://ninochavez.co/blog/is-chatgpt-doing-my-thinking-for-me"},{"slug":"muscle-memory-and-mental-atrophy","title":"Muscle Memory and Mental Atrophy","excerpt":"It starts small. A pause before spelling rhythm. Letting the GPS run when you already know the way. That pause? It used to be fluency. Now it’s drift.","date":"2025-06-06T14:44:43.000Z","category":"Philosophy","tags":["reflection"],"series":null,"minutes":1,"url":"https://ninochavez.co/blog/muscle-memory-and-mental-atrophy"},{"slug":"why-friction-matters","title":"Why Friction Matters","excerpt":"The hardest part of writing isn’t writing—it’s sticking with your own thoughts long enough to figure out what you’re really trying to say. AI makes that part go away. Which is the problem.","date":"2025-06-06T14:44:37.000Z","category":"Philosophy","tags":["reflection"],"series":null,"minutes":1,"url":"https://ninochavez.co/blog/why-friction-matters"},{"slug":"tool-assistant-or-crutch","title":"Tool, Assistant, or Crutch?","excerpt":"Every tool starts off helpful. Then it gets easy. Then it becomes default. And if you’re not careful, default turns into dependency. That’s the moment to pay attention to.","date":"2025-06-06T14:44:29.000Z","category":"Philosophy","tags":["reflection"],"series":null,"minutes":1,"url":"https://ninochavez.co/blog/tool-assistant-or-crutch"},{"slug":"did-you-hear-something","title":"Did You Hear Something?","excerpt":"Sometimes I think I’m hearing subtle signals that no one else picks up. Other times, I wonder if I’m just hallucinating signals to feel smart.","date":"2025-06-06T13:00:57.000Z","category":"Reflection","tags":["reflection"],"series":null,"minutes":2,"url":"https://ninochavez.co/blog/did-you-hear-something"},{"slug":"youre-not-broken-but-youre-not-off-the-hook","title":"You’re Not Broken, But You’re Not Off the Hook","excerpt":"The story that you’re too damaged to change is just another form of avoidance. False exemption doesn’t always sound like pride—it often sounds like hopelessness. But even if you’re not to blame for how the storm started, you’re still responsible for how you move through it.","date":"2025-06-05T19:47:36.000Z","category":"Reflection","tags":["leadership","reflection"],"series":null,"minutes":2,"url":"https://ninochavez.co/blog/youre-not-broken-but-youre-not-off-the-hook"},{"slug":"this-doesnt-work-for-me","title":"This Doesn’t Work for Me","excerpt":"False exemption often hides behind logic. We convince ourselves that the strategy is valid, the tool makes sense—but not for someone like me. This isn’t ignorance. It’s identity-protection dressed up as reason.","date":"2025-06-05T19:47:25.000Z","category":"Reflection","tags":["reflection"],"series":null,"minutes":2,"url":"https://ninochavez.co/blog/this-doesnt-work-for-me"},{"slug":"the-cost-of-staying-dry","title":"The Cost of Staying Dry","excerpt":"Choosing growth isn’t just about courage. It’s about cost. To change, you have to give something up—comfort, certainty, even identity. And deep down, many of us decide we’d rather stay soaked.","date":"2025-06-05T19:47:17.000Z","category":"Leadership","tags":["leadership","reflection"],"series":null,"minutes":2,"url":"https://ninochavez.co/blog/the-cost-of-staying-dry"},{"slug":"standing-in-the-rain","title":"Standing in the Rain","excerpt":"This isn’t about people who don’t know better. It’s about people who do. Who see the storm coming, feel the weight of the umbrella in their hand… and still stand there, getting soaked.","date":"2025-06-05T19:47:06.000Z","category":"Leadership","tags":["leadership","reflection"],"series":null,"minutes":2,"url":"https://ninochavez.co/blog/standing-in-the-rain"},{"slug":"how-i-structure-my-ai-workflows-to-support-real-thinking","title":"How I Structure My AI Workflows to Support Real Thinking","excerpt":"My AI workflow isn’t about speed—it’s about clarity. Here’s how I use LLMs to shape messy ideas and reduce drag when I need to think straight.","date":"2025-06-05T13:00:27.000Z","category":"AI & Automation","tags":["ai-development"],"series":null,"minutes":3,"url":"https://ninochavez.co/blog/how-i-structure-my-ai-workflows-to-support-real-thinking"},{"slug":"the-rules-i-actually-follow","title":"The Rules I Actually Follow","excerpt":"These aren’t feel-good slogans or poster values. Just the quiet rules that have kept me useful, sane, and steady—even when the pressure’s high and the spotlight’s off.","date":"2025-06-04T23:05:04.000Z","category":"Leadership","tags":["leadership"],"series":null,"minutes":2,"url":"https://ninochavez.co/blog/the-rules-i-actually-follow"},{"slug":"what-coaching-teaches-me-about-consulting","title":"What Coaching Teaches Me About Consulting","excerpt":"Coaching club volleyball taught me more about leadership than any workshop ever could. From managing expectations to building trust, this post breaks down the surprising overlaps between the gym and the boardroom.","date":"2025-06-04T23:01:05.000Z","category":"Leadership","tags":["leadership","architecture"],"series":null,"minutes":3,"url":"https://ninochavez.co/blog/what-coaching-teaches-me-about-consulting"},{"slug":"the-bet-i-didnt-know-i-was-making","title":"The Bet I Didn’t Know I Was Making","excerpt":"Some choices don’t reveal their full meaning until much later. It’s not that the decision was wrong—it’s that the person you become after making it sees things differently.","date":"2025-06-04T22:40:15.000Z","category":"Reflection","tags":["reflection"],"series":null,"minutes":2,"url":"https://ninochavez.co/blog/the-bet-i-didnt-know-i-was-making"},{"slug":"quiet-was-armor","title":"Quiet Was Armor","excerpt":"Signal Reflex isn’t a beginning. It’s a different lens. For years, my photos have shown how I see the world. Now I’m sharing how I think—and what that reveals about how I lead, learn, and grow.","date":"2025-06-04T12:32:03.000Z","category":"Reflection","tags":["reflection"],"series":null,"minutes":2,"url":"https://ninochavez.co/blog/quiet-was-armor"},{"slug":"why-i-use-ai-every-day-and-why-i-started-writing-about-it","title":"Why I Use AI Every Day (And Why I Started Writing About It)","excerpt":"I didn’t use AI to go faster—I used it to catch up with myself. This blog is where that journey turned into something useful and real.","date":"2025-06-04T05:00:31.000Z","category":"AI & Automation","tags":["ai-development","reflection"],"series":null,"minutes":2,"url":"https://ninochavez.co/blog/why-i-use-ai-every-day-and-why-i-started-writing-about-it"},{"slug":"grid-level-thinking-wasnt-the-plan","title":"Grid-Level Thinking Wasn't the Plan","excerpt":"This wasn’t meant to be a thought leadership series. It started as a phone call—and a question: Where does AI actually help, right now, for real? I’m Not Hyping AI. I’m Just Using It.","date":"2025-06-04T04:24:23.000Z","category":"AI & Automation","tags":["ai-development","reflection","architecture"],"series":{"slug":"grid-level-thinking","position":5},"minutes":2,"url":"https://ninochavez.co/blog/grid-level-thinking-wasnt-the-plan"},{"slug":"power-without-purpose-is-just-a-bill","title":"Power Without Purpose Is Just a Bill.","excerpt":"Post 4 of 4 in the Grid-Level Thinking series—why using AI for real work has changed how I design systems, think about leverage, and clarify what actually matters.","date":"2025-06-04T04:24:17.000Z","category":"AI & Automation","tags":["ai-development","architecture"],"series":{"slug":"grid-level-thinking","position":4},"minutes":2,"url":"https://ninochavez.co/blog/power-without-purpose-is-just-a-bill"},{"slug":"plug-in-then-rethink-the-system","title":"Plug In. Then Rethink the System.","excerpt":"Post 3 of 4 in the Grid-Level Thinking series—adopting AI isn’t the hard part. Redesigning your system around it is.","date":"2025-06-04T04:24:10.000Z","category":"AI & Automation","tags":["ai-development","architecture"],"series":{"slug":"grid-level-thinking","position":3},"minutes":2,"url":"https://ninochavez.co/blog/plug-in-then-rethink-the-system"},{"slug":"wired-for-power-everyone-got-the-current-not-everyone-built-the-city","title":"Wired for Power: Everyone Got the Current. Not Everyone Built the City.","excerpt":"Post 2 of 4 in the Grid-Level Thinking series—access isn’t the differentiator anymore. Application is.","date":"2025-06-04T04:24:02.000Z","category":"AI & Automation","tags":["ai-development","leadership","architecture"],"series":{"slug":"grid-level-thinking","position":2},"minutes":1,"url":"https://ninochavez.co/blog/wired-for-power-everyone-got-the-current-not-everyone-built-the-city"},{"slug":"wired-for-power-ai-is-the-grid-youre-the-architect","title":"Wired for Power: AI Is the Grid. You’re the Architect.","excerpt":"Post 1 of 4 in the Grid-Level Thinking series—what we miss when we treat AI like magic instead of infrastructure.","date":"2025-06-04T04:23:55.000Z","category":"AI & Automation","tags":["ai-development","architecture"],"series":{"slug":"grid-level-thinking","position":1},"minutes":1,"url":"https://ninochavez.co/blog/wired-for-power-ai-is-the-grid-youre-the-architect"},{"slug":"the-entry-level-developer-evolution-not-extinction","title":"The Entry-Level Developer: Evolution, Not Extinction","excerpt":"The junior dev role isn’t dying. It’s evolving. The job is no longer about churning out syntax—it’s about navigating ambiguity, thinking critically, and collaborating with AI. Here’s what still matters, and how to coach for it.","date":"2025-06-04T02:31:58.000Z","category":"AI & Automation","tags":["ai-development","architecture"],"series":{"slug":"the-talent-engine","position":5},"minutes":2,"url":"https://ninochavez.co/blog/the-entry-level-developer-evolution-not-extinction"},{"slug":"you-cant-engineer-growth","title":"You Can’t Engineer Growth","excerpt":"This started as a rant while driving. A reminder that no matter how much structure we build, growth still happens on its own terms. In club volleyball and in life—you can’t engineer the outcome. You can only build the conditions.","date":"2025-06-04T01:01:50.000Z","category":"Reflection","tags":["reflection"],"series":null,"minutes":2,"url":"https://ninochavez.co/blog/you-cant-engineer-growth"},{"slug":"how-i-built-signal-reflex-in-a-week","title":"How I Built Signal Reflex in a Week","excerpt":"This blog isn’t a new habit—it’s just a new slice of my output. Here’s how I launched Signal Reflex in a week by treating it like any other creative drop: strategy, tools, publishing cadence, and what I learned by shipping.","date":"2025-06-04T00:15:54.000Z","category":"AI & Automation","tags":["ai-development","architecture"],"series":{"slug":"signal-reflex-launch","position":3},"minutes":3,"url":"https://ninochavez.co/blog/how-i-built-signal-reflex-in-a-week"},{"slug":"almost-flickday","title":"Almost Flickday","excerpt":"Flickday started as a nod to “almost Friday.” Now it’s my creative safe space—a place to stay playful, go off-script, and build with others beyond the Nino Chavez brand.","date":"2025-06-03T23:00:40.000Z","category":"Reflection","tags":["reflection"],"series":null,"minutes":2,"url":"https://ninochavez.co/blog/almost-flickday"},{"slug":"why-i-keep-shooting-even-when-its-not-for-work","title":"Why I Keep Shooting, Even When It’s Not for Work","excerpt":"Photography is the clearest signal I know. I don’t shoot for content—I shoot to stay connected to the part of me that still notices the small stuff.","date":"2025-06-03T14:00:58.000Z","category":"Reflection","tags":["reflection"],"series":null,"minutes":2,"url":"https://ninochavez.co/blog/why-i-keep-shooting-even-when-its-not-for-work"},{"slug":"signal-in-the-noise-my-personal-philosophy","title":"Signal in the Noise: My Personal Philosophy","excerpt":"I don't move to be seen. I move when it matters. My personal philosophy is about recognizing signal, trusting pattern, and acting with intent.","date":"2025-06-03T13:00:34.000Z","category":"Insights","tags":["reflection","leadership"],"series":null,"minutes":2,"url":"https://ninochavez.co/blog/signal-in-the-noise-my-personal-philosophy"},{"slug":"why-they-call-me-uncle-nino-an-evolving-leadership-style","title":"Why They Call Me Uncle Nino","excerpt":"I lead with clarity and calm—not noise. Sometimes that looks like being the one who steadies the room. That’s why they call me Uncle Nino.","date":"2025-06-02T21:02:52.000Z","category":"Leadership","tags":["leadership","reflection"],"series":null,"minutes":2,"url":"https://ninochavez.co/blog/why-they-call-me-uncle-nino-an-evolving-leadership-style"},{"slug":"start-smart-not-perfect","title":"Start Smart, Not Perfect","excerpt":"","date":"2025-06-02T20:50:32.000Z","category":"Field Notes","tags":["ai-development"],"series":null,"minutes":2,"url":"https://ninochavez.co/blog/start-smart-not-perfect"},{"slug":"why-i-shoot-senior-nights","title":"Why I Shoot Senior Nights","excerpt":"Senior Night hits different. It’s not just a ceremony—it’s a goodbye. I shoot it like it matters, because for most people in the gym, it does.","date":"2025-06-02T20:21:22.000Z","category":"Reflection","tags":["reflection"],"series":null,"minutes":3,"url":"https://ninochavez.co/blog/why-i-shoot-senior-nights"},{"slug":"how-i-use-ai-in-consulting-for-real","title":"How I Use AI in Consulting (For Real)","excerpt":"","date":"2025-06-02T20:12:28.000Z","category":"AI & Automation","tags":["ai-development","reflection"],"series":null,"minutes":2,"url":"https://ninochavez.co/blog/how-i-use-ai-in-consulting-for-real"},{"slug":"why-i-started-signal-reflex","title":"Why I Started Signal Reflex","excerpt":"I didn’t start writing to build an audience. I started because AI helped me get past the blank page. This is where I work through what matters—before it fades.","date":"2025-06-02T20:08:19.000Z","category":"AI & Automation","tags":["ai-development","reflection"],"series":{"slug":"signal-reflex-launch","position":2},"minutes":1,"url":"https://ninochavez.co/blog/why-i-started-signal-reflex"},{"slug":"the-storefront-is-dead","title":"The Storefront Is Dead","excerpt":"Most customers already know what they want. They’re not browsing. The storefront isn’t where you win anymore—it’s just where you fulfill.","date":"2025-06-02T18:09:50.000Z","category":"Commerce","tags":["commerce","architecture"],"series":{"slug":"the-storefront","position":1},"minutes":2,"url":"https://ninochavez.co/blog/the-storefront-is-dead"},{"slug":"start-here-why-signal-reflex-exists","title":"Start Here: Why Signal Reflex Exists","excerpt":"Signal Reflex isn’t a brand or a content engine. It’s where I write to stay clear, present, and honest—using AI to reduce drag, not replace thinking.","date":"2025-06-02T13:50:37.000Z","category":"Reflection","tags":["reflection"],"series":{"slug":"signal-reflex-launch","position":1},"minutes":2,"url":"https://ninochavez.co/blog/start-here-why-signal-reflex-exists"}],"series":{"slug":"agentic-workflows-in-practice","title":"Agentic Workflows in Practice","description":"Real work, documented as it happens. Not demos, not theory—actual tasks where AI agents execute, iterate, and deliver. A running log of what works, what breaks, and what it means for how we work."}};
